* {{cite news |last1=Huynh |first1=Anjali |last2=Rosenbluth |first2=Teddy |date=July 6, 2023 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr.'s Conspiracy Theories Go Beyond Vaccines |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/06/us/politics/rfk-conspiracy-theories-fact-check.html |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250419001715/https://www.nytimes.com/article/rfk-conspiracy-theories-fact-check.html |archive-date=April 19, 2025 |access-date=October 12, 2023 |newspaper=The New York Times |quote=Robert F. Kennedy Jr., an environmental lawyer, is a leading vaccine skeptic and purveyor of conspiracy theories who has leaned heavily on misinformation as he mounts his long-shot 2024 campaign for the Democratic nomination.}}</ref> including the [[chemtrail conspiracy theory]],<ref name=bbcChemtrails>{{cite news|first1=David|last1=Hambling|accessdate=February 17, 2026|title=Chemtrails: why is RFK Jr battling a debunked conspiracy theory?|url=https://www.theguardian.com/news/2025/jun/12/chemtrails-rfk-jr-truth-behind-conspiracy-theory|newspaper=The Guardian|date=June 12, 2025|issn=0261-3077|via=The Guardian}}</ref><ref name="chemtrails-cnn" /> [[HIV/AIDS denialism]],<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/health-wellness/2024/11/20/rfk-jr-lgbtq-people-trump-hhs/76426549007/ | title=RFK Jr., AIDS and why the LGBTQ+ community is in an uproar | publisher=[[USA Today]] | date=November 20, 2024 | accessdate=August 31, 2025 | last=Oliver | first=David | quote=But long before all that, in the 1980s, the medical and scientific community made a breakthrough: discovering that HIV caused AIDS. Yet that's a fact that's come under the microscope recently, in part thanks to Kennedy, who has repeatedly made false claims that a recreational drug popular among the gay community, "poppers" (amyl nitrite), was the culprit.}}</ref> and the scientifically disproved claim of a causal link between [[vaccines and autism]].<ref name="FCRFKAutism" /> He has drawn criticism for fueling [[vaccine hesitancy]] amid a social climate that gave rise to the deadly [[measles]] outbreaks in [[2019 Samoa measles outbreak|Samoa]] and [[2019 Tonga measles outbreak|Tonga]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://apnews.com/article/rfk-jr-samoa-measles-kennedy-vaccines-pacific-42a9cb583c71f165699b16710884c474 | title=RFK Jr. misled the US Senate on measles deaths, Samoa's health chief says | publisher=[[Associated Press|The Associated Press]] | date=February 3, 2025 | accessdate=August 31, 2025 | last=Graham-McLay | first=Charlotte. | quote=The outbreak devastated the Pacific island nation in 2019, killing 83 people in a population of 200,000. Vaccination rates were historically low because of poor public health management and the 2018 deaths of two babies whose vaccines were incorrectly prepared, prompting fears that the MMR immunization was unsafe before the nature of the error was discovered. The government suspended vaccinations for 10 months before the outbreak—the period when Kennedy visited. His trip was organized by a Samoan anti-vaccine influencer, according to a 2021 blog post by Kennedy.}}</ref> Kennedy is the founder and former chairman<ref>{{cite web |title=Our Team|url=https://childrenshealthdefense.org/about-us/our-team/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241003065617/https://childrenshealthdefense.org/about-us/our-team/|archive-date=October 3, 2024|access-date=January 30, 2025 |website=Children's Health Defense}}</ref> of [[Children's Health Defense]], an [[Anti-vaccine activism|anti-vaccine advocacy]] group and proponent of [[COVID-19 vaccine misinformation]]. He has written books including ''[[The Riverkeepers]]'' (1997), ''[[Crimes Against Nature (2004 book)|Crimes Against Nature]]'' (2004), ''[[The Real Anthony Fauci]]'' (2021), and ''[[A Letter to Liberals]]'' (2022).

In November 2024, President-elect Trump nominated Kennedy for secretary of health and human services (HHS). In February 2025, the Senate confirmed Kennedy by a vote of 52 to 48, and he was sworn in shortly thereafter amid strong opposition from scientists and medical groups. His tenure has been marked by controversial actions on vaccines, public [[health policy]], and agency leadership that have drawn significant legal, scientific, and political criticism.

==Early life and education==
[[File:President John F. Kennedy with Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. (02).jpg|thumb|Kennedy with his uncle, President [[John F. Kennedy]], in the [[Oval Office]] of the [[White House]] in 1961]]

Robert Francis Kennedy Jr. was born at Georgetown University Hospital in Washington, D.C., on January 17, 1954. He is the third of eleven children of senator and US attorney general [[Robert F. Kennedy]] and [[Ethel Skakel]]. He is a nephew of President [[John F. Kennedy]] and Senator [[Ted Kennedy]].{{sfn|Oppenheimer|2015|pp=5–6}}

Kennedy was raised at the [[Kennedy Compound]] in [[Hyannis Port, Massachusetts]], and at [[Hickory Hill (McLean, Virginia)|Hickory Hill]], the family estate in [[McLean, Virginia]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Kennedy |first1=Robert F. Jr. |title=The Riverkeepers: Two Activists Fight to Reclaim Our Environment as a Basic Human Right |date=1999 |publisher=Scribner |page=92 |quote=... Virginia and Cape Cod (Massachusetts) homes of my youth}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Newlyweds Cheryl Hines, Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. Buy Malibu Compound |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Business/photos/newlyweds-cheryl-hines-robert-kennedy-jr-buy-malibu-25677704/image-25678078 |publisher=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] |quote=in Hyannis Port, Massachusetts, where he was raised |access-date=March 5, 2023 |archive-date=September 25, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140925062836/https://abcnews.go.com/Business/photos/newlyweds-cheryl-hines-robert-kennedy-jr-buy-malibu-25677704/image-25678078 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Hickory Hill: RFK's Virginia Home |url=https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/americanexperience/features/rfk-hickory-hill-robert-and-ethel-kennedys-virginia-home/ |publisher=PBS |access-date=May 2, 2023 |archive-date=May 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230502145106/https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/americanexperience/features/rfk-hickory-hill-robert-and-ethel-kennedys-virginia-home/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In June 1972, he graduated from the Palfrey Street School, a day school in [[Watertown, Massachusetts]]. While attending Palfrey, he lived with a surrogate family at a farmhouse in [[Cambridge, Massachusetts]].<ref>{{cite book |last=Oppenheimer |first=Jerry |author-link=Jerry Oppenheimer |year=2015 |title=RFK Jr.: Robert F. Kennedy Jr. and the Dark Side of the Dream |pages=129–145 |url=https://archive.org/details/rfkjrrobertfkenn0000oppe |url-access=registration |publisher=St. Martin's Press |isbn=978-1-250-03295-9 |oclc=908838847}}</ref> Kennedy continued his education at [[Harvard University]], graduating in 1976 with a Bachelor of Arts in American history and literature. With his former roommate [[Peter W. Kaplan]], he did thesis research in Alabama.<ref>{{Cite news |date=October 11, 1975 |title=Notes on People (Published 1975) |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1975/10/11/archives/notes-on-people-hirohito-tours-san-francisco.html |access-date=September 23, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> He earned a [[Juris Doctor]] degree from the [[University of Virginia School of Law]] in 1982<ref>{{cite magazine |date=September 26, 1983 |title=Crash Landing For Bobby |url=https://content.time.com/time/subscriber/article/0,33009,949812,00.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250214145225/https://content.time.com/time/subscriber/article/0,33009,949812,00.html |archive-date=February 14, 2025 |access-date=May 1, 2025 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]}}</ref><ref name="Moore_3/26/2025">{{cite web |last=Moore |first=Ryan |date=March 26, 2025 |title=When Did RFK Jr. Graduate from UVA Law? |url=https://www.lawweekly.org/col/2025/3/26/q2lhlbcl85im9jg094uenf51tj2j53 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250410015456/https://www.lawweekly.org/col/2025/3/26/q2lhlbcl85im9jg094uenf51tj2j53 |archive-date=April 10, 2025 |access-date=March 28, 2025 |website=[[Virginia Law Weekly]] |quote=Is the Encyclopedia Brittanica {{sic}} actually more accurate than Wikipedia? Is Wikipedia's reliance on the collective wisdom of the internet more reliable than the specialized knowledge of Encyclopedia Brittanica's army of paid fact checkers? How do we prove anything? What is the truth?}}</ref> and a [[Master of Laws]] from [[Pace University]] in 1987.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Backbone Cabinet |url=http://www.backbonecampaign.org/cabinet/nominee.cfm?ID=62 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929042337/http://www.backbonecampaign.org/cabinet/nominee.cfm?ID=62 |archive-date=September 29, 2007 |access-date=May 1, 2025 |website=Backbone Campaign}}</ref>

He was nine years old when John F. Kennedy [[Assassination of John F. Kennedy|was assassinated]] in 1963, and 14 when his father [[Assassination of Robert F. Kennedy|was assassinated]] while running for president in 1968.{{sfn|Oppenheimer|2015|p=27}} Kennedy learned of his father's shooting while at [[Georgetown Preparatory School]].<ref>{{cite interview |last=Kennedy |first=Robert Jr. |interviewer=[[Oprah Winfrey]] |title=Oprah Talks to Bobby Kennedy Jr. |url=https://www.oprah.com/omagazine/oprah-interviews-robert-kennedy-jr/all |access-date=July 28, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230424000706/https://www.oprah.com/omagazine/oprah-interviews-robert-kennedy-jr/all |archive-date=April 24, 2023 |url-status=unfit |work=[[O, The Oprah Magazine]] |date=February 2007}}</ref> A few hours later, he flew to Los Angeles on Vice President [[Hubert Humphrey]]'s plane, along with his older siblings, [[Kathleen Kennedy Townsend|Kathleen]] and [[Joseph P. Kennedy II|Joseph]]. He was with his father when he died. Kennedy was a pallbearer at his father's funeral, where he spoke and read excerpts from his father's speeches at the mass commemorating his death at [[Arlington National Cemetery]].<ref>{{cite news | access-date=August 31, 2025 | url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-boston-globe-folk-mass-honors-rfk/130357551/ | title=Folk Mass Honors RFK | date=June 7, 1969 | last=Storrin | first=Matt | page=4 | quote=Robert F. Kennedy Jr., second oldest son of the late senator read the Entrance Antiphon: "Lord, grant that he may have eternal rest forever in the radiance of your light." | via=[[Newspapers.com]] | work=The Boston Globe}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=June 7, 1970 |title=Robert Kennedy's Words Sung In Mass Marking Assassination |newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1970/06/07/archives/robert-kennedys-words-sung-in-mass-marking-assassination.html |url-status=live |access-date=April 12, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113141004/https://www.nytimes.com/1970/06/07/archives/robert-kennedys-words-sung-in-mass-marking-assassination.html |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

After his father's death, Kennedy struggled with drug abuse, which led to his arrest in [[Barnstable, Massachusetts]], for [[Cannabis (drug)|cannabis]] possession at age 16,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kovach |first1=Bill |title=Kennedy, Shriver Boys on Probation |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1970/08/07/archives/kennedy-shriver-boys-on-probation.html |date=August 7, 1970 |access-date=May 2, 2023 |archive-date=May 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230502155353/https://www.nytimes.com/1970/08/07/archives/kennedy-shriver-boys-on-probation.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Robert Kennedy Jr. Marijuana |url=https://newsroom.ap.org/editorial-photos-videos/detail?itemid=ff0d556c9ee5da11af9f0014c2589dfb&mediatype=photo |agency=Associated Press |date=August 6, 1970 |access-date=May 2, 2023 |archive-date=May 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230502155353/https://newsroom.ap.org/editorial-photos-videos/detail?itemid=ff0d556c9ee5da11af9f0014c2589dfb&mediatype=photo |url-status=live}}</ref> and his expulsion from two boarding schools: [[Millbrook School|Millbrook]] and [[Pomfret School|Pomfret]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Taraborrelli |first1=J. Randy |author-link=J. Randy Taraborrelli |title=The Kennedy Heirs: John, Caroline, and the New Generation – A Legacy of Tragedy and Triumph |publisher=St. Martin's Publishing Group |year=2019 |isbn=9781250174086 |page=183}}</ref><ref>{{cite encyclopedia |title=Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopædia Britannica]] |url=https://www.britannica.com/biography/Robert-F-Kennedy-Jr |access-date=May 16, 2024 |date=May 13, 2024 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250429091402/https://www.britannica.com/biography/Robert-F-Kennedy-Jr |archive-date=April 29, 2025}}</ref> During this time, some in the Kennedy family regarded him as the "ringleader" of a pack of spoiled, rich kids who called themselves the "Hyannis Port Terrors", engaging in vandalism, theft, and drug use.<ref>{{cite web |last=McNamara |first=Eileen |author-link=Eileen McNamara |date=February 14, 2024 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is no Jack — or Bobby — Kennedy |url=https://www.wbur.org/cognoscenti/2024/02/14/robert-f-kennedy-jr-superbowl-ad-eileen-mcnamara |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427112037/https://www.wbur.org/cognoscenti/2024/02/14/robert-f-kennedy-jr-superbowl-ad-eileen-mcnamara |archive-date=April 27, 2025 |access-date=August 24, 2024 |publisher=[[WBUR]]}}</ref>{{sfn|Oppenheimer|2015|p=66}} His first cousin [[Caroline Kennedy]] later blamed Kennedy for leading other members of their family "down the path of drug addiction", calling him a "predator".<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jewett |first1=Christina |last2=Gay Stolberg |first2=Sheryl |date=January 28, 2025 |title=Caroline Kennedy Urges Senators to Reject Her Cousin's Nomination |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/01/28/health/caroline-kennedy-rfk-jr-hhs-confirmation.html |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250328094920/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/01/28/health/caroline-kennedy-rfk-jr-hhs-confirmation.html |archive-date=March 28, 2025 |access-date=February 5, 2025 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> At Harvard, Kennedy continued to use [[heroin]] and [[cocaine]], often with his brother David, earning a reputation that has been described as a "pied piper" and "drug dealer".<ref>{{cite web |last=Andersen |first=Kurt |author-link=Kurt Andersen |date=August 23, 2024 |title=RFK Jr. Was My Drug Dealer |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2024/08/rfk-jr-endorse-trump-execute-drug-dealers/679597/ |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250501195414/https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2024/08/rfk-jr-endorse-trump-execute-drug-dealers/679597/ |archive-date=May 1, 2025 |access-date=August 24, 2024 |publisher=[[The Atlantic]]}}</ref><ref name="Hagan_20240702">{{cite magazine |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/news/story/robert-kennedy-jr-shocking-history |title=RFK Jr.'s Family Doesn't Want Him to Run. Even They May Not Know His Darkest Secrets |last=Hagan |first=Joe|magazine=[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]]|date=July 2, 2024|access-date=January 30, 2025}}</ref>

In 1972, Kennedy and [[Roger Ailes]] made a film about wildlife and conservation in Kenya.<ref>{{Cite news |date=September 3, 1975 |title=Notes on People (Published 1975) |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1975/09/03/archives/notes-on-people-florida-invests-a-black-justice.html |access-date=September 23, 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Bobby Kennedy Jr - Final Frontier |url=https://nulightstudios.com/portfolio-items/final-frontier/ |access-date=September 23, 2025 |website=Nulight Studios |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Citation |last=Ailes |first=Roger |title=The Last Frontier |date=October 11, 1974 |type=Documentary |url=https://www.imdb.com/title/tt9163376/ |access-date=September 23, 2025 |others=Roger Ailes, Robert F. Kennedy Jr |publisher=Ailes Communications}}</ref> Kennedy's book on [[Frank Minis Johnson]], a federal judge who led [[Desegregation in the United States|racial desegregation]] in Alabama, was published in 1978.<ref>{{Cite news |date=August 27, 1977 |title=Johnson Is Focus of Timely Book (Published 1977) |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1977/08/27/archives/johnson-is-focus-of-timely-book.html |access-date=September 23, 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=January 29, 2025 |title=RFK Jr. and how his time in Alabama launched his political career |url=https://www.cbs42.com/news/local/rfk-jr-and-how-his-time-in-alabama-launched-his-political-career/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250205162123/https://www.cbs42.com/news/local/rfk-jr-and-how-his-time-in-alabama-launched-his-political-career/ |archive-date=February 5, 2025 |access-date=September 23, 2025 |work=CBS 42 |language=en-US |url-status=deviated }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=RFK Jr. in Alabama: Wrote Alabama book, interviewed Gov. Wallace,… |url=https://1819news.com/news/item/rfk-jr-in-alabama-wrote-alabama-book-interviewed-gov-wallace-lost-and-found-beloved-dog |access-date=September 23, 2025 |website=1819 News |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=July 30, 1978 |title=Heroic Rulings (Published 1978) |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1978/07/30/archives/heroic-rulings.html |access-date=September 23, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

== Career ==
===Manhattan DA's office===
In 1982, Kennedy was sworn in as an [[assistant district attorney]] for [[New York County District Attorney|Manhattan]].<ref name=KW/> After failing the New York [[bar examination in the United States|bar exam]], he resigned in July 1983.<ref name="UPI1989">{{cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Barbara |date=August 20, 1989 |title=Now it's ADA John F. Kennedy Jr. |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1989/08/20/Now-its-ADA-John-F-Kennedy-Jr/7659619588800 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250213011112/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1989/08/20/Now-its-ADA-John-F-Kennedy-Jr/7659619588800 |archive-date=February 13, 2025 |access-date=May 2, 2023 |publisher=United Press International}}</ref>

=== Conviction for heroin possession ===
On September 16, 1983, Kennedy was arrested and charged with [[heroin]] possession in [[Rapid City, South Dakota]], after he fell ill in an airplane bathroom.<ref name="UPI1989"/> In February 1984, he pleaded guilty to a single [[felony]] charge of possession of heroin, and was sentenced to two years of [[probation]] and community service.<ref name="heroin">{{cite news |title=Robert Kennedy Jr. Admits He Is Guilty In Possessing Heroin |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1984/02/18/us/robert-kennedy-jr-admits-he-is-guilty-in-possessing-heroin.html |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=February 18, 1984 |archive-date=January 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140902/https://www.nytimes.com/1984/02/18/us/robert-kennedy-jr-admits-he-is-guilty-in-possessing-heroin.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="living-dangerously">{{cite news |author1=Mark Leibovich |author-link1=Mark Leibovich |title=Another Kennedy Living Dangerously|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/06/25/fashion/25bobby.html |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=The New York Times |date=June 25, 2006 |archive-date=December 29, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171229012701/http://www.nytimes.com/2006/06/25/fashion/25bobby.html |url-status=live}}</ref> After his arrest, he entered a drug treatment center.<ref name="UPI1989"/> To satisfy conditions of his probation, Kennedy worked as a volunteer for the [[Natural Resources Defense Council]] (NRDC) and was required to attend regular drug rehabilitation sessions.<ref name="QuietVictory">{{Cite news |last1=Dunlap |first1=David W. |author1-link=David W. Dunlap |last2=Perlez |first2=Jane |author2-link=Jane Perlez |date=June 4, 1985 |title=New York Day by Day: A Quiet Victory For Robert F. Kennedy Jr. |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1985/06/04/nyregion/new-york-day-by-day-a-quiet-victory-for-robert-f-kennedy-jr.html |url-status=live |access-date=April 12, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171122030818/http://www.nytimes.com/1985/06/04/nyregion/new-york-day-by-day-a-quiet-victory-for-robert-f-kennedy-jr.html |archive-date=November 22, 2017 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Kennedy asserted that this ended his 14 years of heroin use, which he said had begun when he was 15.<ref name="Hagan_20240702"/> His probation ended a year early.<ref name="QuietVictory"/>

===Riverkeeper===
[[File:Robert F. Kennedy Jr. flying a Harris Hawk with his daughter, Kick Kennedy (1989).jpg|thumb|left|Kennedy with his daughter, Kick, holding a [[Harris's hawk]] in 1989]]
In 1984, Kennedy began volunteering at the Hudson River Fisherman's Association, renamed [[Riverkeeper]] in 1986 after a patrol boat it had built with settlement money from legal victories preceding Kennedy's arrival.<ref name="cockatoo"/><ref>{{cite news |last=Worth |first=Robert |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2000/11/05/nyregion/a-kennedy-and-his-mentor-part-ways-over-river-group.html |title=A Kennedy and His Mentor Part Ways Over River Group |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=November 5, 2000 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150527131738/https://www.nytimes.com/2000/11/05/nyregion/a-kennedy-and-his-mentor-part-ways-over-river-group.html |archive-date=May 27, 2015 |access-date=March 3, 2024}}</ref> After he was admitted to the New York bar in 1985, Riverkeeper hired him as senior attorney.<ref name="QuietVictory"/><ref name="cockatoo"/><ref name="auto4">Cronin, John; Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (1997). ''The Riverkeepers: Two Activists Fight to Reclaim Our Environment as a Basic Human Right''. New York: Scribner. p. 304. {{ISBN|0-684-83908-3}}.</ref> Kennedy litigated and supervised [[Environmental law|environmental enforcement]] lawsuits on the east coast estuaries on behalf of Hudson Riverkeeper and the [[Soundkeeper|Long Island Soundkeeper]],<ref>"Our core mission: Environmental enforcement". 2016. ''Riverkeeper Journal''.</ref> where he was also a board member. Long Island Soundkeeper sued several municipalities and cities along the Connecticut and New York coastlines.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soundkeeper's History |url=https://www.savethesound.org/what-we-do/healthy-waters/long-island-soundkeeper/soundkeeper-history/ |access-date=June 8, 2024 |website=Save the Sound}}</ref> On the Hudson, Kennedy sued municipalities and industries, including [[General Electric]], to stop discharging pollution and clean up legacy contamination.<ref name=":2">{{Cite magazine |last=Navasky |first=Bruno |date=August 11, 2016 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. on the Environment, Election, and a 'Dangerous' Donald Trump |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2016/08/robert-f-kennedy-jr-on-the-environment-election-and-donald-trump |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191101010229/https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2016/08/robert-f-kennedy-jr-on-the-environment-election-and-donald-trump |archive-date=November 1, 2019 |access-date=April 12, 2023 |magazine=Vanity Fair}}</ref> His work at Riverkeeper set long-term environmental legal standards.<ref name=":2" />

In 1995, Kennedy advocated for repeal of legislation that he considered unfriendly to the environment.<ref name="auto">{{Cite magazine |last=Werth |first=Barry |author-link=Barry Werth |date=May 2, 2004 |title=Somewhere Down the Crazy River|url=https://www.outsideonline.com/outdoor-adventure/environment/somewhere-down-crazy-river/ |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140853/https://www.outsideonline.com/1886331/somewhere-down-crazy-river |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |access-date=April 12, 2023 |magazine=[[Outside (magazine)|Outside]]}}</ref> In 1997, he worked with John Cronin to write ''The Riverkeepers'', a history of the early Riverkeepers and a primer for the Waterkeeper movement.<ref name="auto4"/>

In 2000, a majority of Riverkeeper's board sided with Kennedy when he insisted on rehiring William Wegner, a wildlife lecturer and falcon trainer<ref name="smuggler">{{cite news |last=Pool |first=Bob |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1995-06-30-me-18808-story.html |title=Cracking a Case of Egg Smuggling |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=June 30, 1995 |access-date=March 2, 2024}}</ref><ref name="cockatoo"/> whom the organization's founder and president, [[Robert H. Boyle]], had fired six months earlier after learning that Wegner had been convicted in 1995 for tax fraud, perjury, and conspiracy to violate wildlife protection laws.<ref name="cockatoo">{{cite news |last=Jamison |first=Peter |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2024/03/02/robert-f-kennedy-jr-rfk-riverkeeper-egg-smuggler/ |title=How RFK Jr. hiring a bird smuggler threw his environmental group into turmoil |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=March 2, 2024 |access-date=March 2, 2024}}</ref><ref name="rare_egg">{{cite news |last=Worth |first=Robert |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2000/06/22/nyregion/eight-at-riverkeeper-resign-over-kennedy-s-hiring-of-a-rare-egg-smuggler.html |title=Eight at Riverkeeper Resign Over Kennedy's Hiring of a Rare-Egg Smuggler |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=June 22, 2000 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130513045042/https://www.nytimes.com/2000/06/22/nyregion/eight-at-riverkeeper-resign-over-kennedy-s-hiring-of-a-rare-egg-smuggler.html |archive-date=May 13, 2013 |access-date=March 2, 2024}}</ref> Wegner had recruited and led a team of at least 10 who smuggled [[cockatoo]] eggs, including species considered endangered by Australia, from Australia to the US over a period of eight years.<ref name="smuggler"/><ref name="cockatoo"/> He served 3.5 years of a five-year sentence and was hired by Kennedy a few months after his release.<ref name="cockatoo"/> After the board's decision, Boyle, eight of the 22 members of the board, and Riverkeeper's treasurer resigned, saying it was not right for an environmental organization to hire someone convicted of environmental crimes and that it would hurt the organization's fundraising.<ref name="cockatoo"/><ref name="rare_egg"/>

While working with Riverkeeper, Kennedy spearheaded a 34-year battle to close the [[Indian Point Energy Center|Indian Point]] nuclear-power plant.<ref name=":7">{{Cite web |date=April 4, 2017 |title="Nuclear Ticking Time Bomb 28 Miles From NYC – America's Lawyer" |url=https://www.ipsecinfo.org/2017/04/04/nuclear-ticking-time-bomb-28-miles-from-nyc-americas-lawyer/ |access-date=May 20, 2024 |publisher=Indian Point Safe Energy Coalition}}</ref> Kennedy was featured in a 2004 documentary about the plant, ''Indian Point: Imagining the Unimaginable'', directed by his sister, the documentary filmmaker [[Rory Kennedy]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Gates |first=Anita |date=September 9, 2004 |title=Seeing a Mushroom Cloud in New York (movie review) |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2004/09/09/arts/television/seeing-a-mushroom-cloud-in-new-york.html |access-date=May 23, 2024 |newspaper=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> In 2017, Kennedy argued that the electricity Indian Point provided could be fully replaced by [[renewable energy]].<ref name=":7" /> In 2022, after the plant's closure, carbon emissions from electricity generation in New York state increased by 37%, compared to 2019, before the start of the closure.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Levitz |first=Eric |date=April 2, 2022 |title=Once Again, Environmentalists Are Sabotaging Climate Progress |url=https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2022/04/environmentalists-are-sabotaging-climate-progress-again.html |access-date=May 20, 2024 |magazine=New York}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Milman |first=Oliver |date=March 20, 2024 |title=A nuclear plant's closure was hailed as a green win. Then emissions went up |url=https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/mar/20/nuclear-plant-closure-carbon-emissions-new-york |access-date=May 20, 2024 |newspaper=The Guardian}}</ref>

Kennedy resigned from Riverkeeper in 2017.<ref name="cockatoo"/><ref>{{cite press release |url=https://www.riverkeeper.org/news-events/news/riverkeeper/robert-f-kennedy-jr-ends-historic-33-year-run-riverkeeper/ |title=Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.'s historic 33-year run with Riverkeeper comes to a close |publisher=[[Riverkeeper]] |date=March 11, 2017 |access-date=March 2, 2024 |archive-date=March 2, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240302015701/https://www.riverkeeper.org/news-events/news/riverkeeper/robert-f-kennedy-jr-ends-historic-33-year-run-riverkeeper/ |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Agee |first=J'nelle |date=March 18, 2017 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Resigns from Riverkeeper |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/hudson-valley/news/2017/03/17/robert-kennedy-jr-resigns-from-riverkeeper |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171004135637/http://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/hudson-valley/news/2017/03/17/robert-kennedy-jr-resigns-from-riverkeeper |archive-date=October 4, 2017 |access-date=April 12, 2023 |website=Spectrum News}}</ref>

===Pace Environmental Litigation Clinic===
In 1987, Kennedy founded the Environmental Litigation Clinic at Pace University School of Law,<ref>{{cite web |title=Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. |work=JW Howard Attorneys |url=https://jwhowardattorneys.com/member/rfk-jr/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230502054603/https://jwhowardattorneys.com/member/rfk-jr/ |archive-date=May 2, 2023 |access-date=May 2, 2023 }}</ref> where for three decades he was the clinic's supervising attorney and co-director and Clinical Professor of Law.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.law.pace.edu/pace-environmental-litigation-clinic|title=Pace Environmental Litigation Clinic |publisher=[[Pace University School of Law]] |access-date=October 15, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171013103626/http://www.law.pace.edu/pace-environmental-litigation-clinic|archive-date=October 13, 2017|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Smith |first=Steve |date=April 29, 2015 |title=RFK Jr. to address College of Law graduates |url=https://news.unl.edu/newsrooms/today/article/rfk-jr-to-address-college-of-law-graduates/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171004085140/http://news.unl.edu/newsrooms/today/article/rfk-jr-to-address-college-of-law-graduates |archive-date=October 4, 2017 |access-date=April 12, 2023 |website=Nebraska Today}}</ref> Kennedy obtained a special order from the New York State Court of Appeals that permitted his 10 clinic students to practice law and try cases against Hudson River polluters in state and federal court, under the supervision of Kennedy and his co-director, Professor Karl Coplan. The clinic's full-time clients are Riverkeeper and Long Island Soundkeeper.<ref name="oregon">Kennedy, Robert F. Jr., Solow, Steven P. (1993). "Environmental Litigation as Clinical Education: A Case Study". ''University of Oregon Journal of Environmental Law and Litigation'' Volume 8.</ref>

The clinic has sued governments and companies for polluting Long Island Sound and the [[Hudson River]] and its tributaries.<ref>"A conversation with the authors of 'The Riverkeepers: Two Activists Fight to Reclaim Our Environment as a Basic Human Right'". November 10, 1997. Charlie Rose.</ref> It argued cases to expand citizen access to the shoreline and won hundreds of settlements for the Hudson Riverkeeper.<ref>Cronin, John, and Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (July 26, 1997). "It's Our River – Let Us Get to It". ''The New York Times''.</ref> Kennedy and his students also sued dozens of municipal wastewater treatment plants to force compliance with the [[Clean Water Act]].<ref name="oregon"/> In 2010, a Pace lawsuit forced [[ExxonMobil]] to clean up tens of millions of gallons of oil from legacy refinery spills in [[Newtown Creek]] in Brooklyn.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Brown |first=Kim |date=July 8, 2004 |title=ExxonMobil Sued Over 55-Acre Oil Spill In Newtown Creek |work=Queens Chronicle |url=https://www.qchron.com/editions/central/exxonmobil-sued-over-55-acre-oil-spill-in-newtown-creek/article_fccf185b-101f-50c8-9e5a-0657f160cb9b.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230820221215/https://www.qchron.com/editions/central/exxonmobil-sued-over-55-acre-oil-spill-in-newtown-creek/article_fccf185b-101f-50c8-9e5a-0657f160cb9b.html |archive-date=August 20, 2023}}<!--archived URL due to link blocked in the European Union --></ref>

On April 11, 2001, ''[[Men's Journal]]'' gave Kennedy its "Heroes" Award for creating the Pace Environmental Litigation Clinic.<ref>Kaydo, Chad (April 13, 2001). "A Manly Awards Party for ''[[Men's Journal]]''". BizBash.</ref> Kennedy and the clinic received other awards for successful legal work cleaning up the environment.<ref>Pace Environmental Litigation Clinic. "Clinic Awards". Pace Law.</ref> The Pace Clinic became a model for similar environmental law clinics throughout the country.<ref>Rutgers School of Law Environmental Law Clinic. "About this Organization". Justia Lawyers.</ref><ref>Environmental Law Courses and Clinics. UCLA Law.</ref><ref>Environmental Law Clinic. Widener Environmental Law Center. Widener Law.</ref><ref>Environmental Law Clinic. Berkeley Law.</ref>

===Waterkeeper Alliance===
In June 1999, as Riverkeeper's success on the Hudson began inspiring the creation of Waterkeepers across North America, Kennedy and a few dozen Riverkeepers gathered in Southampton, Long Island, to found the [[Waterkeeper Alliance]], which is now the umbrella group for the 344 licensed Waterkeeper programs<ref>Gallay, Paul. May 3, 2018. [https://www.riverkeeper.org/blogs/docket/china-enlists-local-groups-in-environmental-enforcement-push/ "China enlists local groups in environmental enforcement push"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230511072402/https://www.riverkeeper.org/blogs/docket/china-enlists-local-groups-in-environmental-enforcement-push/ |date=May 11, 2023}}. Waterkeeper.org.</ref> in 44 countries.<ref>Yaggi, Marc, Waterkeeper Alliance. August 2, 2018. "Let Our Rivers Run Free: A Global Look at How Dams are Destroying our Waterways". ''Waterkeeper Magazine''.</ref> As president, Kennedy oversaw its legal, membership, policy and fundraising programs. The Alliance is dedicated to promoting "swimmable, fishable, drinkable waterways, worldwide".<ref>"Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. Honored at Water's Edge Gala". (2014). Stroudcenter.org.</ref>

Under Kennedy's leadership, Waterkeeper launched its "[[Clean Coal]] is a Deadly Lie"<ref>Baxter, Brandon (June 3, 2014). "Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Praises Obama's Carbon Rules, Blasts Koch Brothers on 'The Ed Show'". ''EcoWatch''.</ref> campaign in 2001, bringing dozens of lawsuits targeting mining practices, including [[mountaintop removal]]<ref>Perks, Rob (March 11, 2010). [https://www.nrdc.org/experts/rob-perks/mountaintop-removal-redux-bobby-vs-blankenship-ii "Mountaintop Removal Redux: Bobby vs. Blankenship II"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140905/https://www.nrdc.org/experts/rob-perks/mountaintop-removal-redux-bobby-vs-blankenship-ii |date=January 13, 2020}}. NRDC.</ref> and [[slurry]] pond construction, as well as coal-burning utilities' mercury emissions and [[coal ash]] piles.<ref>"Bobby Kennedy, Jr. Speaks on Coal Ash at Mt. Island Lake". July 25, 2013. ''Clean Air Carolina''.</ref> Kennedy's Waterkeeper alliance has also been fighting coal export, including from terminals in the [[Pacific Northwest]].<ref>Yaggi, Marc, Waterkeeper Alliance (Winter 2013). "Around the World a Coalition Against Coal: Waterkeepers from Idaho to India Unite to Stop the Deadly International Coal Trade". ''Waterkeeper Magazine''.</ref>

Waterkeeper waged a legal and public relations battle against pollution from [[factory farm]]s. In the 1990s, Kennedy rallied opposition to factory farms among small independent farmers, convened a series of "National Summits" on factory meat products, and conducted press conference whistle-stop tours across North Carolina, Iowa, Kansas, Missouri, Illinois, Ohio, and in Washington, D.C. Beginning in 2000, Kennedy sued factory farms in North Carolina, Oklahoma, Maryland, and Iowa.<ref name="porkchop">{{Cite book |last=Hahn Niman |first=Nicolette |title=Righteous Porkchop: Finding a Life and Good Food beyond Factory Farms |date=February 17, 2009 |publisher=William Morrow |isbn=978-0-06-146649-6}}{{page needed|date=August 2023}}</ref> In a 2003 article, he argued factory farms produce lower-quality, less healthy food, and harm independent family farmers by poisoning their air and water, reducing their property values, and using extensive state and federal subsidies to impose unfair competition against them.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Robert F. Jr. |last1=Kennedy |first2=Eric |last2=Schaeffer |date=September 20, 2003 |title=An Ill Wind From Factory Farms |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2003/09/20/opinion/an-ill-wind-from-factory-farms.html |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140955/https://www.nytimes.com/2003/09/20/opinion/an-ill-wind-from-factory-farms.html |url-status=live |department=Opinion |newspaper=The New York Times |access-date=January 13, 2020}}</ref>

Kennedy and his environmental work have been the focus of several films, including ''The Waterkeepers'' (2000),<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.amazon.com/Waterkeepers-Robert-F-Kennedy-Jr/dp/B01LXLQTXE|title=Amazon.com: The Waterkeepers: Robert F. Kennedy Jr., John Cronin, Bob Boyle, Terry Backer, Rick Dove, BJ Cummings, Steve Fleishli, Les Guthman, Les Guthman, Les Guthman: Prime Video|website=www.amazon.com}}</ref> directed by [[Les Guthman]]. In 2008, he appeared in the [[IMAX]] documentary film ''[[Grand Canyon Adventure: River at Risk]]'', riding the Grand Canyon in a wooden dory with his daughter Kick and anthropologist [[Wade Davis (anthropologist)|Wade Davis]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0858497/|title=Grand Canyon Adventure: River at Risk|date=March 12, 2008|via=IMDb}}</ref>

Kennedy resigned the Waterkeeper Alliance presidency in November 2020.<ref>{{cite web |date=November 10, 2020 |title=Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. Resigns as Waterkeeper Alliance President |url=https://waterkeeper.org/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-resigns-as-waterkeeper-alliance-president/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250401002209/https://waterkeeper.org/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-resigns-as-waterkeeper-alliance-president/ |archive-date=April 1, 2025 |access-date=August 10, 2024 |website=Waterkeeper Alliance}}</ref>

===New York City Watershed Agreement===
Beginning in 1991, Kennedy represented environmentalists and New York City watershed consumers in a series of lawsuits against New York City and upstate watershed polluters. Kennedy authored a series of articles and reports<ref name="watershed">{{cite news |last1=Worth |first1=Robert |author1-link=Robert F. Worth |date=July 1, 2001 |title=Watershed Warrior |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2001/07/01/nyregion/watershed-warrior.html |url-status=live |access-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140919/https://www.nytimes.com/2001/07/01/nyregion/watershed-warrior.html |archive-date=January 13, 2020}}</ref><ref>Gordon, David K., and Kennedy Jr., Robert F. (September 1991). "The Legend of City Water: Recommendations For Rescuing the New York City Water Supply". The Hudson Riverkeeper Fund.</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Kennedy |first=Robert F. Jr. |date=August 22, 1989 |title=New York City's Water: Down the Drain |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1989/08/22/opinion/new-york-city-s-water-down-the-drain.html |via=The New York Times Archives |page=23 |access-date=April 10, 2023 |archive-date=April 16, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230416005411/https://www.nytimes.com/1989/08/22/opinion/new-york-city-s-water-down-the-drain.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www1.nyc.gov/assets/planning/download/pdf/about/cpc/990237.pdf |title=Approval of planning application C 990237 PSX |access-date=October 14, 2017 |date=June 1, 1999 |website=nyc.gov |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170929044431/http://www1.nyc.gov/assets/planning/download/pdf/about/cpc/990237.pdf |archive-date=September 29, 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref> alleging that New York State was abdicating its responsibility to protect the water repository and supply. In 1996, he helped orchestrate the $1.2 billion New York City Watershed Agreement, which ''[[New York (magazine)|New York]]'' magazine recognized in its cover story, "The Kennedy Who Matters".<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Wechsler |first=Pat |date=November 27, 1995 |title=The Kennedy Who Matters |magazine=[[New York (magazine)|New York]]}}</ref> This agreement, which Kennedy negotiated on behalf of environmentalists and New York City watershed consumers, is regarded as an international model in stakeholder consensus negotiations and sustainable development.<ref>Schneeweiss, Jonathan (1997). "Watershed Protection Strategies: A Case Study of the New York City Watershed in Light of the 1996 Amendments to the Safe Drinking Water Act". 8 Vill. ''Environmental Law Journal'' 77.</ref>

===Kennedy & Madonna LLP===
[[File:R.F.K. jr 2000.jpg|thumb|upright|Kennedy in 2000]]

In 2000, Kennedy and the environmental lawyer Kevin Madonna founded the [[environmental law]] firm Kennedy & Madonna, LLP, to represent private plaintiffs against polluters.<ref name="KandMTeam">{{cite web|url=http://kennedy-madonna-1zcf.squarespace.com/team/|title=Team|publisher=Kennedy & Madonna, LLP |access-date=October 15, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170929001322/http://kennedy-madonna-1zcf.squarespace.com/team/|archive-date=September 29, 2017|url-status=live}}</ref> The firm litigates environmental contamination cases on behalf of individuals, non-profit organizations, school districts, public water suppliers, Indian tribes, municipalities and states. In 2001, Kennedy & Madonna organized a team of prestigious plaintiff law firms to challenge pollution from industrial pork and poultry production.<ref>{{Cite news |date=January 7, 2001 |title=Farmers worried by suits targeting hog producers |page=9 |work=Des Moines Register |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-des-moines-register-farmers-worried/130358159/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> In 2004, the firm was part of a legal team that secured a $70 million settlement for property owners in Pensacola, Florida whose properties were contaminated by chemicals from an adjacent [[Superfund]] site.<ref>{{Cite news |date=April 7, 2004 |title=ConocoPhillips agrees to $70M settlement for former Fla. facility |work=EENews.net}}</ref>

Kennedy & Madonna was profiled in the 2010 HBO documentary ''Mann v. Ford'',<ref>[https://www.imdb.com/title/tt1498566/ "Mann v. Ford"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170302180941/http://www.imdb.com/title/tt1498566/ |date=March 2, 2017}} (2010). IMDb.{{Unreliable source?|date=June 2023}}</ref> which chronicles four years of litigation by the firm on behalf of the [[Ramapough Mountain Indians]] against the [[Ford Motor Company]] for dumping toxic waste on tribal lands in northern New Jersey.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hbo.com/documentaries/mann-v-ford/synopsis.html|title=Mann v. Ford: Synopsis|publisher=HBO |access-date=October 15, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171006145520/http://www.hbo.com/documentaries/mann-v-ford/synopsis.html|archive-date=October 6, 2017}}</ref> In addition to a monetary settlement for the tribe, the lawsuit contributed to the community's land being relisted on the federal Superfund list, the first time that a delisted site was relisted.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Strunsky |first=Steve |date=October 14, 2006 |title=Superfund Site Is Relisted, and Inquiry Begins |newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/10/14/nyregion/nyregionspecial2/15njringwood.html |url-status=live |access-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140851/https://www.nytimes.com/2006/10/14/nyregion/nyregionspecial2/15njringwood.html |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

In 2007, Kennedy was one of three finalists nominated by Public Justice as "Trial Lawyer of the Year" for his role in the $396 million jury verdict against [[DuPont]] for contamination from its zinc plant in [[Spelter, West Virginia]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=DuPont Fined $196.2M In Class-Action Suit |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/dupont-fined-1962m-in-class-action-suit/ |access-date=April 12, 2023 |publisher=CBS News |date=October 19, 2007 |archive-date=April 13, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230413000505/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/dupont-fined-1962m-in-class-action-suit/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2017, the firm was part of the trial team that secured a $670 million settlement on behalf of over 3,000 residents from Ohio and West Virginia whose drinking water was contaminated by the toxic chemical [[perfluorooctanoic acid]], which DuPont released into the environment in [[Parkersburg]], West Virginia.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rinehart |first=Earl |date=February 13, 2017 |title=DuPont to pay $670 million to settle C8 lawsuits |url=https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/courts/2017/02/13/dupont-to-pay-670-million/22468972007/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220818144117/https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/courts/2017/02/13/dupont-to-pay-670-million/22468972007/ |archive-date=August 18, 2022 |access-date=April 12, 2023 |newspaper=The Columbus Dispatch}}</ref>

===Morgan & Morgan===
In 2016, Kennedy became counsel to the [[Morgan & Morgan]] law firm.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.forthepeople.com/attorneys/robert-f-kennedy-jr/|title=Attorney Robert F. Kennedy, Jr |access-date=October 14, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170929001139/https://www.forthepeople.com/attorneys/robert-f-kennedy-jr/ |archive-date=September 29, 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref> The partnership arose from the two firms' successful collaboration on the case against SoCalGas Company following the [[Aliso Canyon gas leak]] in California.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cruz |first=Nancy |date=February 18, 2016 |title=Porter Ranch Gas Leak With Attorney Robert F. Kennedy |work=KTLA |url=https://ktla.com/morning-news/porter-ranch-gas-leak-legal-liabilities-pollution-problems/ |access-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230412233549/https://ktla.com/morning-news/porter-ranch-gas-leak-legal-liabilities-pollution-problems/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2017, Kennedy and his partners sued [[Monsanto]] in federal court in San Francisco, on behalf of plaintiffs seeking to recover damages for [[non-Hodgkin's lymphoma]] cases that, the plaintiffs allege, were a result of exposure to Monsanto's [[glyphosate]]-based herbicide, [[Roundup (herbicide)|Roundup]]. Kennedy and his team also filed a class action lawsuit against Monsanto for failing to warn consumers about the dangers allegedly posed by exposure to Roundup.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stecker |first=Tiffany |date=June 27, 2017 |title=Monsanto's Foes Are Branching Out |publisher=Bloomberg Law |url=https://news.bloomberglaw.com/environment-and-energy/monsantos-foes-are-branching-out |url-access=subscription}}</ref>

In September 2018, Kennedy and his partners filed a class-action lawsuit against [[Columbia Gas of Massachusetts]] alleging negligence following [[Merrimack Valley gas explosions|gas explosions]] in three towns north of Boston. Of Columbia Gas, Kennedy said, "as they build new miles of pipe, the same company is ignoring its existing infrastructure, which we now know is eroding and is dilapidated".<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://gazette.com/mass-families-file-class-action-days-after-gas-explosions/article_f1b671b8-bc7c-11e8-ae39-4787469086e9.html |title=Mass. Families file class action days after gas explosions |date=September 19, 2018 |access-date=October 14, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181014091538/https://gazette.com/mass-families-file-class-action-days-after-gas-explosions/article_f1b671b8-bc7c-11e8-ae39-4787469086e9.html |archive-date=October 14, 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Cape Wind===
{{Main|Cape Wind}}
In 2005, Kennedy clashed with national environmental groups over his opposition to the Cape Wind Project, a proposed [[offshore wind farm]] in [[Cape Cod, Massachusetts]] (in [[Nantucket Sound]]). Taking the side of Cape Cod's commercial [[fishing industry]], Kennedy argued that the project was a costly boondoggle. This position angered some environmentalists, and Kennedy was criticized by [[Rush Limbaugh]] and [[John Stossel]].{{citation needed|date=June 2025}} In ''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'', Kennedy wrote, "Vermont wants to take its nuclear plant off line and replace it with clean, green power from Hydro-Québec—power available to Massachusetts utilities—at a cost of six cents per kilowatt hour (kwh). Cape Wind electricity, by a conservative estimate and based on figures they filed with the state, comes in at 25 cents per kwh."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052702304521304576447541604359376 |title=Nantucket's Wind Power Rip-off |newspaper=The Wall Street Journal |date=July 18, 2011 |access-date=March 9, 2020 |archive-date=February 24, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200224101905/https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052702304521304576447541604359376 |url-status=live |first1=Robert F. Jr. |last1=Kennedy}}</ref>

===Other ventures===
In 1999, Kennedy, Chris Bartle and John Hoving created a bottled water company, Keeper Springs, which donated all of its profits to Waterkeeper Alliance.<ref>{{cite news |author1=Patricia Winters Lauro |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is pushing supermarkets to sell a new brand of bottled water, with a twist|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1999/06/03/business/media-business-advertising-robert-f-kennedy-jr-pushing-supermarkets-sell-new.html |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=The New York Times |date=June 3, 1999 |archive-date=January 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140901/https://www.nytimes.com/1999/06/03/business/media-business-advertising-robert-f-kennedy-jr-pushing-supermarkets-sell-new.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

Kennedy was a venture partner and senior advisor at VantagePoint Capital Partners, one of the world's largest [[Clean technology|cleantech]] [[venture capital]] firms. Among other activities, VantagePoint was the original and largest pre-[[IPO]] institutional investor in [[Tesla, Inc.]]<ref>{{Cite news |last=Craig |first=Susanne |date=November 16, 2023 |title=How R.F.K. Jr. Has Turned His Public Crusades Into a Private Windfall |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/11/16/us/rfk-jr-finances.html |access-date=January 9, 2025 |work=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> VantagePoint also backed BrightSource Energy and Solazyme, amongst others. Kennedy is a board member and counselor to several of Vantage Point's portfolio companies in the water and energy space, including Ostara, a Vancouver-based company that markets the technology to remove [[phosphorus]] and other excessive nutrients from [[wastewater]], transforming otherwise pollution directly into high-grade fertilizer.<ref>Wesoff, Eric (May 31, 2012) [https://www.greentechmedia.com/articles/read/ostara-nutrient-recovery-wins-14-5m-from-vantagepoint-et-al "Ostara Nutrient Recovery Wins $14.5M From VantagePoint et al."] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140852/https://www.greentechmedia.com/articles/read/ostara-nutrient-recovery-wins-14-5m-from-vantagepoint-et-al |date=January 13, 2020}}. ''Greentech Media''.</ref> He is also a senior advisor to Starwood Energy Group and has played a key role in a number of the firm's investments.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Wesoff |first=Eric |date=October 7, 2015 |title=Flow Battery Funding: Vionx Teams With Siemens, UTC, 3M, Starwood and Jabil |work=Greentech Media |url=https://www.greentechmedia.com/articles/read/flow-battery-funding-vionx-teams-with-siemens-utc-3m-starwood-and-jabil}}</ref>

He is on the board of Vionx, a Massachusetts-based utility-scale [[vanadium flow battery]] systems manufacturer. On October 5, 2017, Vionx, [[National Grid plc|National Grid]] and the [[United States Department of Energy|US Department of Energy]] completed the installation of advanced flow batteries at [[Holy Name Central Catholic High School|Holy Name High School]] in the city of [[Worcester, Massachusetts]]. The collaboration also includes [[Siemens]] and the [[United Technologies]] Research Center and constitutes one of the largest energy storage facilities in Massachusetts.<ref>{{cite press release|url=http://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20171005006381/en/Vionx-National-Grid-Department-Energy-Complete-Installation |title=Vionx, National Grid, and US Department of Energy Complete Installation of one of the World's Most Advanced Flow Batteries at Holy Name High School, Worcester, MA|website=Business Wire |date=October 5, 2017|access-date=October 15, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171016070018/http://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20171005006381/en/Vionx-National-Grid-Department-Energy-Complete-Installation |archive-date=October 16, 2017|url-status=live}}</ref>

Kennedy served on the board of the New York [[League of Conservation Voters]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=NYLCV and NYCEF Boards of Directors|url=http://nylcv.org/board.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/19991012112205/http://nylcv.org/board.htm|archivedate=October 12, 1999 |access-date=April 17, 2025 |website=New York League of Conservation Voters}}</ref>

Kennedy is a partner in ColorZen, which offers a turnkey-cotton-fiber pre-treatment solution that reduces water usage and toxic discharges in the [[Dyeing|cotton-dyeing]] process.<ref name="Hilburn-3 Jun 2016">{{Cite web |last=Hilburn |first=Rachel Lewis |date=June 3, 2016 |title=CoastLine: Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. on the Origin of CAFOs, Environmental Justice|url=https://www.whqr.org/local/2016-06-03/coastline-robert-f-kennedy-jr-on-the-origin-of-cafos-environmental-justice |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171016014708/http://whqr.org/post/coastline-robert-f-kennedy-jr-origin-cafos-environmental-justice#stream/0 |archive-date=October 16, 2017 |access-date=April 12, 2023 |publisher=WHQR}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.southwestjournal.com/net-worth/robert-f-kennedy-jr-net-worth/ | title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Net Worth 2024 | date=January 10, 2024 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://justfacts.votesmart.org/candidate/biography/211925/robert-kennedy-jr | title=Robert Kennedy, Jr.'s Biography |website=Vote Smart}}</ref>

Kennedy was a co-owner and director of the [[smart grid|smart-grid]] company Utility Integration Solutions (UISol),<ref>{{Cite press release |title=Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. Joins UISOL Board of Directors |date=August 25, 2010 |publisher=McDonnell Group |url=https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/robert-f-kennedy-jr-joins-uisol-board-of-directors-101474434.html}}</ref> which was acquired by [[Alstom]]. He is presently a co-owner and director of GridBright, the market-leading grid management specialist.<ref>"Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. joins Board of Directors". November 1, 2016. GridBright.com.</ref>

In October 2011, Kennedy co-founded ''EcoWatch'', an environmental news site.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Michael Scott |first=The Plain Dealer |date=October 27, 2011 |title=Robert Kennedy Jr. in Cleveland to launch joint EcoWatch-Riverkeeper environmental news, advocacy site |url=https://www.cleveland.com/metro/2011/10/robert_kennedy_jr_in_cleveland.html |access-date=April 17, 2025 |website=cleveland }}</ref> He resigned from its board of directors in 2018.<ref>Spear, Stefanie (November 2, 2011). "EcoWatch and Waterkeeper Launch News Website". EcoWatch.</ref>

===Minority and poor communities===
In his first case as an environmental attorney, Kennedy represented the [[NAACP]] in a lawsuit against a proposal to build a garbage transfer station in a minority neighborhood in [[Ossining, New York (village)|Ossining, New York]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Melvin |first=Tessa |date=April 7, 1991 |title=Expanded Recycling Site Upsets an Ossining Neighborhood |newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1991/04/07/nyregion/expanded-recycling-site-upsets-an-ossining-neighborhood.html |url-status=live |access-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171024043625/http://www.nytimes.com/1991/04/07/nyregion/expanded-recycling-site-upsets-an-ossining-neighborhood.html |archive-date=October 24, 2017 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> In 1987, he successfully sued [[Westchester County]] to reopen the [[Croton Point Park]], which was primarily used by poor and minority communities from [[the Bronx]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Reed |first=Susan |date=July 2, 1990 |title=Polluters, Beware! Riverkeeper John Cronin Patrols the Hudson and Pursues Those Who Foul Its Waters|url=https://people.com/archive/polluters-beware-riverkeeper-john-cronin-patrols-the-hudson-and-pursues-those-who-foul-its-waters-vol-33-no-26/ |url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171016014316/http://people.com/archive/polluters-beware-riverkeeper-john-cronin-patrols-the-hudson-and-pursues-those-who-foul-its-waters-vol-33-no-26/ |archive-date=October 16, 2017 |access-date=April 12, 2023 |magazine=People}}</ref> He then forced the reopening of the [[Pelham Bay Park]], which New York City had closed to the public and converted to a police firing range.<ref name="auto4"/>

===International and indigenous rights===
[[File:UFW's ARturo Rodriguez & Robert Kennedy Jr. (32424580442).jpg|thumb|Kennedy at a [[United Farm Workers]] rally in 2017]]

Starting in 1985, Kennedy helped develop the international program for environmental, energy, and human rights of the [[Natural Resources Defense Council]] (NRDC), traveling to Canada and Latin America to assist indigenous tribes in protecting their homelands and opposing large-scale energy and extractive projects in remote wilderness areas.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Brancaccio |first=David |date=January 21, 2005 |title=Science and Health: Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. |url=http://www.pbs.org/now/science/kennedy.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171019215936/http://www.pbs.org/now/science/kennedy.html |archive-date=October 19, 2017 |work=[[Now on PBS]]}}</ref>

In 1990, Kennedy assisted indigenous [[Pehuenche]]s in Chile in a partially successful campaign to stop the construction of a series of dams on Chile's iconic [[Biobío River]]. That campaign derailed all but one of the proposed dams.<ref>[[Jon Bowermaster|Bowermaster, Jon]]. (November 1992). [http://www.earthriver.com/library/conservation/11-conservation/town-and-country "Last Run Down the Bio Bio"]. ''Town and Country Travel''. {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171019220247/http://www.earthriver.com/library/conservation/11-conservation/town-and-country |date=October 19, 2017}}</ref> Beginning in 1992, he assisted the [[Cree]] Indians of northern Quebec in their campaign against Hydro-Québec to halt construction of some 600 proposed dams on eleven rivers in [[James Bay]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Cree Chief Wins Environmental Prize For Anti-Dam Effort |work=The Christian Science Monitor |url=https://www.csmonitor.com/1994/0420/20042.html |url-status=live |access-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-date=October 19, 2017 |issn=0882-7729 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171019215821/https://www.csmonitor.com/1994/0420/20042.html}}</ref>

In 1993, Kennedy and NRDC, working with the indigenous rights organization [[Cultural Survival]], clashed with other American environmental groups in a dispute about the rights of Indians to govern their own lands in the [[Oriente (Ecuador)|Oriente]] region of Ecuador.<ref name="auto6">{{Cite magazine |last=Kane |first=Joe |date=September 20, 1993 |title=With Spears From All Sides|url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/1993/09/27/with-spears-from-all-sides |url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171019220608/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/1993/09/27/with-spears-from-all-sides |archive-date=October 19, 2017 |access-date=April 12, 2023 |magazine=The New Yorker}}</ref> Kennedy represented the [[CONFENIAE]], a confederation of indigenous peoples, in negotiation with the American oil company [[Conoco]] to limit oil development in Ecuadorian Amazon and, at the same time, obtain benefits from resource extraction for Amazonian tribes.<ref name="auto6"/> Kennedy was a vocal critic of [[Texaco]] for its previous record of polluting the [[Ecuadorian Amazon|Ecuadoran Amazon]].<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (1991). Foreword for ''Amazon Crude'' by Judith Kimerling. Published by Natural Resource Defense Council. February 19, 1991. p. 131. {{ISBN|0-9609358-5-1}}.</ref>

From 1993 to 1999, Kennedy worked with five [[Vancouver Island]] First Nations in their campaign to end industrial logging by [[MacMillan Bloedel]] in [[Clayoquot Sound]], British Columbia.<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (1995). Foreword for ''Clayoquot Mass Trials: Defending the Rainforest'' by Ronald MacIsaac and Anne Champagne (editors), published by New Society Publisher, February 1995, p. 208. {{ISBN|0-86571-320-0}}.</ref> In 1996, he met with Cuban president [[Fidel Castro]] to persuade him to halt his plans to construct a nuclear power plant at [[Juraguá]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rohter |first=Larry |author-link=Larry Rohter |date=February 19, 1996 |title=Kennedy-Castro Encounter Touched by History |newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1996/02/19/world/kennedy-castro-encounter-touched-by-history.html |url-status=live |access-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171028150309/http://www.nytimes.com/1996/02/19/world/kennedy-castro-encounter-touched-by-history.html |archive-date=October 28, 2017 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> During the meeting, Castro reminisced about Kennedy's father and uncle, speculating that US relations with Cuba would have been far better had President Kennedy not been assassinated.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rohter |first=Larry |author-link=Larry Rohter |date=February 25, 1996 |title=February 18–24;Meeting Across an Old Breach |newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1996/02/25/weekinreview/february-18-24-meeting-across-an-old-breach.html |url-status=live |access-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171028150216/http://www.nytimes.com/1996/02/25/weekinreview/february-18-24-meeting-across-an-old-breach.html |archive-date=October 28, 2017 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

Between 1996 and 2000, Kennedy and the NRDC helped Mexican commercial fishermen halt [[Mitsubishi]]'s proposal to build a salt facility in the [[Laguna San Ignacio]], an area in Baja where [[gray whale]]s breed and nurse their calves.<ref>Smith, Matt (November 28, 2001). [http://www.sfweekly.com/news/the-unlikely-environmentalists "The Unlikely Environmentalists"]. ''SFWeekly''. {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171019220231/http://www.sfweekly.com/news/the-unlikely-environmentalists |date=October 19, 2017}}</ref> Kennedy wrote in opposition to the project, and took the campaign to Japan, meeting with [[Prime Minister of Japan|Japanese Prime Minister]] [[Keizo Obuchi]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kennedy |first=Robert F. Jr. |date=December 3, 1998 |title=Rubbing Salt in the World Heritage Plan |newspaper=[[The Japan Times]]}}</ref> In 2000, he assisted local environmental activists to stop Chaffin Light, a real estate developer, and US engineering giant [[Bechtel]] from building a large hotel and resort development that, Kennedy argued, threatened [[coral reef]]s and public beaches used by local Bahamians, at Clifton Bay, [[New Providence]] Island.<ref>Roberts, Bradley, MP, PLP (November 20, 2005). [http://reearth.org/index.php?p=n§id=4&subcat=38&aid=107 "Bradley Roberts on Clifton"]. Address to Bahamas House of Assembly. {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171020002712/http://reearth.org/index.php?p=n%C2%A7id%3D4&subcat=38&aid=107 |date=October 20, 2017 }}</ref>

Kennedy was one of the early editors of ''[[Indian Country Today]]'', North America's largest [[Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Native American]] newspaper.<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (November 29, 2016). [https://www.ecowatch.com/kennedy-dakota-access-pipeline-2118593345.html?page=2 "I'll See You at Standing Rock"]. ''EcoWatch''. {{Webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171020032256/https://www.ecowatch.com/kennedy-dakota-access-pipeline-2118593345.html?page=2 |date=October 20, 2017}}</ref> He helped lead the opposition to the damming of the [[Futaleufú River]] in the [[Zona Sur|Southern Zone]] of Chile.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Schaffer |first=Grayson |date=August 30, 2016 |title=Chile's Best Whitewater Rivers Won't Be Damned – For Now |magazine=Outside |url=https://www.outsideonline.com/2110076/chiles-best-whitewater-rivers-wont-be-dammed-now |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171019220353/https://www.outsideonline.com/2110076/chiles-best-whitewater-rivers-wont-be-dammed-now |archive-date=October 19, 2017}}</ref> In 2016, due to the pressure precipitated by the Futaleufú Riverkeepers campaign against the dams, the Spanish power company [[Endesa]], which owned the right to dam the river, reversed its decision and relinquished all claims to the Futaleufú.<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (September 12, 2016). [https://www.ecowatch.com/futaleufu-whitewater-rafting-2002171427.html "20 Year David and Goliath Fist Fight Saves Patagonia's Futaleufú"]. ''EcoWatch''. {{Webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171007221222/https://www.ecowatch.com/futaleufu-whitewater-rafting-2002171427.html |date=October 7, 2017}}</ref>

===Military and Vieques===
Kennedy has been a critic of environmental damage by the US military.<ref>{{cite web |last=Zukowski |first=Dan |date=June 22, 2017 |url=https://www.environews.tv/062217-act-war-rfk-jr-puts-u-s-military-cafos-blast-trashing-americas-waterways |title='An Act of War': RFK Jr. Puts U.S. Military and CAFOs on Blast for Trashing America's Waterways |website=EnviroNews |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171022033952/https://www.environews.tv/062217-act-war-rfk-jr-puts-u-s-military-cafos-blast-trashing-americas-waterways/ |archive-date=October 22, 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="RFK Jr.-16 May 2003">{{Cite news |last=Kennedy |first=Robert F. Jr. |date=May 16, 2003 |title=Defending our Environment and Health from the U.S. Military |work=Chicago Tribune |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/2003/05/16/defending-our-environment-and-health-from-the-us-military/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171021220307/http://articles.chicagotribune.com/2003-05-16/news/0305160280_1_superfund-sites-epa-report-pentagon |archive-date=October 21, 2017}}</ref>

In a 2001 article, Kennedy described how he sued the [[US Navy]] on behalf of fishermen and residents of [[Vieques]], an island of [[Puerto Rico]], to stop weapons testing, bombing, and other military exercises. Kennedy argued that the activities were unnecessary, and that the Navy had illegally destroyed several endangered species, polluted the island's waters, harmed the residents' health, and damaged its economy.<ref name="vieques">{{cite magazine |first1=Robert F. Jr. |last1=Kennedy |title=Why Are We in Vieques?|url=https://www.outsideonline.com/1819831/why-are-we-vieques |access-date=January 13, 2020 |magazine=Outside |date=January 10, 2001 |archive-date=October 8, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171008130438/https://www.outsideonline.com/1819831/why-are-we-vieques |url-status=live}}</ref> He was arrested for trespassing at [[Camp Garcia Vieques]], the US Navy training facility, where he and others were protesting the use of a section of the island for training. Kennedy served 30 days in a maximum security prison in Puerto Rico.<ref>{{cite news |last=Greenhouse |first=Steven |author-link=Steven Greenhouse |date=July 16, 2001 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2001/07/16/nyregion/he-s-in-charge-in-demand-and-in-prison.html|title=He's in Charge, in Demand, in Prison|newspaper=The New York Times |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171021221049/http://www.nytimes.com/2001/07/16/nyregion/he-s-in-charge-in-demand-and-in-prison.html |archive-date=October 21, 2017 |access-date=August 9, 2024}}</ref>

The trespassing incident forced the suspension of [[live-fire exercise]]s for almost three hours.<ref>{{Cite news |date=August 2, 2001 |title=US Navy resumes Vieques war games |publisher=BBC News |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/americas/1470871.stm |url-status=live |access-date=August 20, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170629181314/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/americas/1470871.stm |archive-date=June 29, 2017}}</ref> The lawsuits and protests by Kennedy, and hundreds of Puerto Ricans who were also imprisoned, eventually forced the termination of naval bombing in Vieques by [[Presidency of George W. Bush|the Bush administration]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hernandez |first=Raymond |date=June 15, 2001 |title=Both Sides Attack Bush Plan To Halt Bombing on Vieques |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2001/06/15/us/both-sides-attack-bush-plan-to-halt-bombing-on-vieques.html |url-status=live |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171021221048/http://www.nytimes.com/2001/06/15/us/both-sides-attack-bush-plan-to-halt-bombing-on-vieques.html |archive-date=October 21, 2017}}</ref>

In a 2003 article for the ''[[Chicago Tribune]]'', Kennedy called the US federal government "America's biggest polluter" and the [[United States Department of Defense|US Department of Defense]] the worst offender. Citing the [[United States Environmental Protection Agency|Environmental Protection Agency]] (EPA), he wrote, "unexploded ordnance waste can be found on 16,000 military ranges{{nbsp}}... and more than half may contain biological or chemical weapons."<ref name="RFK Jr.-16 May 2003"/>

==Political aspirations==
[[File:Nick DNC etown.jpg|thumb|Kennedy at a taping of [[eTown]] during the [[2008 Democratic National Convention]]]]

=== Candidacy aspirations ===
Kennedy considered running for political office [[2000 United States Senate election in New York|in 2000]] when [[Daniel Patrick Moynihan]], a [[United States Senate|US senator]] from New York, did not seek reelection to the [[List of United States senators from New York|seat]] formerly held by Kennedy's father.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bumiller |first1=Elisabeth |title=Public Lives: Putting Family Life Ahead of a Senate Seat |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1998/11/24/nyregion/public-lives-putting-family-life-ahead-of-a-senate-seat.html |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=November 24, 1988 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113141018/https://www.nytimes.com/1998/11/24/nyregion/public-lives-putting-family-life-ahead-of-a-senate-seat.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2005, Kennedy considered running for New York attorney general in the [[2006 New York Attorney General election|2006 election]], which would have put him up against his then-brother-in-law [[Andrew Cuomo]], but he ultimately chose not to, despite being considered the front-runner.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Jonathan P. |last1=Hicks |title=Robert Kennedy Won't Run for State Attorney General|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2005/01/25/nyregion/robert-kennedy-wont-run-for-state-attorney-general.html |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=The New York Times |date=January 25, 2005 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140958/https://www.nytimes.com/2005/01/25/nyregion/robert-kennedy-wont-run-for-state-attorney-general.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

On December 2, 2008, Kennedy said he did not want New York governor [[David Paterson]] to nominate him to the US Senate seat to be vacated by [[Hillary Clinton]], Obama's nominee for [[United States Secretary of State|Secretary of State]]. Some outlets indicated that Kennedy was a possible candidate for the position. He said that Senate service would leave him too little time with his family.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Jonathan P. |last1=Hicks |title=Robert F. Kennedy's Son Not Interested in Senate Seat|url=https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2008/12/02/robert-f-kennedys-son-not-interested-in-senate-seat |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=The New York Times |date=December 2, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191006100343/https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2008/12/02/robert-f-kennedys-son-not-interested-in-senate-seat |archive-date=October 6, 2019}}</ref>

=== 2000s consideration for top environmental jobs ===
As a "well-respected climate lawyer" in the 2000s,<ref name=":4"/> Kennedy was "often linked to top environmental jobs in Democratic administrations", including in the [[2000 United States presidential election|2000]], [[2004 United States presidential election|2004]], and [[2008 United States presidential election|2008 presidential elections]].<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Bogardus |first=Kevin |date=November 13, 2023 |title=RFK Jr. was linked to – but never got – top-tier environmental job |url=https://subscriber.politicopro.com/article/eenews/2023/11/13/rfk-jr-was-linked-to-but-never-got-top-tier-environmental-job-00126798 |access-date=March 30, 2024 |newspaper=[[Politico]] }}</ref> He was considered as a potential chair of the [[White House Council on Environmental Quality]] for [[Al Gore]] in 2000 and considered for the role of [[Administrator of the Environmental Protection Agency|EPA administrator]] under [[John Kerry]] in 2004 and [[Barack Obama]] in 2008.<ref name=":5"/>

According to ''[[Politico]]'', the Obama transition team decided not to nominate Kennedy due to his past heroin conviction and opposition from Senate Republicans. Then [[United States Chamber of Commerce]] lobbyist [[William Kovacic|William Kovacs]] said that Kennedy's nomination "would speak volumes as to where Obama is going with his appointments{{nbsp}}... A Kennedy appointment is as liberal as you can possibly get{{nbsp}}... There is no one [candidate] based firmer in extremes."<ref name=":4"/> Republican Senator [[Jim Inhofe]] of Oklahoma also criticized the proposal, saying Kennedy was too radical and would further a left-wing agenda if appointed.<ref name=":4"/>

===2024 presidential campaign===
{{Main|Robert F. Kennedy Jr. 2024 presidential campaign}}
[[File:Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. with supporter (53514292813).jpg|thumb|Kennedy with a supporter during his 2024 campaign]]
In a speech in New Hampshire on March 3, 2023, Kennedy said he was considering a [[2024 United States presidential election|run for president in 2024]]: "I am thinking about it. I've passed the biggest hurdle, which is that my wife has greenlighted it."<ref name="foxmarch3">{{cite news |last1=Phippen |first1=Thomas |last2=Steinhauser |first2=Paul |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr 'thinking about' launching Democratic challenge to Biden for 2024 White House nomination |url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/robert-f-kennedy-jr-becomes-second-democrat-challenge-biden-2024-nomination |access-date=March 3, 2023 |publisher=[[Fox News]] |date=March 3, 2023 |archive-date=April 7, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230407203042/https://www.foxnews.com/politics/robert-f-kennedy-jr-becomes-second-democrat-challenge-biden-2024-nomination |url-status=live}}</ref>

Kennedy filed his candidacy for the [[2024 Democratic Party presidential primaries|Democratic presidential nomination]] on April 5, 2023.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Price |first1=Michelle |date=April 5, 2023 |title=Anti-vaccine activist RFK Jr. challenging Biden in 2024 |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |url=https://apnews.com/article/robert-kennedy-jr-presidential-campaign-9fb5ed5c8e1fd31d2a4458d44b086593 |access-date=April 5, 2023 |archive-date=April 5, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230405224314/https://apnews.com/article/robert-kennedy-jr-presidential-campaign-9fb5ed5c8e1fd31d2a4458d44b086593 |url-status=live}}</ref> He formally declared his candidacy at a campaign launch event at the [[Boston Park Plaza|Park Plaza Hotel]] in [[Boston]] on April 19.<ref>{{cite web |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. to formally announce 2024 run for president in Boston |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/boston/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-president-race-2024-boston-massachusetts/|website=[[CBS News]] |agency=Associated Press |date=April 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230415184732/https://www.cbsnews.com/boston/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-president-race-2024-boston-massachusetts/ |archive-date=April 15, 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> On October 9, he became an [[Independent politician|independent]] candidate [[Third-party and independent candidates for the 2024 United States presidential election|in the election]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Pellish |first=Aaron |date=October 9, 2023 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. announces independent run for president, ending Democratic primary challenge to Biden|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/10/09/politics/kennedy-independent-campaign/index.html |access-date=October 14, 2023 |publisher=[[CNN]]}}</ref> He is the fifth member of his family to seek the presidency.{{efn|name=kennedy family|[[John F. Kennedy]] ran a [[John F. Kennedy 1960 presidential campaign|successful presidential campaign]] and was [[1960 United States presidential election|elected in 1960]]. [[Robert F. Kennedy]] ran for the [[1968 Democratic Party presidential primaries|Democratic nomination]] for president [[1968 United States presidential election|in 1968]],<ref name="Greenfield 2019 f993">{{cite web | last=Greenfield | first=Jeff | title=How RFK Could Have Become President | website=The Daily Beast | date=December 3, 2019 | url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/how-rfk-could-have-become-president | access-date=July 17, 2023}}</ref> but [[Assassination of Robert F. Kennedy|was assassinated]] in June that year. Kennedy's uncle-by-marriage [[Sargent Shriver]] ran for the nomination [[1976 Democratic Party presidential primaries|in 1976]],<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Koster |first=R. M. |author-link=R. M. Koster |date=February 1976 |title=The Democratic Super Bowl |magazine=[[Harper's]] |publisher=Harper's Foundation |volume=252 |issue=1509 |pages=14–17 |url=https://harpers.org/archive/1976/02/the-democratic-super-bowl/ |access-date=November 18, 2018 }}{{subscription required}}</ref> but later withdrew from the race. [[Ted Kennedy]] ran for the [[1980 Democratic Party presidential primaries|Democratic nomination]] in the [[1980 United States presidential election|1980 election]],<ref name="Farrell 2022 n815">{{cite magazine | last=Farrell | first=John A. | title=Ted Kennedy's Complicated Legacy, from Chappaquidick to Senate Lion | magazine=Time | date=October 29, 2022 | url=https://time.com/6226087/edward-kennedy-biography/ | access-date=July 17, 2023 | archive-date=July 17, 2023 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230717104450/https://time.com/6226087/edward-kennedy-biography/ | url-status=dead }}</ref> but was defeated in the primaries by incumbent Democratic president [[Jimmy Carter]].}}

Listing many false conspiracy theories that Kennedy used during campaign appearances, [[PolitiFact]] named his presidential campaign its 2023 "lie of the year".<ref name="PolitiFactLOTY2023">{{Cite web |first2=Katie |last2=Sanders |first1=Madison |last1=Czopek |date=December 21, 2023 |title=Lie of the Year: RFK Jr.'s conspiracy theory campaign |url=https://www.politifact.com/article/2023/dec/21/robert-f-kennedy-jrs-campaign-of-conspiracy-theori/ |access-date=November 28, 2024 |website=[[PolitiFact]]}}</ref>

In May 2024, Kennedy was considered for the [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian Party's]] nomination for president, but lost to [[Chase Oliver]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/05/26/politics/rfk-jr-nominated-libertarian-party/index.html|title=RFK Jr. loses in first round of Libertarian Party's presidential nomination vote. Trump didn't file paperwork to qualify|first=Aaron|last=Pellish|date=May 26, 2024|website=CNN|access-date=February 13, 2025}}</ref> In Colorado, the state [[Libertarian Party of Colorado|Libertarian Party]] selected Kennedy, but Oliver appeared on the ballot as the Libertarian nominee.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cpr.org/2024/07/24/rfk-jr-wont-be-colorado-libertarian-president-nominee-chase-oliver-will/|title=RFK Jr. probably won't be the Libertarian nominee in Colorado, Chase Oliver will|first=Andrew|last=Kenney|date=July 24, 2024|website=Colorado Public Radio|access-date=February 13, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.coloradopolitics.com/elections/2024/colorado-libertarians-designate-rfk-jr-to-states-november-ballot-after-snubbing-partys-own-ticket/article_74f7e22c-38e2-11ef-bfcc-af2936a5dbee.html|title=Colorado Libertarians designate RFK Jr. to state's November ballot after snubbing party's own ticket|first=Ernest|last=Luning|date=July 3, 2024|website=Colorado Politics|access-date=February 13, 2025}}</ref>

Kennedy's campaign was noted for receiving significant support from Republican donors and Trump allies who believed he would serve as a "[[spoiler effect|spoiler]]", taking the votes of those who would have otherwise voted for the Democratic nominee.<ref name="NYTApril10">{{cite news|title=Trump Allies Have a Plan to Hurt Biden's Chances: Elevate Outsider Candidates|last1=Swan|first1=Jonathan|last2=Haberman|first2=Maggie|last3=Goldmacher|first3=Shane|last4=Davis O'Brien|first4=Rebecca|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/10/us/politics/trump-rfk-jr-biden.html|date=April 10, 2024|work=The New York Times|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240410150345/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/10/us/politics/trump-rfk-jr-biden.html|archive-date=April 10, 2024|access-date=April 10, 2024|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> In August 2023, it was revealed that [[Timothy Mellon]], who gave $15 million to [[Donald Trump]]'s [[super PAC]] [[Make America Great Again Inc.|MAGA Inc.]], also donated $5 million to Kennedy's super PAC, making him Kennedy's largest single donor.<ref name=NYTApril10 /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Yang |first=Mary |date=August 2, 2023 |title=Robert F Kennedy Jr's campaign bankrolled by Republican mega-donor |work=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/02/robert-f-kennedy-jr-republican-donor-super-pac |access-date=August 2, 2023}}</ref> Mellon donated another $5 million to Kennedy's super PAC in April and another $50 million to MAGA Inc. in May.<ref>{{cite news |last=Kamisar |first=Ben |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/timothy-mellon-leads-huge-month-trump-megadonors-pouring-cash-election-rcna158204 |title=Pro-Trump businessman leads huge month for megadonors pouring cash into election |work=[[NBC News]] |date=June 21, 2024 |access-date=June 21, 2024}}</ref><ref name="donor">{{cite news |last1=Goldmacher |first1=Shane |last2=Schleifer |first2=Theodore |title=Timothy Mellon, Secretive Donor, Gives $50 Million to Pro-Trump Group |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/06/20/us/politics/timothy-mellon-trump-donation.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=June 20, 2024 |access-date=June 21, 2024}}</ref> In July 2024, Forbes reported that Mellon had donated $25 million to Kennedy and Kennedy-affiliated groups.<ref>{{cite news |last=Kamin |first=Leo |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/leokamin/2024/07/14/here-are-robert-f-kennedy-jrs-richest-donors/ |title=Here Are Robert F. Kennedy Jr.'s Richest Donors |work=[[Forbes]] |date=July 17, 2024 |access-date=September 2, 2024}}</ref>

In August, facing declining poll numbers, limited campaign funds, and increasing challenges to ballot access, the Kennedy campaign began appealing to the Harris and Trump campaigns, seeking a cabinet post in exchange for an endorsement. Harris reportedly rebuffed Kennedy,<ref>{{cite web |last=Ladden-Hall |first=Dan |date=August 15, 2024 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Lashes Kamala Harris for Spurning His Job-for-Quitting Deal |url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr-tears-into-kamala-harris-after-she-ignored-his-deal-offer |access-date=August 23, 2024 |website=[[The Daily Beast]] |publisher=}}</ref> but Trump said he "probably would [consider the offer], if something like that would happen".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/08/20/politics/trump-consider-appointing-rfk-jr-administration-role/index.html|title=Trump says he'd consider appointing RFK Jr. to role in administration|last=Bradner|first=Eric|publisher=[[CNN]]|date=August 20, 2024|access-date=August 23, 2024}}</ref> On August 22, the Kennedy campaign filed to be removed from the Arizona ballot amid reports he would drop out to endorse Trump.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/08/22/us/elections/rfk-jr-arizona-withdraw.html |title=Kennedy Withdraws From Presidential Contest in Arizona |last1=O'Brien |first1=Rebecca Davis |last2=Browning |first2=Kellen |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=August 23, 2024 |access-date=August 23, 2024 }}</ref>

On August 23, Kennedy dropped out and endorsed Trump, saying he intended to maintain ballot placement in certain non-[[swing state]]s.<ref name="NBCEndorsed">{{cite web|access-date=August 23, 2024|title=RFK Jr. endorses Trump after weeks of back-channel courtship|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/rfk-jr-says-court-filing-endorsing-trump-ending-presidential-hopes-rcna165936|date=August 23, 2024|website=NBC News}}</ref><ref name="AP2024-08-23">{{cite news |url=https://apnews.com/article/rfk-jr-trump-speech-arizona-a2638f89ddcb5de03edbe4574ca17d45 |title=RFK Jr. suspends his presidential bid and backs Donald Trump before appearing with him at his rally |first1=Jonathan J. |last1=Cooper |first2=Michelle L. |last2=Price |first3=Gabriel |last3=Sandoval |date=August 23, 2024 |publisher=AP News |access-date=August 23, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/08/23/politics/rfk-jr-suspends-campaign/index.html |title=RFK Jr. suspends presidential campaign and endorses Trump|last1=Pellish|first1=Aaron|last2=Dovere |first2=Edward-Isaac |author-link3=Edward-Isaac Dovere |publisher=[[CNN]]|date=August 23, 2024 |access-date=August 23, 2024 }}</ref> This was a reversal for Kennedy, who had previously said he would "under no circumstances" join Trump on a presidential ticket, that his and Trump's positions "could not be further apart", and that Trump was a "terrible human being", a "discredit to democracy", and "probably a sociopath".<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Roush |first=Ty |date=August 23, 2024 |title=RFK Jr. Endorses Trump After Calling Him 'Sociopath' — His Reversal, Explained |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/tylerroush/2024/08/23/rfk-jr-endorses-trump-after-calling-him-sociopath---his-reversal-explained/ |access-date=August 24, 2024 |magazine=Forbes |language=en}}</ref><ref name="kennedytrump">{{cite news |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/rfk-jr-joins-trump-onstage-arizona-rally-hours-endorsing-rcna167948 |title=RFK Jr. joins Trump onstage at Arizona rally hours after endorsing him |first1=Megan |last1=Lebowitz |first2=Jake |last2=Traylor |first3=Alex |last3=Tabet |publisher=NBC News |date=August 23, 2024 |access-date=August 24, 2024 }}</ref><ref  name="AP2024-08-23"/> In his speech endorsing Trump, Kennedy described speaking with Trump and his advisers and said he discovered that he and Trump were "aligned on many key issues".<ref name="NBCEndorsed" />

In December, Kennedy was featured in the documentary film ''[[Inactive, Americaʼs Silent Killer]]'', about the growing global epidemic of physical inactivity and its impact on health, particularly in North America.<ref name="RadioCanada">{{cite web |author-first1=Michel |author-last1=Bédard |title=Arts – INactive, un documentaire de Karl Talbot sur le phénomène de l'obésité |url=https://ici.radio-canada.ca/ohdio/premiere/emissions/premiere-heure/segments/rattrapage/1960386/arts-inactive-un-documentaire-karl-talbot-sur-phenomene-obesite |website=[[Canadian Broadcasting Corporation|Radio–Canada]] |date=January 13, 2025 |language=fr |trans-title=Arts – INactive, a documentary by Karl Talbot on the phenomenon of obesity}}</ref>

==Secretary of Health and Human Services (2025–present)==

=== Nomination and confirmation ===
[[File:HHS Secretary Nominee Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Testifies at Confirmation Hearing - January 29, 2025.webm|thumb|thumbtime=44:05|Kennedy testifies at his first Senate confirmation hearing on January 29, 2025, before the Senate Finance Committee.]]
Days before the [[2024 United States presidential election]], [[Donald Trump]] said that Kennedy would have "a big role in health care". According to ''[[The Washington Post]]'', Kennedy's position was not originally meant to be one requiring Senate confirmation.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Reich |first=Greta |date=November 1, 2024 |title=Trump on RFK Jr.: 'He's going to have a big role in health care' |url=https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2024/11/01/2024-elections-live-coverage-updates-analysis/trump-rfk-jr-health-care-vaccines-00186837 |access-date=November 2, 2024 |work=[[Politico]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Diamond |first1=Dan |last2=Weber |first2=Lauren |last3=Dawsey |first3=Josh |author-link3=Josh Dawsey |last4=Scherer |first4=Michael |author-link4=Michael Scherer (journalist) |last5=Roubein |first5=Rachel |date=October 31, 2024 |title=RFK Jr. set for major food, health role in potential Trump administration |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2024/10/31/rfk-trump-administration-health-food-safety/ |access-date=November 2, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> On November 14, after winning the election, Trump announced his intention to nominate Kennedy for [[United States Secretary of Health and Human Services|secretary of health and human services]] (HHS).<ref>{{cite news|last1=Collins|first1=Kaitlan|last2=Holmes|first2=Kristen|last3=Pellish|first3=Aaron|date=November 14, 2024|title=Trump picks Robert F. Kennedy Jr. to be his Department of Health and Human Services secretary|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2024/11/14/politics/robert-f-kennedy-donald-trump-hhs/index.html|access-date=November 25, 2024|work=[[CNN]]}}</ref><ref name="v502">{{cite magazine |last=Dorn |first=Sara |date=November 14, 2024 |title=Trump Taps RFK Jr. As Secretary Of Health And Human Services: Here Are All The Conspiracies He's Promoted |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2024/11/14/rfk-jr-launches-independent-2024-run-here-are-all-the-conspiracies-he-promotes-from-vaccines-to-mass-shootings/ |access-date=November 15, 2024 |magazine=Forbes}}</ref> During the week of December 16, 2024, Kennedy began meeting with senators in advance of his confirmation hearings.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 16, 2024 |title=Robert F. Kenndy Jr. hitting Capitol Hill to lobby lawmakers ahead of Senate confirmation hearings |url=https://www.wptv.com/us-news/robert-f-kenndy-jr-hitting-capitol-hill-to-lobby-lawmakers-ahead-of-senate-confirmation-hearings |access-date=December 17, 2024 |website=WPTV News Channel 5 West Palm |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=December 16, 2024 |title=Trump health pick RFK Jr. begins meeting with Republican senators on Capitol Hill |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/trump-health-pick-rfk-jr-hold-first-meetings-senators-week-rcna184362 |access-date=December 17, 2024 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Weiland |first1=Noah |last2=Miller |first2=Maya C. |date=December 16, 2024 |title=R.F.K. Jr., Trump's Health Secretary Pick, Meets With Lawmakers |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/12/16/us/politics/rfk-jr-republican-senators-health-secretary-bid.html |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241218043055/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/12/16/us/politics/rfk-jr-republican-senators-health-secretary-bid.html |archive-date=December 18, 2024 |access-date=December 16, 2024 |work=The New York Times}}{{cbignore}}</ref> In January 2025, the [[United States Senate Committee on Finance|Senate Committee on Finance]] and the [[United States Senate Committee on Health, Education, Labor and Pensions|Senate Health, Education, Labor, and Pensions Committee]] (HELP) held hearings on Kennedy's nomination.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hearing to consider the nomination of Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., of California, to be Secretary of Health and Human Services {{!}} The United States Senate Committee on Finance |url=https://www.finance.senate.gov/hearings/hearingto-consider-the-nomination-of-robert-f-kennedy-jr-of-california-to-be-secretary-of-health-and-human-services |access-date=January 25, 2025 |website=www.finance.senate.gov |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Nomination of Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. to serve as Secretary of Health and Human Services {{!}} The U.S. Senate Committee on Health, Education, Labor & Pensions |url=https://www.help.senate.gov/hearings/nomination-of-robert-f-kennedy-jr-to-serve-as-secretary-of-health-and-human-services |access-date=January 25, 2025 |website=www.help.senate.gov |language=en}}</ref> Senator [[Bernie Sanders]], the committee's [[ranking member]], was critical of Kennedy during the hearing.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Stolberg |first1=Sheryl Gay |last2=Ruberg |first2=Sara |last3=Mandavilli |first3=Apoorva |date=January 30, 2025 |title=Sanders and Kennedy Trade Barbs on Vaccines and Health Care |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/01/30/us/politics/sanders-rfk-jr-vaccines-hearing.html |access-date=February 4, 2025 |work=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

In December 2024, more than 75 [[List of Nobel laureates|Nobel Laureates]] urged the [[US Senate]] to oppose Kennedy's nomination, saying he would "put the public's health in jeopardy".<ref name="Nobel Letter">{{Cite news |date=December 9, 2024 |title=Read the Letter From Nobel Laureates Urging That Mr. Kennedy Not be Confirmed |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/12/09/health/rfkltr.html |access-date=December 10, 2024 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="Nobel Times">{{Cite news |last=Rosenbluth |first=Teddy |date=December 9, 2024 |title=Nobel Laureates Urge Senate to Turn Down Kennedy's Nomination |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/12/09/health/kennedy-hhs-nobel-laureates.html |work=The New York Times}}</ref> As of January 9, 2025, over 17,000 doctors who are members of Committee to Protect Health Care had signed an open letter urging the Senate to oppose Kennedy's nomination,<ref name="17K">{{Cite web |last=Frazier |first=Kierra |date=January 9, 2025 |title=Over 17,000 doctors sign letter urging Senate to reject RFK Jr. as health secretary - CBS News |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/doctors-senate-rfk-jr-health-secretary/ |access-date=January 11, 2025 |website=www.cbsnews.com }}</ref> arguing that Kennedy had spent decades undermining public confidence in vaccines and spreading false claims and conspiracy theories,<ref name="17K Letter">{{Cite web |title=Physicians Take Action |url=https://committeetoprotect.org/RFK-ACTION/ |access-date=January 11, 2025 |website=Committee to Protect Health Care }}{{Dead link|date=September 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> that he was a danger to national healthcare, and that he was unqualified to lead the Department of Health and Human Services.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Heuer |first=Mike |date=January 9, 2025 |title=17,000 doctors call RFK Jr. 'actively dangerous' to national healthcare |url=https://www.upi.com/Top_News/US/2025/01/09/kennedy-hhs-doctors-oppose/6501736457368/ |access-date=January 25, 2025 |website=UPI }}</ref> As of January 24, 2025, more than 80 organizations had voiced opposition to Kennedy's nomination.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last1=Erman |first1=Michael |last2=Kelly |first2=Stephanie |date=January 24, 2025 |title=Opponents of Kennedy's bid for top US health post urge senators to reject him |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/opponents-kennedys-bid-top-health-post-urge-senators-reject-him-2025-01-24/ |access-date=February 9, 2025 |website=[[Reuters]]}}</ref>

Kennedy disclosed to an HHS ethics official his arrangement with the law firm Wisner Baum, which specializes in pharmaceutical drug injury cases, under which Kennedy earns 10% of the fees awarded in contingency cases he refers to the firm. If confirmed as HHS director, Kennedy would retain the arrangement only in cases that do not directly affect the federal government.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Mansfield |first=Erin |title=RFK Jr. says he'll keep making money on anti-vaccine lawsuits while in office |url=https://www.statesman.com/story/news/politics/elections/2025/01/23/robert-f-kennedy-jr-ethics-agreement/77903354007/ |access-date=January 26, 2025 |website=Austin American-Statesman |language=en-US }}{{Dead link|date=January 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> He listed his income from Wisner Baum for this arrangement as $856,559. Before assuming the position of director of HHS, he will receive from the law firm the complete and final payments for concluded cases against the US government.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://thehill.com/newsletters/health-care/5101678-rfk-jr-releases-financial-disclosure/ |title=RFK Jr. releases financial disclosure |work=The Hill |date=January 22, 2025}}</ref>{{Update inline|date=February 2025}} He added that will assign his son his interests in litigation against the maker of [[Gardasil]], a vaccine given to prevent [[cervical cancer]] caused by [[human papillomavirus]] (HPV).<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Jewett |first1=Christina |last2=Craig |first2=Susanne |last3=Stolberg |first3=Sheryl Gay |date=January 31, 2025 |title=Kennedy Says He Will Give HPV Vaccine Lawsuit Proceeds to His Son |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/01/31/health/rfk-jr-gardasil-hpv-lawsuits-confirmation.html |access-date=February 24, 2025 |work=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

On February 4, 2025, the Senate Committee on Finance voted 14–13 to forward Kennedy's nomination to a full Senate vote.<ref name="auto11">{{Cite news |last=Stolberg |first=Sheryl Gay |date=February 4, 2025 |title=RFK Jr.'s Nomination as Health Secretary Passes Committee Vote: Trump Administration Live Updates |url=https://www.nytimes.com/live/2025/02/04/us/trump-administration-rfk-jr-gabbard|access-date=February 4, 2025 |work=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The deciding vote was from [[Bill Cassidy]], who was originally hesitant, but said he had received "serious commitments" from the Trump administration and "honest counsel" from Vice President [[JD Vance]] in exchange for his support of Kennedy's nomination.<ref name="auto11" /> According to the Senate HELP Committee site, Cassidy, a doctor who practiced for 30 years before becoming a politician, told the committee that he had had a patient with [[acute hepatitis B]] who needed a [[liver transplant]] and had to be transported by [[Medical evacuation|Medivac]]. He called the transplant "an invasive, quarter-of-a-million-dollar surgery—in 2000—that, even if successful, would leave this young woman with a lifetime of $50,000 per year medical bills", adding, "As I saw her take off, I was so depressed. A $50 of vaccine could have prevented this all".<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 30, 2025 |title=Chair Cassidy Delivers Remarks During Hearing on Nomination for HHS Secretary {{!}} The U.S. Senate Committee on Health, Education, Labor & Pensions |url=https://www.help.senate.gov/rep/newsroom/press/chair-cassidy-delivers-remarks-during-hearing-on-nomination-for-hhs-secretary |access-date=February 11, 2025 |website=www.help.senate.gov |language=en}}</ref> Of the two committees that Kennedy spoke before, only the Senate Finance was to vote on his nomination.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/5124466-gabbard-rfk-jr-confirmation-committee-votes/|title=Gabbard, RFK Jr. face make-or-break moment after perilous hearings|last=Weaver|first=Al|date=February 4, 2025|quote="Kennedy appeared for two confirmation hearings, but only the Finance Committee will vote on whether to advance him."|access-date=February 4, 2025|work=The Hill}}</ref>

On February 13, 2025, the Senate confirmed Kennedy as Secretary of Health and Human Services by a vote of 52 to 48, with former [[Senate Republican Conference]] leader [[Mitch McConnell]] the sole Republican to vote against him. A [[polio]] survivor, McConnell was critical of efforts to revoke approval of the [[polio vaccine]]. He said, "Anyone seeking the Senate's consent to serve in the incoming administration would do well to steer clear of even the appearance of association with such efforts." All Democrats also voted against Kennedy.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/5141880-robert-f-kennedy-jr-confirmed/|title=Senate confirms RFK Jr. as Health secretary; McConnell lone GOP dissenter |last=Weixel |first=Nathaniel |date=February 13, 2025 |access-date=February 13, 2025 |work=The Hill }}</ref>

On March 16, 2026, Massachusetts District Court judge Brian Murphy ordered the reversal of all decisions made by the Advisory Committee for Immunization Practices by Kennedy-appointed panelists. Murphy found that the government had disregarded the [[scientific method]] and "thereby undermined the integrity of its actions". His ruling was in response to a lawsuit brought by six medical organizations that contended Kennedy and his appointees had made "arbitrary and capricious" changes to the childhood vaccine schedule, bypassing the evidence-based practice that had underpinned it.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/16/health/childhood-vaccines-lawsuit-kennedy.html| title=Judge Strikes Down Kennedy's Vaccine Policies | publisher=[[The New York Times]]| date=March 16, 2026 | accessdate=March 17, 2026 | last=Mandavilli | first=Apoorva }}</ref>

=== Tenure ===
[[File:President Trump Participates in Swearing in Ceremony for the Secretary of Health and Human Services (2160p 60fps VP9-128kbit AAC).webm|thumb|thumbtime=7:44|Kennedy being sworn in as the [[secretary of health and human services]] on February 13, 2025.]]

On February 13, 2025, Kennedy was sworn in as the 26th secretary of health and human services in the [[Oval Office]] by Justice [[Neil Gorsuch]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/rfk-trump-health-secretary-vote-5dbefeef0537dc241e6fb33b8f2a748b|title=Vaccine skeptic Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is sworn in as Trump's health chief after a close Senate vote|date=February 13, 2025|access-date=February 13, 2025|work=Associated Press|last=Seitz|first=Amanda}}</ref> He is the first [[Independent politician|independent]] or [[Third party (U.S. politics)|third-party]] presidential candidate to become a [[Cabinet of the United States|cabinet member]] after running for president.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Winger |first=Richard |date=February 13, 2025 |title=U.S. Senate Confirms Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. for Cabinet |url=https://ballot-access.org/2025/02/13/u-s-senate-confirms-robert-f-kennedy-jr-for-cabinet/ |access-date=February 14, 2025 |website=Ballot Access News }}</ref>

==== "Make America Healthy Again" executive order ====
Minutes after Kennedy was sworn in, Trump signed Executive Order 14211, which ordered the creation of a "[[Make America Healthy Again]]" [[MAHA Commission|(MAHA) Commission]] to be chaired by Kennedy.<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 19, 2025 |title=RFK Jr. and Trump take aim at mental health meds. Here's what to know |url=https://www.seattletimes.com/seattle-news/mental-health/rfk-jr-and-trumps-new-commission-targets-mental-health-medication/ |access-date=February 21, 2025 |website=The Seattle Times }}</ref><ref name=":9">{{Cite web |date=February 13, 2025 |title=Establishing the President's Make America Healthy Again Commission |url=https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/02/establishing-the-presidents-make-america-healthy-again-commission/ |access-date=February 21, 2025 |website=The White House }}</ref> Its objectives include investigating the incidence and causes of chronic childhood diseases and "assess[ing] the prevalence of and threat posed by the prescription of [[selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor]]s, [[antipsychotic]]s, [[mood stabilizer]]s, stimulants, and weight-loss drugs".<ref name=":9" />

==== Firing staff====
On February 14, 2025, agencies including the [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention]] (CDC) and the [[National Institutes of Health]] (NIH) were informed that approximately 5,200 newly hired federal health workers were to be fired that day.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Owermohle |first1=Sarah |last2=Lawrence |first2=Lizzy |last3=Branswell |first3=Helen |last4=Herper |first4=Matthew |date=February 14, 2025 |title=Trump administration to fire thousands at health agencies |url=https://www.statnews.com/2025/02/14/federal-employee-layoffs-at-hhs-cdc-on-first-day-robert-kennedy-health-secretary/ |access-date=February 15, 2025 |website=STAT }}</ref>

In April 2025, Kennedy fired most of the staff of the [[National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health]], shuttering nearly all its departments. Programs, including approvals of new workplace safety equipment and research into firefighter health, were abruptly canceled.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 3, 2025 |title=Worker safety agency NIOSH lays off most remaining staff|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/worker-safety-agency-niosh-lays-off-most-remaining-staff/ |access-date=May 3, 2025 |website=CBS News }}</ref>

In June 2025, Kennedy announced that he was removing all 17 members of the [[Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices]] (ACIP) and replacing them with new members.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kekatos |first=Mary |date=June 9, 2025 |title=RFK Jr. removes all 17 members of CDC's vaccine advisory committee |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Health/rfk-jr-removing-17-members-cdcs-vaccine-advisory/story?id=122670046 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250610022328/https://abcnews.go.com/Health/rfk-jr-removing-17-members-cdcs-vaccine-advisory/story?id=122670046 |archive-date=June 10, 2025 |access-date=June 10, 2025 |website=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] }}</ref>

==== Stopping ads for vaccine to reduce severity of seasonal flu ====
On February 20, 2025, during an unusually severe influenza season, HHS instructed the CDC to suspend its ad campaign promoting flu vaccination. The advertising, in part a response to declining flu vaccination rates, promoted the message that vaccination would result in much milder symptoms and lower chances of becoming severely ill for those with the flu.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stone |first=Will |date=February 20, 2025 |title=Trump administration yanks CDC flu vaccine campaign |url=https://www.npr.org/sections/shots-health-news/2025/02/19/nx-s1-5302906/cdc-flu-vaccine-campaign-terminated |access-date=February 24, 2025 |work=NPR }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Branswell |first=Helen |date=February 20, 2025 |title=HHS orders CDC to halt some vaccine ads, saying RFK Jr. wants message focused on 'informed consent' |url=https://www.statnews.com/2025/02/20/cdc-vaccine-promotions-rfk-jr-informed-consent/ |access-date=February 24, 2025 |website=[[Stat (website)|STAT News]] }}</ref>

==== 2025 Southwest United States measles outbreak ====
{{Main|2025 Southwest United States measles outbreak}}

Kennedy's tenure began during a measles outbreak in the southwestern US, including the first measles death in a decade. The [[Texas Department of State Health Services]] reported 146 cases, 20 hospitalizations, and one death in late February.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Measles Outbreak – Feb. 28, 2025 {{!}} Texas DSHS |url=https://www.dshs.texas.gov/news-alerts/measles-outbreak-feb-28-2025 |access-date=February 28, 2025 |website=www.dshs.texas.gov}}</ref> In his first public comments, on February 26, Kennedy said there had been two deaths and that "there have been four measles outbreaks this year. In this country last year there were 16. So it's not unusual. We have measles outbreaks every year." Such outbreaks of the disease had been declared domestically eliminated until the [[measles resurgence in the United States]] that began in the 2010s.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Paules |first1=Catharine I. |last2=Marston |first2=Hilary D. |last3=Fauci |first3=Anthony S. |date=June 6, 2019 |title=Measles in 2019 — Going Backward |url=http://www.nejm.org/doi/10.1056/NEJMp1905099 |journal=The New England Journal of Medicine |language=en |volume=380 |issue=23 |pages=2185–2187 |doi=10.1056/NEJMp1905099 |pmid=30995368 |issn=0028-4793|url-access=subscription }}</ref> He also falsely claimed that the people hospitalized were done so "mainly for quarantine",<ref>{{Cite web |last=McDonald|first=Jessica |date=February 27, 2025 |title=Fact check: RFK Jr. minimizes measles outbreak in Texas |url=https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/national-international/fact-check-rfk-jr-minimizes-measles-outbreak-in-texas/6167365/ |access-date=February 28, 2025 |website=NBC New York |language=en-US}}</ref> a claim healthcare professionals refuted.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Agrawal |first=Nina |date=February 28, 2025 |title=Measles Outbreaks: What to Know About Virus Severity and Vaccine Effectiveness |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/02/28/well/measles-outbreaks-texas-rfk-jr.html |access-date=February 28, 2025 |work=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> US Senator [[Ron Wyden]] wrote: "Nothing about kids dying from measles is normal. Anti-vaxxers like RFK Jr. and the Republicans who enable them are responsible for every single one of these deaths."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/rfk-claims-measles-outbreak-that-killed-first-us-kid-in-decades-is-totally-normal/|title=RFK Dismisses Measles Outbreak That Killed Kid as No Biggie|work=[[The Daily Beast]]|date=February 26, 2025|accessdate=February 27, 2025}}</ref>

Days later, Kennedy called the outbreak a "top priority" for the department.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/01/kennedy-jr-measles-outbreak-health-department |title=Kennedy Jr backtracks and says US measles outbreak is now a 'top priority' for health department |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=March 1, 2025 |accessdate=March 1, 2025}}</ref> His messaging regarding the outbreak cited fringe theories blaming poor diet and health. He promoted [[cod liver oil]], steroid inhalation, an antibiotic, [[vitamin A]], and other questionable treatments that he called "almost miraculous". While recommending vaccination against measles, he also overstated the vaccine's potential harms and suggested that immunity from natural infection would be better. The CDC says that the MMR vaccine is "much safer than getting measles, mumps, or rubella". The antibiotic is not effective against a viral disease such as measles. Vitamin A is part of measles treatment, primarily in areas where children may be deficient in it.<ref>{{cite news |last=Rosenbluth |first=Teddy |date=March 10, 2025 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/03/10/health/measles-texas-kennedy-fox.html |title=Kennedy Links Measles Outbreak to Poor Diet and Health, Citing Fringe Theories |work=The New York Times}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |first=Maria |last=Godoy |date=March 7, 2020 |title=Amid a growing measles outbreak, doctors worry RFK Jr. is sending the wrong message |url=https://www.npr.org/2025/03/07/nx-s1-5320352/measles-rfk-west-texas-outbreak |access-date=March 12, 2025 |work=NPR }}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |first=Steve |last=Benen |date=March 11, 2025 |title=New State Reports Measles Cases as RFK Jr. Pushes Fake Cures |url=https://newrepublic.com/post/192616/robert-f-kennedy-jr-oklahoma-measles|access-date=March 12, 2025 |magazine=The New Republic}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |date=March 12, 2025 |title=RFK Jr. Makes Unhinged Claim About Measles Vaccine as Cases Rise |url=https://newrepublic.com/post/192641/robert-f-kennedy-jr-claim-measles-vaccine |access-date=March 12, 2025 |magazine=The New Republic}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |first=Nathaniel |last=Weixel |date=March 13, 2025 |title=Experts give Kennedy low marks as measles outbreak tests him|url=https://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/5191976-measles-outbreak-kennedy-response/ |access-date=March 13, 2025 |work=The Hill}}</ref>

On February 28, HHS's top spokesperson, Thomas Corry, abruptly resigned, two weeks after being sworn in as the assistant secretary of public affairs. He reportedly clashed with Kennedy over his management of the department during the measles outbreak.<ref name="guard-3mar2025">{{cite news |last1=Betts |first1=Anna |title=US health official quits after reported clashes with RFK Jr over measles |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/03/hhs-resignation-rfk-jr-measles |access-date=March 3, 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=March 3, 2025}}</ref><ref name="pol-3mar2025">{{cite news |last1=Cancryn |first1=Adam |title=Top HHS spokesperson quits after clashing with RFK Jr. |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/03/03/top-hhs-spokesperson-quits-00207000 |access-date=March 3, 2025 |work=[[Politico]] |date=March 3, 2025}}</ref>

On March 2, Kennedy was criticized for writing an op-ed for [[Fox News]] that called vaccines a "personal choice" and recommended vitamins and good nutrition to combat measles.<ref name="guard-4mar2025">{{cite news |last1=Glenza |first1=Jessica |title=RFK Jr sparks alarm after backing vitamins to treat measles amid outbreak |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/04/rfk-jr-vitamins-measles-outbreak |access-date=March 4, 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=March 4, 2025}}</ref> But he also wrote in the piece: "Vaccines not only protect individual children from measles, but also contribute to community immunity, protecting those who are unable to be vaccinated due to medical reasons."<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://www.bmj.com/content/388/bmj.r454|title=Robert F Kennedy Jr offers qualified support for MMR vaccine as measles spreads across US and Canada|first=Owen|last=Dyer|date=March 5, 2025|journal=BMJ|volume=388|pages=r454|via=www.bmj.com|doi=10.1136/bmj.r454|pmid=40044223 |url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.hhs.gov/about/news/2025/03/03/measles-outbreak-call-to-action-for-all-of-us.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250306214643/https://www.hhs.gov/about/news/2025/03/03/measles-outbreak-call-to-action-for-all-of-us.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=March 6, 2025|title=Measles Outbreak is Call to Action for All of Us|date=March 3, 2025|website=HHS.gov}}</ref>

After Kennedy took to the media to falsely claim that vitamin A is both a [[prophylactic]] and treatment for measles, doctors in Texas began to see children infected with measles also having symptoms of [[vitamin A toxicity]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rosenbluth |first=Teddy |date=March 25, 2025 |title=For Some Measles Patients, Vitamin A Remedy Supported by RFK Jr. Leaves Them More Ill |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/03/25/health/measles-kennedy-vitamin-a.html |access-date=March 26, 2025 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Goodman|first1=Brenda|last2=Mukherjee |first2=Neha|date=March 6, 2025 |title=Kennedy touts unconventional therapies for measles linked to disciplined Texas doctor |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/03/06/health/kennedy-measles-texas-doctor-treatment/index.html |access-date=March 26, 2025 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref>

On March 28, Kennedy told [[Peter Marks (physician)|Peter Marks]], the head of the FDA's vaccine program, that he should resign or be fired. Marks wrote a resignation letter that lamented Kennedy's attempts to erode trust in vaccines: "It has become clear that truth and transparency are not desired by the secretary, but rather he wishes subservient confirmation of his misinformation and lies."<ref name="marks-nytimes">{{cite news |last1=Jewett |first1=Christina |last2=Stolberg |first2=Sheryl Gay |last3=Weiland |first3=Noah |title=Top F.D.A. Vaccine Official Resigns, Citing Kennedy's 'Misinformation and Lies' |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/03/28/health/fda-vaccines-rfk-jr-peter-marks.html |access-date=March 29, 2025 |work=The New York Times |date=March 29, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> Days earlier, the CDC head of communications said after his own resignation, "Kennedy and his team are working to bend science to fit their own narratives, rather than allowing facts to guide policy."<ref name="Griffis">{{cite news |last1=Griffis |first1=Kevin |title=Why I left my job leading public health messaging for the CDC |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2025/03/25/rfk-hhs-cdc-public-health-misinformation-spread/` |access-date=March 29, 2025 |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=March 25, 2025}}</ref>

In April, Kennedy praised Texas doctor Ben Edwards as one of two "extraordinary healers" using unverified treatments for measles; the week before, Edwards was aware that he was infected with measles when he met children and parents at his clinic without wearing a mask.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Michelle |title=Video shows doctor with measles treating kids. RFK Jr later praised him as an 'extraordinary' healer |url=https://apnews.com/article/texas-measles-outbreak-rfk-jr-ben-edwards-2dd7c79d47c64ad2e6d4a4ac3c87ec1f |access-date=June 10, 2025 |work=Associated Press |date=April 19, 2025}}</ref>

==== MAHA report ====
{{further|MAHA Commission|MAHA report}}

On May 22, 2025, the MAHA Commission released a report about childhood chronic disease.<ref name=":12" /> It was described as "wide-ranging" and claimed that a range of factors, including diet, vaccinations, medical prescriptions, physical stress, food additives, and pesticides, are "potential drivers behind the rise in childhood chronic disease that present the clearest opportunities for progress".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Tin |first=Alexander |date=May 22, 2025 |title=RFK Jr. releases "MAHA" report on childhood chronic disease. Here's what to know. |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/rfk-jr-maha-report-childhood-chronic-disease/ |access-date=May 29, 2025 |work=CBS}}</ref>

On May 29, [[NOTUS (website)|NOTUS]] first reported that some of the studies the MAHA Commission's report cited did not exist; the authors of several other studies the report cited said their work was mischaracterized.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Gedeon |first=Joseph |date=May 29, 2025 |title=RFK Jr's 'Maha' report found to contain citations to nonexistent studies |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/29/rfk-jr-maha-health-report-studies |access-date=May 29, 2025 |work=The Guardian}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Kennard |first1=Emily |last2=Manto |first2=Margaret |date=May 29, 2025 |title=The MAHA Report Cites Studies That Don't Exist |url=https://www.notus.org/health-science/make-america-healthy-again-report-citation-errors |access-date=May 29, 2025 |work=NOTUS}}</ref><ref name=":12">{{Cite news |last=Dorn |first=Sara |date=May 29, 2025 |title=White House Blames Nonexistent Medical Citations In RFK Jr.'s 'MAHA' Report On 'Formatting Issues' |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2025/05/29/white-house-blames-nonexistent-medical-citations-in-rfk-jrs-maha-report-on-formatting-issues/ |access-date=May 29, 2025 |work=Forbes}}</ref> Medical researcher [[Ivan Oransky]] said the errors were characteristic of [[generative AI]] usage: "They come up with references that share a lot of words and authors and even journals, journal names, but they're not real."<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Rodriguez |first1=Adrianna |last2=Alltucker |first2=Ken |date=May 29, 2025 |title=White House blames 'formatting' for errors in RFK Jr.'s MAHA report. Authors push back. |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2025/05/29/rfk-jr-maha-report-citation-errors/83925319007/ |access-date=May 29, 2025 |work=USA Today}}</ref> Some of the report's citation [[URLs]] contained the string "oaicite", indicating a tool produced by [[OpenAI]] was used.<ref name=":13">{{Cite news |last1=Weber |first1=Lauren |last2=Gilbert |first2=Caitlin |date=May 29, 2025 |title=White House MAHA Report may have garbled science by using AI, experts say |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2025/05/29/maha-rfk-jr-ai-garble/ |access-date=May 29, 2025 |newspaper=The Washington Post}}</ref>

[[White House press secretary]] [[Karoline Leavitt]] said these errors were "formatting issues that are being addressed and the report will be updated. But it does not negate the substance of the report, which, as you know, is one of the most transformative health reports that has ever been released by the federal government."<ref>{{Cite news |last=Seitz |first=Amanda |date=May 29, 2025 |title=WATCH: MAHA report with non-existent sources had 'formatting issues,' White House says |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-maha-report-with-non-existent-sources-had-formatting-issues-white-house-says |access-date=May 29, 2025 |work=PBS |agency=Associated Press}}</ref><ref name=":12" /> Asked whether the MAHA Commission's report used generative AI, Leavitt said, "I can't speak to that."<ref name=":12" /> The report was repeatedly updated that day.<ref name=":13" /> NOTUS released a follow-up addressing the updates and reported that while several errors from the original report had been edited or removed, new errors were found, including updated citations that misinterpret scientific studies.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.msnbc.com/rachel-maddow-show/maddowblog/rfk-jrs-team-adds-new-errors-maha-report-making-fiasco-worse-rcna210319|title=RFK Jr's team adds new errors to its 'MAHA' report, making the fiasco worse|date=June 2, 2025|accessdate=June 9, 2025|first=Steve|last=Benen|work=MSNBC.com}}</ref>

==== CDC leadership departures ====
[[File:RFK Jr. Takes a Chainsaw to the CDC to Implement His MAHA Agenda.jpg|thumb|A [[political cartoon]] depicting Kennedy using a [[chainsaw]] labeled "[[Make America Healthy Again|MAHA]]" to saw the sign of the [[National Center for Immunization and Respiratory Diseases]] (NCIRD) headquarters, in reference to the several oustings of NCIRD staff under his tenure]]

When CDC director [[Susan Monarez]] was ousted in August 2025, several CDC leaders quit over Kennedy's anti-science policies. In a resignation note, [[National Center for Immunization and Respiratory Diseases]] director [[Demetre Daskalakis]] wrote that Kennedy and his appointees "threaten the lives of the youngest Americans and pregnant people". In a statement, lawyers for Monarez said that Kennedy had been "weaponizing public health for political gain" and "putting millions of American lives at risk". Also among those leaving were [[Debra Houry]], the chief medical officer, and the heads of several other directorates. Houry's farewell announcement referenced vaccine misinformation, the 2025 [[Robert F. Kennedy Jr.#2025 Southwest United States measles outbreak|measles outbreak]], and the 2025 [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention#2025 headquarters shooting|shooting attack on the CDC]].<ref>{{Cite news |first1=Lena H. |last1=Sun |first2=Dan |last2=Diamond |first3=Lauren |last3=Webber |date=August 27, 2025 |title=White House fires CDC director who says RFK Jr. is 'weaponizing public health' |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2025/08/27/susan-monarez-cdc-director-ousted/ |access-date=August 28, 2025 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |first1=Sheryl |last1=Gay Stolberg |first2=Apoorva |last2=Mandavilli |first3=Christina |last3=Jewett  |date=August 27, 2025 |title=C.D.C. Director Is Ousted After Clashes With Kennedy Over Vaccines |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/08/27/health/cdc-monarez-kennedy-vaccines.html |access-date=August 27, 2025 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en}}</ref> Days after the departure, nine former CDC leaders from both Democratic and Republican administrations wrote in ''The New York Times'' that the shakeup at the agency was "unlike anything our country has ever experienced".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Romo |first=Vanessa |date=September 2, 2025 |title=Former CDC leaders warn public health system is 'raging fire' under health secretary |url=https://www.npr.org/2025/09/02/nx-s1-5524975/former-cdc-leaders-warn-public-health-system-is-raging-fire-under-health-secretary |access-date=September 7, 2025 |work=[[NPR]] |language=en}}</ref>

==== Autism Data Science Initiative ====
On September 22, 2025, Kennedy approved a $50 million grant to the [[National Institute of Health]] (NIH) for 13 projects to "help transform autism research" through the proposed Autism Data Science Initiative.<ref name=sept222025 /><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Pearson |first=Hellen |date=September 26, 2025 |title=US autism research gets $50 million funding boost — amid row over Tylenol |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-025-03126-0 |journal=[[Nature (journal)|Nature]] |doi=10.1038/d41586-025-03126-0|pmid=41006924 |url-access=subscription }}</ref>

==== CDC revises stance on autism and vaccines ====
{{see also|CDC#CDC revises stance on autism and vaccines}}
[[File:P20250418MR-0680.jpg|thumb|left|Kennedy swearing in [[Mehmet Oz]] as administrator of the [[Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services]] on April 18, 2025]]
On November 19, 2025, the CDC website on "Autism and Vaccines" was radically changed from its September 2025 version.<ref name="CDC_RFK_Autism_Vaccines_09/23/2025">{{cite web |title=Autism and Vaccines |website=[[CDC]] |date=September 23, 2025 |url=https://www.cdc.gov/vaccine-safety/about/autism.html}}</ref><ref name="CDC_RFK_Autism_Vaccines_11/19/2025">{{cite web |title=Autism and Vaccines |website=[[CDC]] |date=November 19, 2025 |url=https://www.cdc.gov/vaccine-safety/about/autism.html |access-date=November 20, 2025}}</ref> In an interview, Kennedy reportedly said that "he personally instructed the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention to abandon its longstanding position that vaccines do not cause autism—a move that underscores his determination to challenge scientific consensus and bend the health department to his will."<ref name="Stolberg_11/21/2025">{{cite news |last=Stolberg |first=Sheryl Gay |title=RFK Jr. Says He Instructed CDC to Change Vaccines and Autism Language on Website |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=November 21, 2025 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/21/us/politics/rfk-jr-cdc-vaccines-autism-website.html |access-date=November 22, 2025}}</ref>

[[FactCheck.org]] wrote that the change was not evidence-based.<ref name="McDonald_Yandell_11/20/2025">{{cite web |last1=McDonald |first1=Jessica |last2=Yandell |first2=Kate |title=Revised CDC Website About Autism and Vaccines Is Not Evidence-Based |website=[[FactCheck.org]] |date=November 20, 2025 |url=https://www.factcheck.org/2025/11/revised-cdc-website-about-autism-and-vaccines-is-not-evidence-based/ |access-date=November 22, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251121020418/https://www.factcheck.org/2025/11/revised-cdc-website-about-autism-and-vaccines-is-not-evidence-based/ |archive-date=November 21, 2025}}</ref> The revised site makes several false statements, contradicting longstanding scientific evidence that supports ''the absence'' of a causal link between childhood vaccines and autism.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Cirruzzo |first1=Chelsea |last2=Branswell |first2=Helen |last3=Payne |first3=Daniel |date=November 21, 2025 |title=Under RFK Jr., CDC changes website to reflect vaccine skepticism |url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/2025/11/20/nation/cdc-vaccine-site-adds-link-autism/ |access-date=November 25, 2025 |website=The Boston Globe |language=en-US}}</ref> According to CDC staff, required scientific vetting for all public-facing agency webpages was entirely bypassed in this instance.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Butler |first=Kiera |date=November 21, 2025 |title="Embarrassing" and "horrifying": CDC workers describe the new vaccines and autism page |url=https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2025/11/embarrassing-and-horrifying-cdc-workers-describe-the-new-vaccines-and-autism-page/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251125112320/https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2025/11/embarrassing-and-horrifying-cdc-workers-describe-the-new-vaccines-and-autism-page/ |archive-date=November 25, 2025 |access-date=November 25, 2025 |work=Mother Jones |language=en-US}}</ref> The ''Wall Street Journal'' wrote, "no association has been proven between vaccines and autism".<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=White |first1=Liz Essley |last2=Siddiqui |first2=Sabrina |date=November 20, 2025 |title=CDC changes webpage to say vaccines may cause autism, revising prior language |journal=The Wall Street Journal |via=Proquest U.S. Newsstream}}</ref> Numerous medical associations and professionals immediately issued statements protesting the changes, including experts from the [[Center for Infectious Disease Research and Policy]] (CIDRAP); [[Children's Hospital of Philadelphia]]; [[Stanford University School of Medicine]]; Texas Children's Hospital Center for Vaccine Development;<ref>{{Cite web |title=Vaccine Development {{!}} Texas Children's |url=https://www.texaschildrens.org/texas-childrens-global-health/programs/vaccine-development |access-date=November 25, 2025 |website=www.texaschildrens.org |language=en}}</ref> the [[American Academy of Pediatrics]] (AAP); [[Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health]]; and The Autism Science Foundation.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Autism Science Foundation |url=https://autismsciencefoundation.org/ |access-date=November 25, 2025 |website=Autism Science Foundation - Supporting and sharing autism research to improve the real lives of real people |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Szabo |first1=Liz |last2=Bergeson |first2=Laine |date=November 20, 2025 |title=After unprecedented autism-vaccine messaging change, scientists, advocates say CDC no longer trustworthy |url=https://www.cidrap.umn.edu/childhood-vaccines/after-unprecedented-autism-vaccine-messaging-change-scientists-advocates-say-cdc |access-date=November 25, 2025 |website=Center for Infectious Disease Research and Policy (CIDRAP) |language=en}}</ref>

====Hepatitis B vaccines====
On December 5, 2025, Kennedy's [[Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices]] (ACIP) vaccine panel voted to end the CDC's universal recommendation that newborn babies receive [[Hepatitis B vaccine]] doses and instead "recommend individual-based decision-making for parents deciding whether to give the hepatitis B vaccine, including the birth dose, to infants born to women who test negative for the virus. For those infants not receiving the birth dose, ACIP suggested in its recommendation that the initial dose be administered no earlier than two months of age".<ref name=hepbvaccine>{{cite news|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/12/05/rfk-jr-vaccine-panel-newborns-hep-b-shots-00678277|title=RFK Jr.'s vaccine panel says not all newborns need Hep B shots|first1=Sophie|last1=Gardner|first2=Lauren|last2=Gardner|publisher=Politico|date=December 5, 2025|accessdate=December 5, 2025}}</ref><ref name=parentdecisionmaking>{{cite web | title=ACIP Recommends Individual-Based Decision-Making for Hepatitis B Vaccine for Infants Born to Women Who Test Negative for the Virus | website=U.S. [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention]] (CDC) | date=December 5, 2025 | url=https://www.cdc.gov/media/releases/2025/2025-acip-recommends-individual-based-decision-making-for-hepatitis-b-vaccine-for-infants-born-to-women.html | access-date=December 5, 2025}} {{PD-notice}}</ref>

The Kennedy-led ACIP also voted to "recommend that when evaluating the need for a subsequent hepatitis B vaccine dose in children, parents should consult with health care providers to decide whether to test antibody levels to hepatitis surface antigen to evaluate adequacy of protection through serology results."<ref name=hepbvaccine /><ref name=parentdecisionmaking /> This vote came "despite no evidence suggesting fewer doses than the standard series of three offer lifelong protection".<ref name=hepbvaccine />

Joseph Hibbeln, an ACIP member who voted against the change, said: "This has a great potential to cause harm, and I simply hope that the committee will accept its responsibility when this harm is caused."<ref name=hepbvaccine />

[[United States Senate Committee on Health, Education, Labor and Pensions|Senate Health, Education, Labor and Pensions Committee]] chairman [[Bill Cassidy]] wrote: "Ending the recommendation for newborns makes it more likely the number of cases will begin to increase again. This makes America sicker."<ref name=hepbvaccine />

==Anti-vaccine advocacy and conspiracy theories on public health==
Kennedy is a prominent voice in the anti-vaccine movement, spreading anti-vaccine [[misinformation]], [[disinformation]], and [[propaganda]].<ref name="KW" /><ref name="mnookin-2017">{{Cite magazine |last=Mnookin |first=Seth |author-link=Seth Mnookin |date=January 11, 2017|title=How Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., Distorted Vaccine Science |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/how-robert-f-kennedy-jr-distorted-vaccine-science1/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170112025044/https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/how-robert-f-kennedy-jr-distorted-vaccine-science1/ |archive-date=January 12, 2017 |magazine=[[Scientific American]] |quote=For more than a decade, Kennedy has promoted anti-vaccine propaganda completely unconnected to reality.}}</ref><ref name="NBC_2021-03-11">{{cite news |last1=Zadrozny |first1=Brandy |author-link1=Brandy Zadrozny |last2=Adams |first2=Char |date=March 11, 2021 |title=Covid's devastation of Black community used as 'marketing' in new anti-vaccine film |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/nbcblk/covid-s-devastation-black-community-used-marketing-new-anti-vaxxer-n1260724 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210318224620/https://www.nbcnews.com/news/nbcblk/covid-s-devastation-black-community-used-marketing-new-anti-vaxxer-n1260724 |archive-date=March 18, 2021 |access-date=March 15, 2021 |work=[[NBC News]] |quote=The video{{snd}}the newest in a series of anti-vaccine propaganda films produced or promoted by Kennedy{{snd}}was distributed through Kennedy's organization, Children's Health Defense,}}</ref><ref name="Smith_12/15/2021" /><ref name="Time20230614">{{Cite magazine |first=Vera |last=Bergengruen |date=June 14, 2023 |title=Inside the Very Online Campaign of RFK Jr. |url=https://time.com/6287001/rfk-jr-interview-2024-campaign/ |access-date=June 14, 2023 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |archive-date=June 14, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230614115633/https://time.com/6287001/rfk-jr-interview-2024-campaign/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> The infectious disease specialist [[Michael Osterholm]] has said that Kennedy's "anti-vaccine disinformation" is effective "because it's portrayed to the public with graphs and figures and what appears to be scientific data. He has perfected the art of illusion of fact." Osterholm added:<ref name="Time20230614" /><blockquote>This is about people's lives. And the consequences of promoting this kind of disinformation, as credible as it may seem, is simply dangerous.</blockquote>Kennedy has said that he is not against vaccines but wants them to be more thoroughly tested and investigated.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.science.org/content/article/exclusive-qa-robert-f-kennedy-jr-trumps-proposed-vaccine-commission |title=Exclusive Q&A: Robert F. Kennedy Jr. on Trump's proposed vaccine commission|last=Wadman|first=Meredith|date=January 10, 2017 |website=Science |access-date=April 14, 2020 |archive-date=April 8, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200408012519/https://www.sciencemag.org/news/2017/01/exclusive-qa-robert-f-kennedy-jr-trumps-proposed-vaccine-commission |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.dispatch.com/opinion/20190627/letter-stronger-testing-required-to-make-vaccines-safe|title=Stronger testing required to make vaccines safe |last=Kennedy |first=Robert F. Jr.|date=June 27, 2019 |website=The Columbus Dispatch |access-date=April 14, 2020 |archive-date=April 8, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200408012523/https://www.dispatch.com/opinion/20190627/letter-stronger-testing-required-to-make-vaccines-safe |url-status=live }}</ref> In ''[[Thiomersal|Thimerosal]]: Let the Science Speak'' (2015), he writes that he does not see himself as anti-vaccine: "People who advocate for safer vaccines should not be marginalized or denounced as anti-vaccine. I am pro-vaccine. I had all six of my children vaccinated. I believe that vaccines have saved the lives of hundreds of millions of humans over the past century and that broad vaccine coverage is critical to public health. But I want our vaccines to be as safe as possible."<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr., ''Thimerosal: Let the Science Speak'' (2015), p. 28.</ref> But in 2023, Kennedy said, "There's no vaccine that is safe and effective."<ref>{{Cite news |last=McDonald |first=Jessica |date=November 8, 2023 |title=RFK Jr. Incorrectly Denies Past Remarks on Vaccine Safety and Effectiveness |url=https://www.factcheck.org/2023/11/scicheck-rfk-jr-incorrectly-denies-past-remarks-on-vaccine-safety-and-effectiveness/ |access-date=November 22, 2024 |work=FactCheck.org}}</ref><ref>{{Citation| last1 = Smith| first1 = Michelle R. | last2 = Swenson| first2 = Ali| title = RFK Jr. says he's not anti-vaccine. His record shows the opposite. It's one of many inconsistencies| publisher = [[Associated Press]]| date = July 31, 2023| url = https://apnews.com/article/rfk-kennedy-election-2024-president-campaign-621c9e9641381a1b2677df9de5a09731| access-date = November 16, 2024}}</ref>

In January 2024, Kennedy published a podcast about [[Lyme disease]] in which he said it is "highly likely to have been a military weapon" developed at the [[Plum Island Animal Disease Center]]. Multiple experts and authoritative sources have debunked the charge and called it "absurd".<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel Patrick |last=Galgano |url=https://factcheck.afp.com/doc.afp.com.36X42X4 |title=RFK Jr Senate hearing resurrects Lyme disease bioweapon conspiracy theory |work=AFP Fact Check |date=February 5, 2025 |access-date=February 11, 2025}}  <!-- see also: https://subscriber.politicopro.com/article/eenews/2025/01/30/rfk-jr-offers-conflicting-views-on-lyme-disease-00201560 --></ref>

=== Vaccines and autism claims ===
{{see also|Vaccines and autism|Thiomersal and vaccines}}
From 2015 to 2023, Kennedy chaired [[Children's Health Defense]], an anti-vaccine advocacy group.<ref name="KW" /><ref name="Smith_12/15/2021">{{Cite news |last=Smith |first=Michelle R. |date=December 15, 2021 |title=How a Kennedy built an anti-vaccine juggernaut amid COVID-19 |url=https://apnews.com/article/how-rfk-jr-built-anti-vaccine-juggernaut-amid-covid-4997be1bcf591fe8b7f1f90d16c9321e |access-date=April 1, 2023 |publisher=Associated Press |quote=Dr. Richard Allen Williams, a cardiologist, professor of medicine at UCLA and founder of the Minority Health Institute, said Kennedy is leading 'a propaganda movement' |archive-date= December 18, 2021 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20211218203449/https://apnews.com/article/how-rfk-jr-built-anti-vaccine-juggernaut-amid-covid-4997be1bcf591fe8b7f1f90d16c9321e |url-status=live}}</ref> In its early years, the group focused on mercury in industry and medicine, especially the ethylmercury used in thimerosal in vaccines.<ref name= "notav1">{{Cite news |last= Rabin |first= Roni Caryn |date= May 8, 2019 |title= Brother and Sister of Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Accuse Him of Spreading Misinformation on Vaccines |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/08/health/vaccines-kennedy-measles.html |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190621221444/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/08/health/vaccines-kennedy-measles.html |archive-date=June 21, 2019 |access-date=June 21, 2019 |work=The New York Times}}</ref><ref name= "notav2">{{Cite news |last=Golgowski |first=Nina |date=May 8, 2019 |title=Robert Kennedy Jr.'s Vaccine Views Slammed As 'Tragically Wrong' By Family |url=https://www.huffingtonpost.ca/entry/siblings-oped-calls-robert-f-kennedy-jrs-vaccine-views-tragically-wrong_n_5cd2ca19e4b07ce6ef78850e |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190621221641/https://www.huffingtonpost.ca/entry/siblings-oped-calls-robert-f-kennedy-jrs-vaccine-views-tragically-wrong_n_5cd2ca19e4b07ce6ef78850e |archive-date=June 21, 2019 |access-date=June 21, 2019 |work=The Huffington Post}}</ref>

The group alleges that exposure to certain chemicals and radiation has caused a range of conditions in many American children, including [[autism]], [[attention deficit hyperactivity disorder]] (ADHD), [[food allergies]], [[cancer]], and [[autoimmune disease]]s. Children's Health Defense has blamed and campaigned against vaccines, [[fluoridation of drinking water]], [[Paracetamol|acetaminophen]] (paracetamol), [[aluminum]], and [[Wireless device radiation and health|wireless communication]], among other things. The group has been identified as one of two major buyers of anti-vaccine [[Facebook]] advertising in 2018 and 2019.<ref name="KW" /><ref name="journ">{{Cite journal |last1=Jamison |first1=A.M. |last2=Broniatowski |first2=D.A. |last3=Dredze |first3=M. |date=November 13, 2019 |title=Vaccine-related advertising in the Facebook Ad Archive |journal=Vaccine |volume=38|issue=3|pages=512–520|doi=10.1016/j.vaccine.2019.10.066|pmid=31732327|pmc=6954281| issn = 0264-410X }}</ref><ref name=study>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2019/11/15/majority-anti-vaccine-ads-facebook-were-funded-by-two-groups |title=Majority of anti-vaccine ads on Facebook were funded by two groups |last=Sun |first=Lena H. |date=November 15, 2019 |newspaper=The Washington Post |access-date=November 16, 2019 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191117003403/https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2019/11/15/majority-anti-vaccine-ads-facebook-were-funded-by-two-groups |archive-date=November 17, 2019}}</ref> Members of his family have criticized Kennedy and his organization, saying he spreads "dangerous misinformation" and that his work has "heartbreaking" consequences.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rabin |first=Roni Caryn |date=May 9, 2019 |title=Family of Robert F Kennedy Jr criticise his 'dangerous' stance on vaccines |url=https://www.irishtimes.com/life-and-style/health-family/family-of-robert-f-kennedy-jr-criticise-his-dangerous-stance-on-vaccines-1.3886209 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509115316/https://www.irishtimes.com/life-and-style/health-family/family-of-robert-f-kennedy-jr-criticise-his-dangerous-stance-on-vaccines-1.3886209 |archive-date=May 9, 2019 |access-date=June 7, 2020 |newspaper=The Irish Times}}</ref>

Kennedy and Children's Health Defense have falsely claimed that vaccines cause autism.<ref name="FCRFKAutism">{{Cite web |last=Yandell |first=Kate |date=August 10, 2023 |title=What RFK Jr. Gets Wrong About Autism |url=https://www.factcheck.org/2023/08/scicheck-what-rfk-jr-gets-wrong-about-autism/ |access-date=November 15, 2024 |website=[[FactCheck.org]] |publisher=Annenberg Public Policy Center}}</ref><ref name=notav1/><ref name="nasem">{{cite web|title=Vaccines do not cause autism|url=https://www.nationalacademies.org/based-on-science/vaccines-do-not-cause-autism|publisher=National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine|access-date=April 29, 2023|archive-date=April 29, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230429223750/https://www.nationalacademies.org/based-on-science/vaccines-do-not-cause-autism|url-status=live}}</ref> Kennedy focused on [[Thiomersal and vaccines|the subset of vaccines that contained thimerosal]], a [[Mercury (element)|mercury]]-based anti-microbial that has been falsely claimed to cause autism.<ref name="shifting">{{cite journal |last2=Gerber |first2=Jeffrey S. |last3=Offit |first3=Paul A. |last1=Plotkin |first1=Stanley |title=Vaccines and Autism: A Tale of Shifting Hypotheses |journal=Clinical Infectious Diseases |date=February 15, 2009 |volume=48 |issue=4 |pages=456–461 |doi=10.1086/596476|pmid=19128068 |pmc=2908388}}</ref> [[Thimerosal]] has never been used in [[MMR vaccine|MMR]], [[Varicella vaccine|chickenpox]], [[Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine|pneumococcal conjugate]], or [[Inactivated polio vaccine|inactivated polio]] vaccines.<ref name="CDC-thimerosal" /> In 2001, thimerosal was removed from all other childhood (under six years old) vaccines except for a few versions of flu and hepatitis vaccines.<ref>{{Cite journal |author1=Asif Doja |author2=Wendy Roberts |title=Immunizations and Autism: A Review of the Literature |journal=Canadian Journal of Neurological Sciences |year=2006 |volume=33 |issue=4 |pages=341–346 |doi=10.1017/S031716710000528X |pmid=17168158 |s2cid=4670282 |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/16B999364BFFD9F0DA3B09F25C1DE28C/S031716710000528Xa.pdf/immunizations-and-autism-a-review-of-the-literature.pdf%7Cpage%3D343 |access-date=June 5, 2023 |archive-date=May 15, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230515101945/https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/16B999364BFFD9F0DA3B09F25C1DE28C/S031716710000528Xa.pdf/immunizations-and-autism-a-review-of-the-literature.pdf%7Cpage=343 |url-status=live|doi-access=free }}</ref> No childhood vaccine now contains more than traces (1 microgram or less) of thimerosal, except for flu, which is also available without thimerosal in the US.<ref name="FDA Thimerosal and Vaccines">{{Cite web |url=https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/safety-availability-biologics/thimerosal-and-vaccines|title=Thimerosal and Vaccines|publisher=[[Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research]], U.S. Food and Drug Administration |access-date=August 24, 2019 |archive-date=April 19, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190419070239/https://www.fda.gov/biologicsbloodvaccines/safetyavailability/vaccinesafety/ucm096228|url-status=dead}}</ref> For those six years and older, including pregnant women, all vaccines are now available in versions with only trace amounts of thimerosal.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/vaccines/thimerosal-vaccines-questions-and-answers |title=Thimerosal in Vaccines Questions and Answers |date=February 18, 2021 |publisher=[[Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research]], U.S. Food and Drug Administration |access-date=May 13, 2023 |archive-date=April 18, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230418195953/https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/vaccines/thimerosal-vaccines-questions-and-answers |url-status=dead}}</ref>

In 2015, Kennedy participated in a Speakers' Forum to promote the film ''[[Trace Amounts]]'', which promotes the discredited claim of a link between [[Vaccines and autism|autism]] and [[Thiomersal and vaccines|mercury]] in vaccinations. He called the increased diagnosis of cases of autism a "holocaust".<ref>{{cite news |last=White |first=Jeremy B. |date=April 7, 2015 |url=https://www.sacbee.com/news/politics-government/capitol-alert/article17814440.html |title=Robert Kennedy Jr. warns of vaccine-linked 'holocaust' |work=[[The Sacramento Bee]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150411002553/http://www.sacbee.com/news/politics-government/capitol-alert/article17814440.html |archive-date=April 11, 2015 |url-status=live}}</ref> He has been heavily criticized for such statements.<ref name="Kessler_4/25/2025">{{cite news | last=Kessler | first=Glenn | author-link=Glenn Kessler (journalist) | title=RFK Jr.'s absurd statistic on the spike in chronic diseases in the U.S. | newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] | date=April 25, 2025 | url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2025/04/25/rfk-jr-chronic-diseases-false/ | access-date=April 30, 2025 | quote=He's long been a purveyor of the fiction that vaccines cause autism, and one of his key points of evidence is that the percentage of people with autism has increased. But the percentage of people diagnosed with autism spectrum disorder has gone up mainly because of expanded definitions and better detection. There is no blood test for autism, so a diagnosis is based on observations of a person's behavior. Indeed, while autism diagnoses have increased, those of intellectual disability have decreased, indicating that previously, children may have been misdiagnosed with other conditions.}}</ref><ref name="Wadman_4/16/2025">{{cite web | last=Wadman | first=Meredith | title=Claiming autism 'epidemic,' RFK Jr. describes NIH initiative to find environmental causes | website=[[Science (journal)|Science]] | date=April 16, 2025 | url=https://www.science.org/content/article/claiming-autism-epidemic-rfk-jr-describes-nih-initiative-find-environmental-causes | access-date=April 30, 2025}}</ref><ref name="Geddie_4/11/2025">{{cite web | last=Geddie | first=Becky | title=Statement on Robert F. Kennedy Jr.'s Comments Regarding the Cause of Autism and Misleading Deadline | website=[[Autism Society of America]] | date=April 11, 2025 | url=https://autismsociety.org/statement-on-robert-f-kennedy-jr-s-comments-regarding-the-cause-of-autism-and-misleading-deadline/ | access-date=April 30, 2025}}</ref><ref name="Wendling_4/11/2025">{{cite web | last=Wendling | first=Mike | title=RFK Jr pledges to find the cause of autism 'by September' | website=[[BBC]] | date=April 11, 2025 | url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cj0z9nmzvdlo | access-date=April 30, 2025}}</ref>

In 2020, the [[Center for Countering Digital Hate]] said that Kennedy uses his status as an environmental activist to bolster the anti-vaccination movement, appearing in online conversations with the discredited British former doctor [[Andrew Wakefield]], the anti-vaccination activist [[Del Bigtree]], and the conspiracy theorist [[Rashid Buttar]].<ref name="Ind" /> Journalist [[Brian Deer]] wrote that Kennedy is friends with Wakefield and Bigtree.<ref name=":14" /> Deer describes Wakefield as "a former doctor whose medical license was revoked in his native Britain in 2010 amid charges of ethical violations."<ref name=":14">{{Cite news |last=Deer |first=Brian |author-link=Brian Deer |date=November 26, 2024 |title=Opinion: I'll never forget what Kennedy did during Samoa's measles outbreak |url=https://www.sltrib.com/opinion/commentary/2024/11/26/opinion-ill-never-forget-what/ |access-date=August 20, 2025 |work=The New York Times |via=The Salt Lake Tribune}}</ref> In 2019 (days before [[Robert F. Kennedy Jr.#Samoa measles outbreak|his letter to Samoa]]), Kennedy said, "In any just society, we would be building statues to Andy Wakefield."<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.pbs.org/video/shot-in-the-arm-18lrwu/ |title=Shot in the Arm |last=Kennedy |first=Scott Hamilton |author-link=Scott Hamilton Kennedy |type=Television production |language=en |publisher=[[PBS]] |year=2023 |time=43:11 |quote=[RFK Jr.] In any just society, we would be building statues to Andy Wakefield.}}</ref><ref name=":14" /> Kennedy has worked with [[Nation of Islam]] leader [[Louis Farrakhan]] to promote the conspiracy theory that vaccines are intentionally used to hurt black people. Kennedy met with Farrakhan in 2015 and spoke at a Nation of Islam event in 2021 on vaccines, calling Farrakhan a "truly great partner". In 2023, during his presidential campaign, Kennedy denounced Farrakhan, claiming he was unaware of his antisemitic views, but Kennedy had disparaged Farrakhan's "Jewish xenophobia" years earlier in his diary.<ref>{{cite web|last=Ross|first=Chuck |title=RFK Jr. Disavows Anti-Semite Louis Farrakhan, Whom He Once Called a 'Truly Great Partner'|website=[[The Washington Free Beacon]]|date=July 19, 2023 |url=https://freebeacon.com/democrats/rfk-jr-disavows-anti-semite-louis-farrakhan-whom-he-once-called-a-truly-great-partner/|access-date= December 14, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Nation of Islam|website=[[Anti-Defamation League]]|date=September 1, 2021|url=https://www.adl.org/resources/profile/nation-islam|access-date= December 14, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Smith|first=David|title=Robert Kennedy Jr's racist, antisemitic and xenophobic views go back decades, report says
|website=[[The Guardian]]|date=July 19, 2023|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/19/robert-kennedy-jr-racist-antisemitic-xenophobic-views|access-date=December 14, 2025}}</ref> Kennedy and the Children's Health Defense have cooperated with internet streamer and antisemitic conspiracy theorist James Corbett to promote antivaccine views.<ref>{{cite web|last=Kornbluh|first=Jacob|title=RFK Jr. tied to controversial streamer who claimed Hitler was 'utterly set up' by Jews
|website=[[The Forward]]|date=July 6, 2023|url=https://forward.com/fast-forward/553262/rfk-jr-streamer-hitler-vaccine-corbett-antisemitism-conspiracy-theories/|access-date=December 14, 2025}}</ref>

Kennedy is listed as executive producer of ''Vaxxed II: The People's Truth'', the 2019 sequel to Wakefield's and Bigtree's anti-vaccination propaganda film ''[[Vaxxed]]''.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Pilkington |first=Ed |date=October 31, 2019 |title=Release of ''Vaxxed'' sequel prompts fears dangerous propaganda will spread again |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/oct/31/vaxxed-2-movie-sequel-release-fears-propaganda |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191117171757/https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/oct/31/vaxxed-2-movie-sequel-release-fears-propaganda |archive-date=November 17, 2019 |access-date=November 18, 2019 |newspaper=The Guardian}}</ref>

In February 2021, Kennedy's [[Instagram]] account was deleted "for repeatedly sharing debunked claims" about [[COVID-19 vaccine]]s.<ref name="Singh-11 Feb 2021">{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/feb/10/instagram-removes-anti-vaxxer-robert-f-kennedy-jr|title=Instagram removes anti-vaxxer Robert F Kennedy Jr for false Covid-19 claims|first=Mannvi|last=Singh|newspaper=The Guardian|date=February 11, 2021|access-date=February 11, 2021|archive-date=February 11, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210211055917/https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/feb/10/instagram-removes-anti-vaxxer-robert-f-kennedy-jr|url-status=live}}</ref> In March 2021, the Center for Countering Digital Hate identified Kennedy as one of 12 people responsible for 65% of anti-vaccine content on Facebook and Twitter.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Srikanth |first1=Anagha |title=12 prominent people opposed to vaccines are responsible for two-thirds of anti-vaccine content online: report|url=https://thehill.com/changing-america/well-being/prevention-cures/544712-twelve-anti-vaxxers-are-responsible-for-two |newspaper=The Hill |access-date=June 11, 2021 |date=March 24, 2021}}</ref>

Kennedy has said that governments and the media are conspiring to deny that vaccines cause autism.<ref name="glob">{{Cite web |last=Scott |first=Katie |date=February 16, 2017 |title=Robert De Niro, Robert F. Kennedy Jr. offer $100K to anyone who can provide proof vaccines are safe |url=https://globalnews.ca/news/3253840/robert-de-niro-robert-f-kennedy-jr-offer-100g-to-anyone-who-can-provide-proof-vaccines-are-safe |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190303172615/https://globalnews.ca/news/3253840/robert-de-niro-robert-f-kennedy-jr-offer-100g-to-anyone-who-can-provide-proof-vaccines-are-safe |archive-date=March 3, 2019 |access-date=March 3, 2019 |website=Global TV}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Cadeski |first=Eric |date=July 11, 2019 |title=The Surrey Board of Trade shouldn't help anti-vaxxer Robert F. Kennedy Jr. build his brand |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/opinion/kennedy-surrey-1.5206701 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190914091610/https://www.cbc.ca/news/opinion/kennedy-surrey-1.5206701 |archive-date=September 14, 2019 |access-date=November 18, 2019 |publisher=CBC News}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Schmunk |first=Rhianna |date=June 17, 2019 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. will speak at Surrey event despite outcry, board of trade says |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/british-columbia/robert-f-kennedy-jr-surrey-speaking-event-1.5178877 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190914091604/https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/british-columbia/robert-f-kennedy-jr-surrey-speaking-event-1.5178877 |archive-date=September 14, 2019 |access-date=November 18, 2019 |publisher=CBC News}}</ref>

==== Writings and speeches promoting anti-vaccine theories ====
In 2005, Kennedy wrote an article, "[[Deadly Immunity]]", that appeared in ''[[Rolling Stone]]'' and ''[[Salon.com]]'' and alleged a government conspiracy to conceal a connection between thimerosal and childhood [[neurodevelopmental disorders]], including [[autism]].<ref name="auto1">{{cite news |author1=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. |title=Deadly Immunity|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/deadly-immunity-180037 |access-date=January 13, 2020 |magazine=Rolling Stone |date=February 8, 2011 |archive-date=January 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140904/https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/deadly-immunity-180037/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Moreno |first1=Joelle |title=Toxic Torts, Autism, and Bad Science: Why the Courts May Be Our Best Defense Against Scientific Relativism |journal=New England Law Review |date=2006 |volume=40 |pages=409 |url=https://ecollections.law.fiu.edu/faculty_publications/34/ |ssrn=884465 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.cbsnews.com/news/rolling-stone-retracts-autism-article-but-lots-of-junk-journalism-remains/ | title=Rolling Stone Retracts Autism Article, but Lots of Junk Journalism Remains | work=[[CBS News]] | date=January 22, 2011 | accessdate=November 22, 2015 | author-last1=Edwards|author-first1= Jim}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.salon.com/2005/06/22/iom_thimerosal/|title=Letters|work=Salon.com|date=June 22, 2005|accessdate=September 22, 2025}}</ref>
The article contained factual errors, leading ''Salon'' to issue five corrections.<ref name="auto2">{{cite web|first=Kerry |last=Lauerman |author-link=Kerry Lauerman| date=January 16, 2011 |url=http://www.salon.com/2011/01/16/dangerous_immunity |title=Correcting our Record|website=[[Salon.com]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171104122950/https://www.salon.com/2011/01/16/dangerous_immunity |archive-date=November 4, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date= |title=Salon.com 2005 Corrections |url=http://www.salon.com/letters/corrections/2005/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060213205621/http://www.salon.com/letters/corrections/2005/index.html |archive-date=February 13, 2006 |website=www.salon.com}}</ref> [[Joan Walsh]], ''Salon.com''{{'}}s editor-in-chief and sole ''Salon'' editor of the piece, said she had mistakenly relied on ''Rolling Stone''{{'}}s fact-checking, a process she later learned was "less than arduous". As soon as the piece was up, she said, "We were besieged by scientists and advocates showing how Kennedy had misunderstood, incorrectly cited, and perhaps even falsified data{{nbsp}}... It was the worst mistake of my career. I probably should have been fired."<ref name="Walsh">{{Cite magazine |last=Walsh |first=Joan |date=June 22, 2023 |title=Just Another RFK Jr. Lie. I Know, Because It's About Me. |magazine=[[The Nation]] |url=https://www.thenation.com/article/politics/rfk-jr-vaccine-disinformation/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230626091529/https://www.thenation.com/article/politics/rfk-jr-vaccine-disinformation/ |archive-date=June 26, 2023 |access-date=August 22, 2023 |issn=0027-8378}}</ref>

In 2011, ''Salon.com'' retracted the article in its entirety.<ref name="auto2" /> It said the retraction was motivated by accumulating evidence of alleged errors and scientific fraud underlying the vaccine-autism claim.<ref>{{cite web |last=Oransky |first=Ivan |date=January 16, 2011 |url=http://retractionwatch.com/2011/01/16/salon-retracts-2005-robert-f-kennedy-jr-piece-on-alleged-autism-vaccine-link |title=''Salon'' retracts 2005 Robert F. Kennedy Jr. piece on alleged autism-vaccine link" |publisher=[[Retraction Watch]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831053935/http://retractionwatch.com/2011/01/16/salon-retracts-2005-robert-f-kennedy-jr-piece-on-alleged-autism-vaccine-link |archive-date=August 31, 2017}}</ref> A corrected version of the article was published on ''Rolling Stone''{{'}}s website.<ref name="auto1" /> Kennedy said on ''[[The Joe Rogan Experience]]''—and was paraphrased in ''The New York Times'' as saying—that "''Salon'' caved to pressure from government regulators and the pharmaceutical industry." Walsh responded: "That's just another lie. We caved to pressure from the incontrovertible truth and our journalistic consciences."<ref name="Walsh" />

In 2013, Kennedy delivered the keynote address at the anti-vaccination<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://blogs.plos.org/thepanicvirus/2012/05/15/more-media-stupidity-chicago-sun-times-runs-propaganda-piece-for-jenny-mccarthys-anti-vaccine-conference |title=More media stupidity: ''Chicago Sun-Times'' runs propaganda piece for Jenny McCarthy's anti-vaccine conference |website=The Panic Virus |access-date=January 28, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190129064155/https://blogs.plos.org/thepanicvirus/2012/05/15/more-media-stupidity-chicago-sun-times-runs-propaganda-piece-for-jenny-mccarthys-anti-vaccine-conference |archive-date=January 29, 2019 }}</ref> AutismOne / [[Generation Rescue]] conference.<ref name="Slate_2013">{{Cite magazine |last1=Plait |first1=Phil |author-link1=Phil Plait |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr.: Anti-Vaxxer|url=http://www.slate.com/blogs/bad_astronomy/2013/06/05/robert_f_kennedy_jr_advocate_for_antiscience_and_antivaccination.html |magazine=Slate |access-date=February 13, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180208145313/http://www.slate.com/blogs/bad_astronomy/2013/06/05/robert_f_kennedy_jr_advocate_for_antiscience_and_antivaccination.html |archive-date=February 8, 2018 |date=June 5, 2013}}</ref><ref name="AutismOne_2013">{{cite web |title=Generation Rescue Conference 2013 |url=http://www.autismone.org/content/autismone-generation-rescue-conference-2013 |website=AutismOne |access-date=February 13, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130730131034/http://www.autismone.org/content/autismone-generation-rescue-conference-2013 |archive-date=July 30, 2013}}</ref>

In 2014, Kennedy's book ''Thimerosal: Let the Science Speak: The Evidence Supporting the Immediate Removal of Mercury—a Known Neurotoxin—from Vaccines'', was published. While [[methylmercury]] is a potent neurotoxin, thimerosal is not. According to the CDC, there is "no convincing evidence of harm caused by the low doses of thimerosal in vaccines".<ref name="CDC-thimerosal">{{cite web |title=Thimerosal and Vaccines |url=https://www.cdc.gov/vaccinesafety/concerns/thimerosal/index.html |publisher=Centers for Disease Control and Prevention |date=August 25, 2020 |access-date=April 28, 2023 |archive-date=August 17, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110817063316/http://www.cdc.gov/vaccinesafety/concerns/thimerosal/index.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="FDA Thimerosal and Vaccines" /> The book's preface is by [[Mark Hyman (doctor)|Mark Hyman]], a proponent of the [[Alternative medicine|alternative medical treatment]] called [[functional medicine]].<ref name="Cracked31">{{cite AV media |title=Functional Medicine (CS31) |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3EDW_upV3ZU |via=YouTube |access-date=April 18, 2019 |publisher=McGill Office for Science and Society |date=April 13, 2019 |time=6:18}}</ref> Kennedy has published articles on the inclusion of [[Thiomersal and vaccines|thimerosal in vaccines]].<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (May 11, 2010). [http://www.huffingtonpost.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr/central-figure-in-cdc-vac_b_494303.html "Central Figure in CDC Vaccine Cover-Up Absconds With $2M"]. ''HuffPost''. {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171017141128/https://www.huffingtonpost.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr/central-figure-in-cdc-vac_b_494303.html |date=October 17, 2017}}</ref><ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (February 8, 2017). [https://www.ecowatch.com/yale-vaccine-study-kennedy-2246059411.html "Yale University Study Shows Association Between Vaccines and Brain Disorders"]. ''EcoWatch''. {{Webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171013120638/https://www.ecowatch.com/yale-vaccine-study-kennedy-2246059411.html |date=October 13, 2017}}</ref><ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (January 18, 2017) [https://www.ecowatch.com/cdc-mercury-vaccines-kennedy-2199157054.html "CDC Knew its Vaccine Program Was Exposing Children to Dangerous Mercury Levels Since 1999"]. ''EcoWatch''. {{Webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171007220719/https://www.ecowatch.com/cdc-mercury-vaccines-kennedy-2199157054.html |date=October 7, 2017}}</ref><ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (July 10, 2005). [https://archive.boston.com/news/globe/editorial_opinion/oped/articles/2005/07/01/autism_mercury_and_politics/ "Autism, Mercury, and Politics"]. ''The Boston Globe''. [https://archive.boston.com/news/globe/editorial_opinion/oped/articles/2005/07/01/autism_mercury_and_politics/]</ref>

==== Meeting with Donald Trump ====
In January 2017, incoming [[White House Press Secretary|White House press secretary]] [[Sean Spicer]] confirmed that Kennedy and [[President-elect of the United States|President-elect]] [[Donald Trump]] met to discuss a position in the Trump administration. Kennedy said he had accepted an offer from Trump to chair the Vaccine Safety Task Force, but a spokeswoman for Trump's transition said no final decision had been made.<ref name="skeptic">{{Cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/trump-to-meet-with-proponent-of-debunked-tie-between-vaccines-and-autism/2017/01/10/4a5d03c0-d752-11e6-9f9f-5cdb4b7f8dd7_story.html |author1=Phillip, Abby |author1-link=Abby Phillip |author2=Sun, Lena H. |author3=Bernstein, Lenny |date=January 10, 2017 |title=Vaccine skeptic Robert Kennedy Jr. says Trump asked him to lead commission on 'vaccine safety' |newspaper=The Washington Post |access-date=January 11, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170111010144/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/trump-to-meet-with-proponent-of-debunked-tie-between-vaccines-and-autism/2017/01/10/4a5d03c0-d752-11e6-9f9f-5cdb4b7f8dd7_story.html |archive-date=January 11, 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref> In an August 2017 interview with ''[[Stat (website)|STAT News]]'' reporter [[Helen Branswell]], Kennedy said he had been meeting with federal public health regulators at the White House's request to discuss defects in vaccine safety science.<ref>[https://worldmercuryproject.org/news/interview-stat-news-august-14-2017 "An interview with Robert Kennedy Jr. on vaccines"]. Interviewed by [[Helen Branswell]]. August 21, 2017. World Mercury Project. {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171013172227/https://worldmercuryproject.org/news/interview-stat-news-august-14-2017 |date=October 13, 2017}}</ref>

==== Controversy with Robert De Niro ====
In February 2017, Kennedy and actor [[Robert De Niro]] gave a press conference at the [[National Press Club (United States)|National Press Club]] in Washington, in which they said the press was working for the vaccination industry and did not allow debates on vaccination science. They offered a $100,000 reward to any journalist or citizen who could point to a study showing that it is safe to inject mercury into babies and pregnant women at levels currently contained in [[flu vaccine]]s. Craig Foster, a psychology professor who studies [[pseudoscience]], deemed the challenge "not science", calling it a "carefully constructed 'contest' that allows its creators to generate the misleading outcome they presumably want to see". Foster added, "Proving that something is safe is importantly different than proving that something is harmful."<ref name="Craig Foster">{{cite journal |last1=Foster |first1=Craig |title=The $100,000 vaccine challenge: Another method of promoting anti-vaccination pseudoscience |journal=Vaccine |date=2017 |volume=35 |issue=32 |pages=3905–3906 |doi=10.1016/j.vaccine.2017.06.012 |pmid=28624304}}</ref>

==== Samoa measles outbreak ====
{{Main|2019 Samoa measles outbreak}}

In 2019, during a visit to [[Samoa]], coinciding with its 57th annual independence celebration, Kennedy appeared in an Instagram photo with Australian-Samoan anti-vaccine activist [[Taylor Winterstein]]. Kennedy's charity and Winterstein have perpetuated the allegation that the [[MMR vaccine]] played a role in the 2018 deaths of two Samoan infants, despite the revelation that the infants had mistakenly received a [[muscle relaxant]] along with the vaccine. Kennedy has drawn criticism for fueling [[vaccine hesitancy]] amid a social climate, that gave rise to the 2019 Samoa measles outbreak, which killed over 70 people, and the [[2019 Tonga measles outbreak]].<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Michelle |last2=Swenson |first2=Ali |date=October 18, 2023 |title=RFK Jr. spent years stoking fear and mistrust of vaccines. These people were hurt by his work |url=https://apnews.com/article/rfk-kennedy-election-vaccines-2ccde2df146f57b5e8c26e8494f0a16a |access-date=October 24, 2023 |publisher=Associated Press}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Lanuola |first1=Tusani Tupufia |title=John F. Kennedy's nephew joins Samoa's Independence celebration|url=https://www.samoaobserver.ws/category/samoa/42888 |access-date=November 30, 2019 |work=Samoa Observer |date=June 1, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191130201157/https://www.samoaobserver.ws/category/samoa/42888 |archive-date=November 30, 2019 |ref=SamoaObserver_20190601 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Guarino |first1=Ben |last2=Satija |first2=Neena |last3=Sun |first3=Lena H. |title=Deadly measles outbreak hits children in Samoa after anti-vaccine fears |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2019/11/26/deadly-measles-outbreak-hits-children-samoa-after-anti-vaccine-fears |access-date=November 30, 2019 |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=November 27, 2019 |ref=WaPo_Samoa_measles_20191127 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191130201455/https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2019/11/26/deadly-measles-outbreak-hits-children-samoa-after-anti-vaccine-fears |archive-date=November 30, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==== Tylenol and autism-related health policies ====
{{Wikisource|President Trump Makes an Announcement on Medical and Scientific Findings for America's Children}}
At a White House press briefing on September 22, 2025, President Trump, joined by Kennedy and other senior officials, said the FDA would revise drug labels to discourage the use of [[acetaminophen]] in [[Tylenol]] during pregnancy, citing a possible link to autism. Medical scientists disagreed with this recommendation. Steven J. Fleischman, president of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, wrote, "It is highly unsettling that our federal health agencies are willing to make an announcement that will affect the health and well-being of millions of people without the backing of reliable data."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.npr.org/sections/shots-health-news/2025/09/22/nx-s1-5550153/trump-rfk-autism-tylenol-leucovorin-pregnancy |title=Trump blames Tylenol for autism. Science doesn't back him up |first1=Jon |last1=Hamilton |first2=Yuki |last2=Noguchi |publisher=NPR News |date=September 22, 2025 |accessdate=September 22, 2025}}</ref><ref name=sept222025>{{cite press release |url=https://www.hhs.gov/press-room/hhs-trump-kennedy-autism-initiatives-leucovorin-tylenol-research-2025.html |title=President Trump, Secretary Kennedy Announce Bold Actions to Tackle Autism Epidemic |publisher=U.S. Department of Health and Human Services |date=September 22, 2025 |accessdate=September 22, 2025}}</ref><ref name=sept222025credibility>{{cite news|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/trump-administration/live-blog/trump-rfk-jr-autism-china-tiktok-shutdown-h1-b-kirk-bondi-live-updates-rcna232650|title=Live updates: Trump and RFK Jr. hold briefing on autism|publisher=NBC News|date=September 22, 2025|accessdate=September 22, 2025}}</ref> 

On October 29, Kennedy retracted his statements from the press conference, saying that Tylenol use in pregnancy is not linked to autism.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Heavey |first1=Susan |last2=Aboulenein |first2=Ahmed |last3=Choudhury |first3=Kamal |date=October 29, 2025 |title=RFK Jr. says data does not show Tylenol causes autism |url=https://www.staradvertiser.com/2025/10/29/breaking-news/rfk-jr-says-data-does-not-show-tylenol-causes-autism/ |access-date=October 30, 2025 |website=Honolulu Star-Advertiser |language=en}}</ref> That same day, Kennedy and Trump backed the [[FDA]] approval of the chemotherapy drug [[leucovorin]] to also help alleviate the symptoms of autism.<ref name=sept222025 /> Their justification for this approval was based on limited evidence.<ref name=sept222025credibility />

====Allegations about circumcision====
On October 9, 2025, Kennedy alleged a link between autism and [[circumcision]],<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2025/10/09/circumcision-autism-tylenol-kennedy-trump-rfk/86606151007/|title=Health Secretary Kennedy, Trump link circumcision to autism through Tylenol|first=Bart|last=Jansen|publisher=USA Today|date=October 9, 2025|accessdate=October 13, 2025}}</ref><ref name=rfjrcircumsicisionautism>{{cite news|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/rfk-jr-circumcision-linked-autism-experts/|title=RFK Jr. suggests circumcision is linked to autism. Here's what experts say.|first=Sara|last=Moniuszko|publisher=CBS News|date=October 10, 2025|accessdate=October 12, 2025}}</ref> citing a 2015 [[Denmark|Danish]] study.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/5549892-kennedy-tylenol-autism-circumcision-link/|title=Kennedy clarifies remarks about circumcision, autism, Tylenol|first=Joseph|last=Choi|date=October 10, 2025 |work=The Hill |accessdate=October 13, 2025}}</ref> Scientists have rejected Kennedy's assertion about a link between autism and circumcision.<ref name=rfjrcircumsicisionautism /><ref>{{cite news |first=Ariana Eunjung |last=Cha |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2025/10/10/circumcision-tylenol-autism-rfk/ |title=RFK Jr. linked circumcision and Tylenol to autism. Here's what scientists say |newspaper=The Washington Post|date=October 10, 2025|accessdate=October 13, 2025}}</ref>

=== COVID-19 ===
{{see also|COVID-19 misinformation}}
During the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], Kennedy promoted conspiracy theories including false claims that [[Anthony Fauci]] and the [[Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation]] were trying to profit off a vaccine,<ref>{{cite web |last1=Corsi |first1=Jerome |author1-link=Jerome Corsi |title=Says Dr. Anthony Fauci's name appears on '4 U.S. patents for a key glycoprotein' used to 'create the current COVID-19 epidemic' |url=https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/may/12/jerome-corsi/conspiracy-theorist-spreads-false-claim-about-fauc/ |website=PolitiFact |access-date=January 31, 2021 |archive-date=January 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128133726/https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/may/12/jerome-corsi/conspiracy-theorist-spreads-false-claim-about-fauc/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=April 27, 2020|title=Robert F. Kennedy, Jr says Dr. Fauci and Bill Gates stand to profit from COVID-19 vaccine|url=https://www.irishcentral.com/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-dr-fauci-covid19-vaccine|access-date=September 1, 2020|website=IrishCentral.com|archive-date=August 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200813122331/https://www.irishcentral.com/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-dr-fauci-covid19-vaccine|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=May 7, 2020|title=Robert Kennedy Jr. claims Bill Gates 'owns the WHO'|url=https://www.irishcentral.com/news/robert-kennedy-jr-bill-gates-owns-who|access-date=September 1, 2020|website=IrishCentral.com|archive-date=September 18, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200918145339/https://www.irishcentral.com/news/robert-kennedy-jr-bill-gates-owns-who|url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Bill Gates]] would cut off access to money for people who do not get vaccinated, allowing them to starve.<ref>{{Cite magazine|last=Porterfield|first=Carlie|title=Debunked Bill Gates Conspiracy Gets A Boost From RFK Jr., Marla Maples|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/carlieporterfield/2020/08/15/debunked-bill-gates-conspiracy-gets-a-boost-from-rfk-jr-marla-maples/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210214130753/https://www.forbes.com/sites/carlieporterfield/2020/08/15/debunked-bill-gates-conspiracy-gets-a-boost-from-rfk-jr-marla-maples/|archive-date=February 14, 2021|access-date=February 15, 2021|magazine=Forbes|quote=The digitalized economy? We get rid of cash and coins. We give you a chip. We put all your money in your chip. If you refuse a vaccine, we turn off the chip and you starve!}}</ref> In August 2020, Kennedy appeared in an interview with [[Alec Baldwin]] on [[Instagram]] and touted incorrect claims about vaccines and public health measures related to the pandemic. Health officials and scientists criticized Baldwin for letting Kennedy's claims go unchallenged.<ref>{{Cite web|date=August 13, 2020|title=Robert F. Kennedy, Jr makes false COVID claims in live talk with Alec Baldwin|url=https://www.irishcentral.com/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-alec-baldwin|access-date=September 1, 2020|website=IrishCentral.com|archive-date=August 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200817121932/https://www.irishcentral.com/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-alec-baldwin|url-status=live}}</ref>

In May 2021, Kennedy petitioned the FDA to rescind authorization for all current and future COVID vaccines. The vaccines had saved about 140,000 lives in the US. John Moore, a professor of immunology, called Kennedy's request "an appalling error of judgment".<ref name="NYT Petition">{{Cite news |last=Jewett |first=Christina |date=January 17, 2025 |title=RFK Jr. Sought to Stop Covid Vaccinations 6 Months After Rollout |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/01/17/health/rfk-jr-covid-vaccines.html |access-date=January 17, 2025 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

Kennedy has promoted misinformation about the [[COVID-19 vaccine]], falsely suggesting that it contributed to the death of [[Hank Aaron]] and others.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Montgomery|first=Blake|date=January 23, 2021|title=RFK Jr. Stoops to New Low by Falsely Tying Hank Aaron's Death to Vaccine|url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr-stoops-to-new-low-by-falsely-tying-hank-aarons-death-to-vaccine|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210216205211/https://www.thedailybeast.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr-stoops-to-new-low-by-falsely-tying-hank-aarons-death-to-vaccine|archive-date=February 16, 2021|access-date=February 15, 2021|website=The Daily Beast|quote=Hank Aaron's tragic death is part of a wave of suspicious deaths among elderly closely following administration of COVID vaccines. He received the Moderna vaccine on Jan. 5 to inspire other Black Americans to get the vaccine.}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|title=RFK, Jr.'s niece speaks out against his COVID vaccine misinformation|url=https://www.boston.com/news/coronavirus/2020/12/30/rdk-jr-niece-speaks-out-against-anti-covid-vaccine-stance/|access-date=January 23, 2021|newspaper=The Boston Globe|archive-date=January 4, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210104064030/https://www.boston.com/news/coronavirus/2020/12/30/rdk-jr-niece-speaks-out-against-anti-covid-vaccine-stance|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=KW/> In February 2021, his Instagram account was blocked for "repeatedly sharing debunked claims about the coronavirus or vaccines".<ref name="Singh-11 Feb 2021" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Iyengar |first1=Rishi |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. has been banned from Instagram|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/02/10/tech/robert-kennedy-jr-instagram-ban/index.html |publisher=CNN |access-date=February 11, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210211171417/https://www.cnn.com/2021/02/10/tech/robert-kennedy-jr-instagram-ban/index.html |archive-date=February 11, 2021 |date=February 10, 2021 |url-status=live}}</ref> The [[Center for Countering Digital Hate]] identified Kennedy as one of the main propagators of conspiracy theories about Bill Gates and [[5G]] phone technology. His conspiracy theory activities considerably increased his social media impact. In 2020 his Instagram account grew from 121,000 followers to 454,000.<ref name=Ind>{{Cite report|date=2020|title=The Anti-Vaxx Industry|publisher=[[Center for Countering Digital Hate]]|url=https://www.counterhate.com/_files/ugd/f4d9b9_6910f8ab94a241cfa088953dd5e60968.pdf}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Mostrous|first=Alexi|date=September 16, 2020|title=How a Kennedy became a 'superspreader' of hoaxes on COVID-19, vaccines, 5G and more|newspaper=[[The Globe and Mail]]|url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-robert-f-kennedy-jr-medical-misinformation/|url-status=live|quote=These posts include claims that 5G damages human DNA, causes cancer and is being installed in order to carry out mass surveillance. It's easy to see a motivation behind his sudden interest: 5G-related posts have garnered him more than 400,000 likes or other interactions.|archive-date=February 11, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210211030922/https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-robert-f-kennedy-jr-medical-misinformation/}}</ref>

Kennedy has expressed skepticism about the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic]], contending that it served to benefit billionaires. According to Kennedy, the pandemic resulted in a "$4.4 trillion shift in wealth from the American middle class to this new oligarchy that we created—500 new billionaires with the lockdowns, and the billionaires that we already had increased their wealth by 30%".<ref name="unherd1" />

In November 2021, Kennedy's book ''[[The Real Anthony Fauci: Bill Gates, Big Pharma, and the Global War on Democracy and Public Health]]'' was published. In it, Kennedy alleges that Fauci sabotaged treatments for AIDS, violated federal laws, and conspired with Gates and social media companies to suppress information about COVID cures, to leave vaccines as the only option to fight the pandemic.<ref name="Real Fauci">{{cite book |last1=Kennedy |first1=Robert F. Jr. |title=The Real Anthony Fauci: Bill Gates, Big Pharma, and the Global War on Democracy and Public Health|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ApUfEAAAQBAJ |date=November 16, 2021|publisher=Simon and Schuster |isbn=978-1-5107-6681-5|access-date=November 24, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211124070005/https://books.google.com/books/about/The_Real_Anthony_Fauci.html?id=ApUfEAAAQBAJ&source=kp_book_description |archive-date=November 24, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Canales|first=Katie|date=July 25, 2021|title=How JFK's nephew became one of Facebook's most prolific anti-vax misinformation spreaders|work=Business Insider|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr-jfk-nephew-anti-vax-misinformation-facebook-2021-7|access-date=July 26, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210726214328/https://www.businessinsider.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr-jfk-nephew-anti-vax-misinformation-facebook-2021-7|archive-date=July 26, 2021}}</ref> Kennedy calls Fauci a "powerful technocrat who helped orchestrate and execute 2020's historic coup d'état against Western democracy". He claims without proof that Fauci and Gates had schemed to prolong the pandemic and exaggerate its effects, promoting expensive vaccinations for the benefit of "a powerful vaccine cartel".<ref name="NZZ">{{Cite news|last=Müller|first=Katja|date=December 6, 2021|title=The conspiracy-theorist Kennedy|newspaper=[[Neue Zürcher Zeitung]]|url=https://www.nzz.ch/english/robert-kennedys-new-book-is-full-of-covid-19-conspiracy-theories-ld.1658762|access-date=December 29, 2021|archive-date=December 29, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211229050015/https://www.nzz.ch/amp/english/robert-kennedys-new-book-is-full-of-covid-19-conspiracy-theories-ld.1658762|url-status=live}}</ref>

The book repeats discredited myths about the pandemic, notably about the effectiveness of [[ivermectin]].<ref name="Smith_12/15/2021" /> The ''[[Neue Zürcher Zeitung]]'' wrote that "polemics alternate with chapters that pedantically seek to substantiate Kennedy's accusations with numerous quotations and studies".<ref name="NZZ" /> Kennedy released a video depicting Fauci with a Hitler mustache.<ref name="The Guardian">{{cite news |date=January 25, 2022 |title=Robert F Kennedy Jr apologizes for Anne Frank comparison in anti-vax speech |work=The Guardian |agency=Associated Press |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/25/robert-f-kennedy-jr-vaccine-anne-frank |access-date=January 25, 2022 |archive-date=January 25, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220125180739/https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/25/robert-f-kennedy-jr-vaccine-anne-frank |url-status=live}}</ref> In response, Fauci called Kennedy "a very disturbed individual" and said that, having met with Kennedy to discuss vaccines, he "[doesn't] know what's going on in [Kennedy's] head, but it's not good".<ref>{{cite news |date=December 22, 2021 |title=Fauci Calls RFK Jr. A 'Very Disturbed Individual' over Career Attacks |newspaper=Thewrap |url=https://www.thewrap.com/dr-fauci-rfk-jr-shame-kennedys-disturbed-individual/ |access-date=August 22, 2022 |archive-date=July 20, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220720145744/https://www.thewrap.com/dr-fauci-rfk-jr-shame-kennedys-disturbed-individual/|url-status=live |last1=Lash |first1=Jolie }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Robertson |first=Nick |date=July 2, 2024 |url=https://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/4751459-fauci-criticizes-rfk-jr/ |title=Fauci on RFK Jr.: 'I don't know what's going on in his head, but it's not good' |work=The Hill |access-date=February 23, 2026 }}</ref>

Kennedy wrote the foreword to ''Plague of Corruption'', a 2020 book by the former research scientist and anti-vaccine conspiracy theorist [[Judy Mikovits]].<ref name="KW" />

In August 2020, Kennedy appeared as a speaker at a partially violent demonstration in Berlin where [[populist]] groups called for an end to restrictions caused by COVID.<ref>[[Rundfunk Berlin-Brandenburg]]: [https://www.rbb24.de/politik/thema/2020/coronavirus/beitraege_neu/2020/08/demonstrationen-samstag-corona-querdenken-gegendemos.html "Proteste in Berlin Fast 40.000 Menschen bei Corona-Demos - Sperren am Reichstag durchbrochen" ] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200901090757/https://www.rbb24.de/politik/thema/2020/coronavirus/beitraege_neu/2020/08/demonstrationen-samstag-corona-querdenken-gegendemos.html |date=September 1, 2020}} (''in German''). August 29, 2020, accessed September 1, 2020.</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Ross|first1=Alexander Reid|last2=Zhubi|first2=Patricia|url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/rfk-jrs-latest-anti-vaxx-stunt-backed-by-weird-pro-qanon-german-group-querdenken-711|title=Inside the Weird Pro-QAnon German Group Behind RFK Jr.'s Latest Anti-Vaxx Stunt|work=The Daily Beast|date=September 2, 2020|access-date=December 19, 2020|archive-date=November 26, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201126032953/https://www.thedailybeast.com/rfk-jrs-latest-anti-vaxx-stunt-backed-by-weird-pro-qanon-german-group-querdenken-711|url-status=live}}</ref> His YouTube account was removed in September 2021 for breaking policies on vaccine misinformation.<ref>{{cite news|last=Alba|first=Davey|author-link=Davey Alba|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/29/technology/youtube-anti-vaxx-ban.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20211228/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/29/technology/youtube-anti-vaxx-ban.html |archive-date=December 28, 2021 |url-access=limited|title=YouTube bans all anti-vaccine misinformation|work=The New York Times|date=September 29, 2021|access-date=September 30, 2021 |quote=YouTube said on Wednesday that it was banning the accounts of several prominent anti-vaccine activists from its platform, including those of Joseph Mercola and Robert F. Kennedy Jr., as part of an effort to remove all content that falsely claims that approved vaccines are dangerous.}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

In January 2022, during a speech at an anti-vaccination rally on the National Mall in Washington D.C., Kennedy said: "Even in Hitler's Germany, you could cross the Alps into Switzerland, you could hide in the attic like Anne Frank did. Today the mechanisms are being put in place that will make it so none of us can run, none of us can hide."<ref>{{cite news|last=Hanau|first=Shira|url=https://www.jta.org/2022/01/24/politics/none-of-us-can-hide-rfk-jr-uses-holocaust-analogy-in-speech-at-anti-vaccine-mandate-rally|title='None of us can hide': RFK Jr. uses Holocaust analogy in speech at anti-vaccine mandate rally|publisher=Jewish Telegraphic Agency|date=January 24, 2022|access-date=January 24, 2022|archive-date=January 24, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220124083646/https://www.jta.org/2022/01/24/politics/none-of-us-can-hide-rfk-jr-uses-holocaust-analogy-in-speech-at-anti-vaccine-mandate-rally|url-status=live}}</ref> The [[Auschwitz Memorial]] responded on Twitter: "Exploiting of the tragedy of people who suffered, were humiliated, tortured & murdered by the totalitarian regime of Nazi Germany—including children like Anne Frank—in a debate about vaccines & limitations during global pandemic is a sad symptom of moral & intellectual decay." Kennedy's wife, the actress [[Cheryl Hines]], also condemned his comments, tweeting that the reference to Frank was "reprehensible and insensitive".<ref>{{cite news|last1=Fortinsky|first1=Sarah|last2=Graef|first2=Aileen|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2022/01/23/politics/robert-f-kennedy-nazi-germany-offensive-anti-vaccine-speech/index.html|title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. invokes Nazi Germany in offensive anti-vaccine speech|publisher=CNN|date=January 24, 2022|access-date=January 25, 2022|archive-date=January 24, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220124230600/https://edition.cnn.com/2022/01/23/politics/robert-f-kennedy-nazi-germany-offensive-anti-vaccine-speech/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Two days later, Kennedy apologized.<ref name="The Guardian" /> In June 2023, Instagram reinstated his account.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Lima |first=Cristiano |date=June 4, 2023 |title=Instagram reinstates Robert Kennedy Jr. after launch of presidential bid |newspaper=The Washington Post |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2023/06/04/robert-kennedy-instagram/ |access-date=June 5, 2023 |issn=0190-8286 |archive-date=June 5, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230605034220/https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2023/06/04/robert-kennedy-instagram/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

In July 2023, at a private dinner, Kennedy was recorded saying, "There is an argument that [COVID-19] is ethnically targeted", adding, "COVID-19 is targeted to attack Caucasians and Black people. The people who are the most immune are Ashkenazi Jews and Chinese ... we don't know whether it's deliberately targeted or not." The [[American Jewish Committee]] and [[Anti-Defamation League]] condemned his remarks, with the latter saying that Kennedy's statement "feeds into sinophobic and antisemitic conspiracy theories".<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Koenig |first1=Lauren |last2=Shelton |first2=Shania |date=July 15, 2023 |title=Jewish groups denounce RFK Jr.'s false remarks that Covid-19 was 'ethnically targeted' to spare Jews and Chinese people |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/07/15/politics/rfk-jr-covid-jewish-groups/index.html |access-date=July 16, 2023 |publisher=CNN}}</ref><ref name="Weissman-15 Jul 2023" />

Kennedy responded that he "never, ever suggested that the COVID-19 virus was targeted to spare Jews" and that he does not "believe and never implied that the ethnic effect was deliberately engineered". He explained his remarks by citing a 2021 study that he said showed that "COVID-19 appears to disproportionately affect certain races" due to racial differences in the effectiveness of COVID-19's [[furin]] cleave docking site, thus serving "as a kind of proof of concept for ethnically targeted bioweapons".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Horovitz |first1=Michael |title=RFK Jr. suggests COVID-19 was 'ethnically targeted' to avoid Ashkenazi Jews |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/rfk-jr-suggests-covid-19-was-ethnically-targeted-to-avoid-ashkenazi-jews/ |access-date=July 16, 2023 |newspaper=[[Times of Israel]] |date=July 15, 2023}}</ref> Experts strongly criticized these further claims, saying the study said nothing about Chinese people or bioweapons and that Chinese people and Ashkenazi Jews contract COVID-19 at rates similar to other ethnic groups and nationalities. The virologist [[Angela Rasmussen]] said, "Jewish or Chinese protease consensus sequences are not a thing in biochemistry, but they are in racism and antisemitism."<ref name="Weissman-15 Jul 2023" />

=== Medical racism conspiracy theory ===
{{See also|Racial disparities in the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|Medical racism in the United States}}

Kennedy targets Black Americans with anti-vaccine propaganda and conspiracy theories, linking vaccination with instances of [[medical racism]] such as the [[Tuskegee Syphilis Study]].<ref name="NPR2021-06-08">{{Cite news |last=Stone |first=Will |date=June 8, 2021 |title=An Anti-Vaccine Film Targeted To Black Americans Spreads False Information |work=NPR News |url=https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2021/06/08/1004214189/anti-vaccine-film-targeted-to-black-americans-spreads-false-information |access-date=June 9, 2023}}</ref><ref name="NBC_2021-03-11" /> Echoing others in the anti-vaccination movement, Children's Health Defense claimed that the U.S. government seeks to harm ethnic minorities by prioritizing them for COVID-19 vaccines. In March 2021, Children's Health Defense released an anti-vaccine propaganda video, "[[Medical Racism: The New Apartheid]]", that promotes COVID-19 conspiracy theories and claims that COVID-19 vaccination efforts are medical experiments on Black people. Kennedy appears in the video, inviting viewers to disregard information dispensed by health authorities and doctors. Brandi Collin-Dexter, a Fellow at the [[Shorenstein Center on Media, Politics and Public Policy]], said, "the notorious figures and false narratives in the documentary were recognizable" and "the film's incompatible narratives sought to take advantage of the pain felt by Black communities."<ref name="NBC_2021-03-11" /><ref name="FC">{{Cite web |last1=Hale Spencer |first1=Saranac |last2=Fichera |first2=Angelo |date=March 11, 2021 |title=RFK Jr. Video Pushes Known Vaccine Misrepresentations |url=https://www.factcheck.org/2021/03/scicheck-rfk-jr-video-pushes-known-vaccine-misrepresentations/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210312005215/https://www.factcheck.org/2021/03/scicheck-rfk-jr-video-pushes-known-vaccine-misrepresentations/ |archive-date=March 12, 2021 |access-date=March 21, 2021 |website=Factcheck.org |publisher=Annenberg Public Policy Center}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Stone |first=Will |date=June 9, 2021 |title=An Anti-Vaccine Film Targeted to Black Americans Spreads False Information |url=https://khn.org/news/article/an-anti-vaccine-film-targeted-to-black-americans-spreads-false-information/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220202004104/https://khn.org/news/article/an-anti-vaccine-film-targeted-to-black-americans-spreads-false-information/ |archive-date=February 2, 2022 |access-date=February 2, 2022 |publisher=[[Kaiser Family Foundation]]}}</ref> At the urging of disinformation experts, the film was removed from Facebook, but Kennedy was permitted to keep his account.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Murphy |first1=Hannah |last2=Venkataramakrishnan |first2=Siddharth |last3=Stacey |first3=Kiran |date=March 15, 2021 |title=Facebook and Twitter resist calls to ban anti-vaxx campaigner |newspaper=Financial Times |url=https://www.ft.com/content/cbf152f8-e774-4bc1-8bbb-f707c5027b8d |access-date=June 15, 2023}}</ref>

=== HIV/AIDS denialism ===
In his 2021 book ''The Real Anthony Fauci: Bill Gates, Big Pharma, and the War on Democracy and Public Health'', Kennedy writes that he takes "no position on the relationship between HIV and AIDS",<ref name="Real Fauci" />{{rp|347}} but spent over 100 pages quoting [[HIV/AIDS denialism|HIV denialists]] such as [[Peter Duesberg]] who question the isolation of HIV and the [[etiology]] of AIDS.<ref name=Debunk>{{Cite AV media |last=Wilson |first=Dan |date=May 31, 2022 |title=RFK Jr. Goes full HIV/AIDS denial in his terrible book about Anthony Fauci |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c5TxLesI_kU |via=YouTube |access-date=May 20, 2023 |archive-date=May 20, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230520233600/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c5TxLesI_kU |url-status=live}}</ref> Kennedy refers to the "orthodoxy that HIV alone causes AIDS"<ref name="Real Fauci" />{{rp|348}} and the "theology that HIV is the sole cause of AIDS",<ref name="Real Fauci" />{{rp|351}} and repeats the false HIV/AIDS denialist claim that no one has isolated the HIV virion and "No one has been able to point to a study that demonstrates their hypothesis using accepted scientific proofs."{{rp|348}} He also repeats the false claim that the early AIDS drug [[AZT]] is "absolutely fatal"<ref name="Real Fauci" />{{rp|332}} due to its "horrendous toxicity".<ref name="Real Fauci" />{{rp|298}}

Molecular biologist [[Dan Wilson (biologist)|Dan Wilson]] said that Kennedy falsely claimed that [[Luc Montagnier]], the discoverer of HIV, was a "convert" to Duesberg's fringe hypothesis. Wilson concludes that Kennedy is a "full blown" HIV/AIDS denialist.<ref name=Debunk/><ref name="Real Fauci" /> Epidemiologist [[Tara C. Smith]] suggests that Kennedy's book "even flirts with outright germ theory denial", quoting from a portion in which Kennedy contrasts the [[germ theory of disease]] with [[terrain theory]]<ref name=":8">{{Cite journal |last=Smith |first=Tara C. |author-link=Tara C. Smith |date=October 12, 2024 |title=HIV Denial in the COVID Era |journal=[[AIDS and Behavior]] |volume=29 |issue=1 |pages=309–316 |language=en |doi=10.1007/s10461-024-04528-3 |pmid=39395068 |issn=1573-3254|doi-access=free |pmc=11739256 }}</ref> and another in which he writes that [[Louis Pasteur]] "is said to have recanted" germ theory [[Deathbed confession|on his deathbed]] in favor of [[Antoine Béchamp]]'s terrain theory,<ref name=":8" />{{rp|location=Table 1}} an unproven claim that circulates among germ theory denialists.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Gorski |first=David |author-link=David Gorski |date=January 4, 2021 |title=Germ theory denial in the age of the COVID-19 pandemic |url=https://sciencebasedmedicine.org/germ-theory-denial-in-the-age-of-the-covid-19-pandemic/ |access-date=November 17, 2024 |work=[[Science-Based Medicine]]}}</ref>

=== Chemtrail conspiracy theory ===
{{Main|Chemtrail conspiracy theory}}

In August 2024, after endorsing Trump for president and starting to work with Trump's campaign, Kennedy posted, "We are going to stop this crime" of [[chemtrails]]. Belief in chemtrails involves a conspiracy theory that airplane water vapor trails ([[contrail]]s) are purposely dumped chemicals designed to harm people.<ref name="bbcChemtrails" /><ref name="chemtrails-cnn">{{cite news |last1=O'Sullivan |first1=Donie |date=October 10, 2024 |title=Amid hurricanes, the chemtrail conspiracy theory has its moment in the sun |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/10/10/weather/hurricanes-chemtrail-conspiracy-theory-trump/index.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250127204302/https://www.cnn.com/2024/10/10/weather/hurricanes-chemtrail-conspiracy-theory-trump/index.html |archive-date=January 27, 2025 |access-date=November 15, 2024 |work=CNN |language=en}}</ref><ref name="chemtrails">{{cite news |last=Bump |first=Philip |date=August 27, 2024 |title=The thread connecting Robert Kennedy Jr., chemtrails and Project 2025 |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2024/08/27/thread-connecting-robert-kennedy-jr-chemtrails-project-2025/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241206114554/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2024/08/27/thread-connecting-robert-kennedy-jr-chemtrails-project-2025/ |archive-date=December 6, 2024 |access-date=November 15, 2024 |newspaper=The Washington Post }}</ref>

=== Miasma theory ===
{{Main|Miasma theory}}

Kennedy praises what he calls "miasma theory", which he says "emphasizes preventing disease by fortifying the immune system through nutrition and reducing exposures to environmental toxins and stresses". Historians say this is an inaccurate understanding of miasma theory, which has been debunked.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stein |first=Rob |date=June 14, 2025 |title=Ancient miasma theory may help explain Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr.'s vaccine moves |url=https://www.npr.org/sections/shots-health-news/2025/06/14/nx-s1-5429732/ancient-miasma-theory-may-help-explain-health-secretary-robert-f-kennedy-jr-s-vaccine-moves |access-date=December 19, 2025 |work=NPR |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Wu |first=Katherine J. |date=November 19, 2025 |title=RFK Jr.'s Miasma Theory of Health Is Spreading |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/health/2025/11/nih-pandemic-miasma/684979/ |access-date=December 19, 2025 |website=The Atlantic |language=en}}</ref>

=== Pushback from the Kennedy family ===
Several members of Kennedy's close family have distanced themselves from his anti-vaccination activities and conspiracy theories on public health, and condemned his comments equating public health measures with [[Nazi]] war crimes.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Nagourney |first=Adam |author-link=Adam Nagourney |date=February 26, 2022 |title=A Kennedy's Crusade Against Covid Vaccines Anguishes Family and Friends |newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/26/us/robert-kennedy-covid-vaccine.html |access-date=March 2, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220228163440/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/26/us/robert-kennedy-covid-vaccine.html |archive-date=February 28, 2022}}</ref> In May 2019, his niece [[Maeve Kennedy McKean]] and elder siblings Kathleen and Joseph wrote an open letter saying that while Kennedy has championed admirable causes, he "has helped to spread dangerous misinformation over social media and is complicit in sowing distrust of the science behind vaccines". They cited the roles played by President John F. Kennedy and Senator Ted Kennedy in (respectively) signing and reauthorizing the Vaccination Assistance Act of 1962.<ref name=ff>{{Cite news |url=https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2019/05/08/robert-kennedy-jr-measles-vaccines-226798/ |title=RFK Jr. Is Our Brother and Uncle. He's Tragically Wrong About Vaccines |last1=Kennedy Townsend |first1=Kathleen |author-link1=Kathleen Kennedy Townsend |date=May 8, 2019 |newspaper=Politico |access-date=May 8, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508183738/https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2019/05/08/robert-kennedy-jr-measles-vaccines-226798 |archive-date=May 8, 2019 |url-status=live |last2=Kennedy |first2=Joseph P. |author-link2=Joseph P. Kennedy II |last3=Kennedy McKean |first3=Maeve}}</ref>

On December 30, 2020, another niece, Kerry Kennedy Meltzer, a physician, wrote a similar open letter, saying that her uncle published misinformation about COVID vaccines' side effects.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Kennedy Meltzer|first=Kerry|date=December 30, 2020|title=Vaccines Are Safe, No Matter What Robert Kennedy Jr. Says|newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/12/30/opinion/covid-vaccine-bobby-kennedy.html|url-status=live|access-date=January 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210101052643/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/12/30/opinion/covid-vaccine-bobby-kennedy.html|archive-date=January 1, 2021}}</ref> John F. Kennedy's daughter [[Caroline Kennedy]] said her family was generally united in supporting public health infrastructure, citing the work of Ted Kennedy and [[Eunice Kennedy Shriver]]. She added, "I think Bobby Kennedy [Jr.]'s views on vaccines are dangerous, but I don't think that most Americans share them..."<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ray |first=Siladitya |date=November 18, 2024 |title=Caroline Kennedy Calls Cousin RFK Jr.'s Vaccine Stance 'Dangerous,' In Farewell Speech As Ambassador To Australia |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/siladityaray/2024/11/18/caroline-kennedy-calls-cousin-rfk-jrs-vaccine-stance-dangerous-in-farewell-speech-as-ambassador-to-australia/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250219224041/https://www.forbes.com/sites/siladityaray/2024/11/18/caroline-kennedy-calls-cousin-rfk-jrs-vaccine-stance-dangerous-in-farewell-speech-as-ambassador-to-australia/ |archive-date=February 19, 2025 |access-date=November 22, 2024 |work=Forbes}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Breuninger |first=Kevin |date=November 18, 2024 |title=Caroline Kennedy calls Trump HHS pick RFK Jr.'s vaccine views 'dangerous' |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2024/11/18/rfk-trump-hhs-vaccines-caroline-kennedy.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250128212741/https://www.cnbc.com/2024/11/18/rfk-trump-hhs-vaccines-caroline-kennedy.html |archive-date=January 28, 2025 |access-date=November 22, 2024 |work=CNBC}}</ref>

In January 2025, Caroline Kennedy publicly denounced Kennedy in a letter she sent U.S. senators calling him a "predator" and "hypocrite" who was unqualified to be the secretary of the Department of Health and Human Services. She accused him of animal cruelty and "encouraging" other family members, such as his brother David Kennedy, into substance abuse that led to addiction, illness, and death. Caroline Kennedy's cousin Stephen Smith Jr. said, "I completely support my cousin Caroline's view that RFK Jr. is unqualified...for the role of Secty of HHS."<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Nagourney |first1=Adam |last2=O'Brien |first2=Rebecca Davis |date=January 28, 2025 |title=Caroline Kennedy's Video Exposes the Fight Over a Fading Family Legacy |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/01/28/us/politics/caroline-robert-kennedy-trump.html |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250220174340/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/01/28/us/politics/caroline-robert-kennedy-trump.html |archive-date=February 20, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2025 |work=[[The New York Times]] |quote=On the eve of his Senate confirmation hearing, Ms. Kennedy described Mr. Kennedy as not just unqualified to run the Department of Health and Human Services, the position that President Trump has nominated him to fill, but also a "predator" and a hypocrite. She not only wrote this in a letter to senators; she also posted a video of her reading it, coolly and methodically. The fact that the public rarely sees her speaking made it all the more devastating.}}</ref>

==Political views==
{{Further|Robert F. Kennedy Jr. 2024 presidential campaign#Political positions}}
Kennedy's political rhetoric often invokes [[conspiracy theories]].<ref name="Weissman-15 Jul 2023">{{cite news |last=Weissman |first=Jonathan |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Airs Bigoted New Covid Conspiracy Theory About Jews and Chinese |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/15/us/politics/rfk-jr-remarks-covid.html |access-date=July 22, 2023 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=July 15, 2023 |archive-date=July 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230721210310/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/15/us/politics/rfk-jr-remarks-covid.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Cabral |first=Sam |title=RFK Jr's conspiracy theories and Republican supporters |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-66226103 |access-date=July 22, 2023 |publisher=BBC News |date=July 18, 2023 |archive-date=July 22, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230722175939/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-66226103 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hunnicutt |first1=Trevor |last2=Holland |first2=Steve |title=White House blasts RFK Jr for 'antisemitic conspiracy theories' |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/white-house-blasts-rfk-jr-antisemitic-conspiracy-theories-2023-07-17/ |access-date=July 22, 2023 |publisher=Reuters |date=July 17, 2023 |archive-date=July 22, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230722180436/https://www.reuters.com/world/us/white-house-blasts-rfk-jr-antisemitic-conspiracy-theories-2023-07-17// |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Economic inequality===
Kennedy has argued that poor communities shoulder a disproportionate burden of environmental pollution.<ref name="Hilburn-3 Jun 2016" /> Speaking at the 2016 [[South by Southwest]] environment conference, he said, "Polluters always choose the soft target of poverty", noting that Chicago's [[South Side, Chicago|South Side]] has the highest concentration of toxic waste dumps in the U.S.,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Wang |first=Ucilia |date=October 10, 2016 |title=Robert F Kennedy Jr takes big business to task over pollution at SXSW Eco |newspaper=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sustainable-business/2016/oct/10/robert-f-kennedy-jr-sxsw-eco-climate-change-big-business-economic-policy |url-status=live |access-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171016013920/https://www.theguardian.com/sustainable-business/2016/oct/10/robert-f-kennedy-jr-sxsw-eco-climate-change-big-business-economic-policy |archive-date=October 16, 2017 |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> and added that 80% of "uncontrolled toxic waste dumps" are in black neighborhoods, with the largest site in [[Emelle, Alabama]], which is 90% black.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nirenberg |first=Michael Lee |date=May 23, 2017 |title=Conversation with Robert Kennedy Jr. |url=https://www.huffpost.com/entry/conversation-with-robert-kennedy-jr_b_59239362e4b0b28a33f62f03 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210302124911/https://www.huffpost.com/entry/conversation-with-robert-kennedy-jr_b_59239362e4b0b28a33f62f03 |archive-date=March 2, 2021 |access-date=April 12, 2023 |website=HuffPost}}</ref>

Kennedy has said that "systematic" [[Middle-class squeeze|erosion of the middle class]] is taking place, remarking in a 2023 interview with [[UnHerd]] that American politicians have "been systematically hollowing out the American middle class and printing money to make billionaires richer". He said that the [[financial industry]] and the [[military–industrial complex]] are funded at the expense of the American [[middle class]]; that the U.S. government is dominated by [[corporate power]]; the [[United States Environmental Protection Agency|Environmental Protection Agency]] is run by the "[[oil industry]], the [[coal industry]], and the [[pesticide|pesticide industry]]"; and that the [[Food and Drug Administration]] is dominated by "[[Big Pharma conspiracy theories|Big Pharma]]".<ref name="unherd1">{{cite web |last=Sayers |first=Freddie |date=May 3, 2023 |url=https://unherd.com/2023/05/robert-kennedy-jr-america-needs-a-revolution/ |title=Robert Kennedy Jr: America needs a revolution |website=unherd.com |publisher=The Post |access-date=May 11, 2023 |archive-date=May 11, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230511051430/https://unherd.com/2023/05/robert-kennedy-jr-america-needs-a-revolution/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Kennedy sees a "vibrant middle class" as the economy's backbone and has said that the economy has deteriorated because the middle class has become poorer.<ref name="hill1">{{cite web |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3964616-rfk-jr-claims-middle-class-was-systematically-wiped-out-under-covid-lockdowns/ |title=RFK Jr. claims middle class was 'systematically' wiped out under COVID lockdowns |last=Shapero |first=Julia |date=April 22, 2023 |website=The Hill |access-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-date=May 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230510163646/https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3964616-rfk-jr-claims-middle-class-was-systematically-wiped-out-under-covid-lockdowns/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

In an interview with [[Andrew Serwer]], Kennedy said that the gap between rich and poor in the U.S. had become too great and that "the very wealthy people should pay more taxes and corporations". He also expressed support for [[List of United States senators from Massachusetts|Massachusetts senator]] [[Elizabeth Warren]]'s wealth tax plan, which would impose an annual tax of 2% on every dollar of a household's net worth over $50 million and 6% on every dollar of net worth over $1 billion.<ref name="yahoo1">{{cite web |url=https://finance.yahoo.com/news/robert-kennedy-jr-on-middle-class-123520806.html |title=Robert Kennedy Jr: 'We've destroyed the middle class' |last=Belmonte |first=Adriana |date=January 19, 2020 |website=finance.yahoo.com |access-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230521063921/https://finance.yahoo.com/news/robert-kennedy-jr-on-middle-class-123520806.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Foreign affairs and military intervention===
Kennedy is critical of the United States' alliances with [[dictatorship]]s like [[Saudi Arabia]]. He criticized the [[Saudi-led intervention in the Yemeni civil war]], calling it a "genocide against the Iranian-backed Houthi tribe".<ref name="politic1"/> Kennedy is a supporter of [[Israel]]. In December 2023, he had a heated exchange with ''[[Breaking Points]]'' host [[Krystal Ball]], in what Rabbi [[Shmuley Boteach]] called "the single greatest defense of Israel on videos since the start of the" [[Gaza war]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Sen |first=Sumanti |date=December 19, 2023 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr hailed for 'single greatest defense of Israel': 'Worth three minutes of your time' |url=https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-hailed-for-single-greatest-defense-of-israel-worth-three-minutes-of-your-time-101702960343411.html |work=The Hindustan Times}}</ref> In a March 2025 HHS news release, he referred to student protests against Israel's actions in Gaza as "anti-semitism" and the product of "woke cancel culture".<ref name="guard-4mar2025-2">{{cite news |last=Betts |first=Anna |title=Trump threatens to pull federal funds for US schools allowing 'illegal protests' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/04/trump-university-funding-protests |access-date=March 4, 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=March 4, 2025}}</ref><ref name="hhs-3mar2025">{{cite web |title=HHS, ED, and GSA Announce Additional Measures to End Anti-Semitic Harassment on College Campuses |url=https://www.hhs.gov/press-room/ending-anti-semitic-harassment-on-college-campuses.html |website=[[U.S. Department of Health and Human Services]] |access-date=March 4, 2025 |date=March 3, 2025 }}</ref>

An opponent of the [[military industry]] and [[Foreign interventions by the United States|foreign interventions]], Kennedy was critical of the [[Iraq War]] as well as American support for Ukraine against [[Russian invasion of Ukraine|Russia's invasion of the country]]. He condemned the Russian invasion of Ukraine,<ref>{{cite news |last=Vanden Heuvel |first=Katrina |author-link=Katrina vanden Heuvel |date=July 5, 2023 |title=Robert Kennedy Jr. Is a Flawed Heretic |url=https://www.thenation.com/article/politics/robert-kennedy-presidential-campaign-war-military-spending/ |work=The Nation}}</ref> but called the [[Russo-Ukrainian War]] "a U.S. war against Russia" and said the war's goal was to "sacrifice the flower of Ukrainian youth in an abattoir of death and destruction for the geopolitical ambition of the neocons".<ref name="unherd1"/> He called for a peace agreement in Ukraine based on the [[Minsk agreements|Minsk Accords]]; in his view, the [[Donbas region]] should remain in Ukraine but also be given territorial autonomy and placed under the jurisdiction of [[United Nations]] [[peacekeeping]] forces, while [[Aegis Ballistic Missile Defense System|Aegis missile systems]] should be removed from Eastern Europe.<ref name="moddip1">{{cite web |url=https://moderndiplomacy.eu/2023/05/08/robert-kennedy-jr-lets-be-honest-its-a-us-war-against-russia/ |title=Robert Kennedy Jr: 'Let's be honest: it's a US war against Russia' |date=May 8, 2023 |website=moderndiplomacy.eu |access-date=May 20, 2023 |archive-date=May 20, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230520171046/https://moderndiplomacy.eu/2023/05/08/robert-kennedy-jr-lets-be-honest-its-a-us-war-against-russia/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Kennedy said Ukraine should be [[Ukraine–NATO relations#Russian opposition to Ukrainian NATO membership|forbidden from joining NATO]], and announced that as president he would consider admitting Russia to NATO and deescalating tensions with [[China]].<ref name="unherd1"/><ref name="moddip1"/> He said the 2014 [[Revolution of Dignity|Ukrainian revolution]] was an [[Coup d'état|attempted coup]] sponsored by the U.S. against the Ukrainian government, and that the Ukrainian government [[Disinformation in the Russian invasion of Ukraine#Allegations of "genocide" in Donbas|committed atrocities]] against the Russian population in Donbas, wrongly claiming that all casualties of the [[Donbas War]] between 2014 and 2022 (about 14,000) were Russians.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 15, 2023 |title=Robert Kennedy Jr. Repeats Russia's False Justification for Ukraine War |url=https://www.polygraph.info/a/fact-check-robert-kennedy-jr-repeats-russia-s-false-justification-for-ukraine-war-/7138506.html |access-date=May 16, 2024 |publisher=[[Polygraph.info]]}}</ref> He said that Russians living there "were being systematically killed by the Ukrainian government".<ref name="unherd1" />

Kennedy denounced the operations of former [[CIA]] director [[Allen Dulles]], condemning U.S.-backed coups and interventions such as the [[1953 Iranian coup d'état]] as "bloodthirsty", and blamed U.S. interventions in countries such as Syria and Iran for the rise of terrorist organizations such as ISIS and creating [[anti-American sentiment]] in the region.<ref name="politic1">{{cite web |url=https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2016/02/rfk-jr-why-arabs-dont-trust-america-213601/ |title=Why the Arabs Don't Want Us in Syria |last=Kennedy |first=Robert F. Jr. |date=February 22, 2016 |website=Politico |access-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230521063921/https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2016/02/rfk-jr-why-arabs-dont-trust-america-213601/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Kennedy said the CIA has no accountability and declared his intention to restructure the agency.<ref name="unherd1"/>

Kennedy's disapproval of U.S. intervention in foreign governments was expressed in a 1974 ''[[Atlantic Monthly]]'' article titled "Poor Chile", discussing [[1973 Chilean coup d'état|the overthrow of Chilean president Salvador Allende]].<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (February 1974). "Poor Chile". ''Atlantic Monthly''.</ref> He also wrote editorials against the execution of Pakistani president [[Zulfikar Ali Bhutto]] by General [[Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq]].<ref>Kennedy Jr. Robert F. (February 25, 1979). "Plea to Pakistan". ''The Washington Post''.</ref><ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (February 23, 1979). "Legacy at Stake in Pakistan". ''The Boston Globe''.</ref> In 1975, he published an article in ''The Wall Street Journal'' criticizing assassination as a foreign policy tool.<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (December 16, 1975) "Politics and Assassinations". ''The Wall Street Journal''</ref> In 2005, he wrote an article for the ''[[Los Angeles Times]]'' decrying President Bush's use of torture as anti-American.<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (December 17, 2005). "America's anti-torture tradition". ''Los Angeles Times''.</ref> His uncle Senator Ted Kennedy entered the article into the ''[[Congressional Record]]''.<ref>Congressional Record Index. (Vol 151 – Part 23). January 4, 2005 – December 30, 2005. by Senator Edward M. Kennedy, p. 2054.</ref>

In an article titled "Why the Arabs Don't Want Us in Syria" published in ''[[Politico]]'' in February 2016, Kennedy referred to the "bloody history that modern [[Interventionism (politics)|interventionists]] like [[George W. Bush]], [[Ted Cruz]], and [[Marco Rubio]] miss when they recite their narcissistic trope that Mideast nationalists 'hate us for our freedoms.' For the most part they don't; instead they hate us for the way we betrayed those freedoms—our own ideals—within their borders."<ref name="politic1" /> Kennedy blames the Syrian war on a pipeline dispute. He cites apparent [[WikiLeaks]] disclosures alleging that the CIA led military and intelligence planners to foment a [[Sunni]] uprising against [[Syria]]'s president, [[Bashar al-Assad]], following his rejection of a proposed [[Qatar-Turkey pipeline]] through Syria in 2009, well before the [[Arab Spring]].<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (February 25, 2016). "Syria: Another Pipeline War". ''EcoWatch''.</ref>

In a June 2023 interview, Kennedy said that in broad terms, he believes that [[Foreign relations of the United States|U.S. foreign relations]] should involve significantly reducing the military presence in other nations. He specifically said the country must "start unraveling the Empire" by closing [[List of United States military bases|U.S. bases in different locations worldwide]].<ref name="Unravel">{{cite web|url=https://www.realclearpolitics.com/video/2023/06/15/robert_kennedy_jr_unravel_the_empire_of_us_military_bases_around_the_world.html|title=Robert Kennedy Jr.: "Unravel The Empire" Of U.S. Military Bases Around The World|author-last1=Hains|author-first1= Tim|date=June 15, 2023|work=[[Real Clear Politics]]|access-date=June 25, 2023}}</ref>

Kennedy believes that the administration of President Joe Biden, in large part, caused the 2022 invasion of Ukraine by Russia due to reckless and militant action; he has specifically cited the issue of [[NATO expansion into Eastern Europe]]. At the same time, he has clarified that he refuses to connect this criticism with anything considered support of the [[government of Russia]] under Putin, particularly given Kennedy's [[Ethics|ethical opposition]] to [[Putinism|the regime's beliefs and politics]]. He has called Putin a "monster", a "thug", and a "gangster".<ref name="Unravel"/> He also criticized the Trump and Biden administrations' "provocative policies" in regard to [[China–United States relations|U.S. relations with China]], saying that "China does not want a hot war" and calling for a reduction in tensions.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kine |first=Phelim |date=June 6, 2024 |title=The U.S.-China lunar race gets real |url=https://www.politico.eu/newsletter/china-watcher/the-u-s-china-lunar-race-gets-real/ |access-date=December 18, 2024 |work=[[Politico]]}}</ref>

===Environmental policy===
Kennedy promotes populist and [[anti-establishment]] environmental policies, claiming that "[[Bill Gates]] and the [[World Economic Forum]] and the billionaire boys' club in Davos" have hijacked the [[climate crisis]].<ref name="unherd1"/> In a 2015 interview, Kennedy said of politicians skeptical of global warming that he "wished there were a law you could punish them under".<ref>{{cite book |last1=Bennett |first1=William John |last2=Cribb |first2=John T.E. |title=America the Strong: Conservative Ideas to Spark the Next Generation |date=September 22, 2015 |publisher=Tyndale House Publishers |isbn=978-1-4964-0975-1 |page=54 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=jruaBwAAQBAJ |access-date=June 9, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Mull |first1=Teresa |date=September 23, 2014 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. wants a law to 'punish global warming skeptics' |url=https://theweek.com/speedreads/445746/robert-f-kennedy-jr-wants-law-punish-global-warming-skeptics |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250221133932/https://theweek.com/speedreads/445746/robert-f-kennedy-jr-wants-law-punish-global-warming-skeptics |archive-date=February 21, 2025 |access-date=June 9, 2023 |website=The Week}}</ref> He has said that environmentalists' priority should be to tackle the "carbon industry". He has called the current society and economy unsustainable and largely based on a "longtime deadly addiction to coal and oil" and contended that the economic system rewards pollution. In 2020, Kennedy said: "Right now, we have a market that is governed by rules that were written by the carbon incumbents to reward the dirtiest, filthiest, most poisonous, most toxic, most war-mongering fields from hell, rather than the cheap, clean, green, wholesome and patriotic fields from heaven."<ref name="yahoo2">{{cite web |url=https://finance.yahoo.com/news/robert-kennedy-jr-on-climate-change-140205979.html |title=Climate change is cost of society's 'addiction to coal and oil,' says Robert Kennedy Jr. |last=Zahn |first=Max |date=January 16, 2020 |website=finance.yahoo.com  |access-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230521063921/https://finance.yahoo.com/news/robert-kennedy-jr-on-climate-change-140205979.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="irishcentral1">{{cite web |url=https://www.irishcentral.com/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-climate-change |title=Robert Kennedy, Jr says climate change will bring 'major disruptions' to civilization |date=January 17, 2020 |website=irishcentral.com  |access-date=June 12, 2023 |archive-date=March 4, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230304212613/https://www.irishcentral.com/news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-climate-change |quote="Right now, we have a market that is governed by rules that were written by the carbon incumbents to reward the dirtiest, filthiest, most poisonous, most toxic, most war-mongering fields from hell, rather than the cheap, clean, green, wholesome, and patriotic fields from heaven. We need to rationalize our marketplace so that it does the things that market is supposed to do, which is to create a society that we're all proud of and that will sustain our children." |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="desmog1">{{cite web |url=https://www.desmog.com/2017/05/06/read-excerpt-horsemen-apocalypse-dick-russell/ |title=Read This Excerpt From 'Horsemen of the Apocalypse' |date=May 6, 2017 |website=desmog.com |publisher=[[DeSmog]] |access-date=June 12, 2023 |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324004456/https://www.desmog.com/2017/05/06/read-excerpt-horsemen-apocalypse-dick-russell/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Kennedy has advocated for a global transition from [[fossil fuels]] to [[renewable energy]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ruf |first=Cory |date=May 25, 2013 |title=Robert Kennedy Jr. on his crusade for a 'green' economy |publisher=CBC News |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/hamilton/robert-kennedy-jr-on-his-crusade-for-a-green-economy-1.1411847}}</ref><ref>Overheard with Evan Smith. (May 5, 2011). "Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. – Fossil Fuels". PBS.</ref> but has opposed hydropower from dams.<ref name="auto10">{{cite news |last1=Trueheart |first1=Charles |author1-link=Charles Trueheart |title=Quebec Power Company Finds Its High-Voltage Plans Short-Circuited |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1994/04/07/quebec-power-company-finds-its-high-voltage-plans-short-circuited/4ba36f07-d34b-4105-b3b4-849b08e1431b |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=April 7, 1994 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140903/https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1994/04/07/quebec-power-company-finds-its-high-voltage-plans-short-circuited/4ba36f07-d34b-4105-b3b4-849b08e1431b/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="auto8">[[Jon Bowermaster|Bowermaster, Jon]] (November 1992). "Last Run Down the Bio Bio". ''Town and Country''.</ref><ref name="auto7">{{Cite news |last=Guynup |first=Sharon |date=June 7, 2002 |title=Belize Dam Fight Heats Up as Court Prepares to Rule |work=National Geographic Today}}</ref><ref name="auto9">{{Cite news |last=Leaney |first=Stephen |date=April 11, 2003 |title=British finding 'secret war' in green laws by Kennedy |agency=International Press Service}}</ref><ref name="auto5">{{cite journal |first=Ari |last=Hershowitz |title=A Solid Foundation: Belize's Chalillo Dam and Environmental Decisionmaking |journal=Ecology Law Quarterly |volume=35 |issue=1 |date=January 2008 |pages=73–105 |publisher=University of California |location=Berkeley |jstor=44321074}}</ref><ref name="auto3">"Robert F. Kennedy Jr. on the river along with numerous leaders of conservation organizations". August 2, 2004. Canadian Parks And Wilderness Society – Quebec Chapter.</ref> He has argued that switching to solar and wind energy reduces costs and greenhouse gases while improving air and water quality, citizens' health, and the number and quality of jobs.<ref>Staff writer (2015). "At Pennsylvania Coal Mine, Feds Come to Rescue after State Fails". ''Waterkeeper Magazine'' Vol. 11. Issue 1.</ref> Kennedy's fight to stop [[Appalachian Mountains|Appalachian]] mountaintop removal mining was the subject of the film ''[[The Last Mountain]]''.<ref>''[https://www.imdb.com/title/tt1787816/ The Last Mountain] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201003114356/https://www.imdb.com/title/tt1787816/ |date=October 3, 2020}}'' (2011) IMDb.{{Unreliable source?|date=June 2023}}</ref>

In one of his first environmental cases, Kennedy sued [[Mobil Oil]] for polluting the Hudson.<ref name="auto4"/> He had been an early supporter of [[natural gas]] as a viable bridge fuel to renewables and a cleaner alternative to coal,<ref>Isensee, Laura (October 28, 2009). "Robert Kennedy Jr: solar and natural gas belong together". Reuters.</ref> but said he turned against this controversial extraction method after investigating its cost to public health, climate, and road infrastructure.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Cusick |first=Marie |date=October 3, 2013 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. calls natural gas a "catastrophe" |work=StateImpact Pennsylvania |url=http://stateimpact.npr.org/pennsylvania/2013/10/03/robert-f-kennedy-jr-calls-natural-gas-a-catastrophe/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131004032100/http://stateimpact.npr.org/pennsylvania/2013/10/03/robert-f-kennedy-jr-calls-natural-gas-a-catastrophe/ |archive-date=October 4, 2013}}</ref> As a member of Governor [[Andrew Cuomo]]'s [[fracking]] commission, Kennedy helped engineer a 2013 ban on fracking in New York State.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hakim |first1=Danny |title=Shift by Cuomo on Gas Drilling Prompts Both Anger and Praise |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/10/01/nyregion/with-new-delays-a-growing-sense-that-gov-andrew-cuomo-will-not-approve-gas-drilling.html |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=The New York Times |date=September 30, 2012 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140919/https://www.nytimes.com/2012/10/01/nyregion/with-new-delays-a-growing-sense-that-gov-andrew-cuomo-will-not-approve-gas-drilling.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2013, Kennedy assisted the [[Chipewyan]] First Nation and the [[Beaver Lake Cree Nation|Beaver Lake Cree]] in fighting to protect their land from [[tar sands]] production.<ref name="macleans">{{cite news |last1=Weber |first1=Bob |title=Prominent U.S. Keystone critic Robert F. Kennedy Jr. to visit oilsands |url=https://www.macleans.ca/news/prominent-u-s-keystone-critic-robert-f-kennedy-jr-to-visit-oilsands |access-date=January 13, 2020 |work=Macleans |date=June 12, 2013 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140914/https://www.macleans.ca/news/prominent-u-s-keystone-critic-robert-f-kennedy-jr-to-visit-oilsands/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In February 2013, while protesting the [[Keystone XL Pipeline]] Kennedy, along with his son, Conor, was arrested for blocking a thoroughfare in front of the White House during a protest.<ref>Spear, Stefanie (February 13, 2013). "Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., Bill McKibben, Michael Brune, Among Others Will Risk Arrest Today at White House to Stop Tar Sands, Keystone XL Pipeline". ''EcoWatch''.</ref>

In August 2016, Kennedy and Waterkeepers participated in protests to block the extension of the [[Dakota Access pipeline]] across the [[Standing Rock Sioux Reservation]]'s water supply.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Manning |first=Sarah Sunshine |date=November 16, 2016 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Visits Standing Rock on #NoDAPL Day of Action |url=https://ictnews.org/archive/robert-f-kennedy-jr-visits-standing-rock-on-nodapl-day-of-action |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241222185932/https://ictnews.org/archive/robert-f-kennedy-jr-visits-standing-rock-on-nodapl-day-of-action |archive-date=December 22, 2024 |access-date=May 3, 2025 |work=Indian Country Today}}</ref>

Kennedy has maintained that the oil industry remains competitive against renewables and electric cars only due to massive direct and indirect subsidies and political interventions on the oil industry's behalf. In a June 2017 interview on ''EnviroNews'', he said of the oil industry: "That's what their strategy is: build as many miles of pipeline as possible. And what the industry is trying to do is to increase that level of infrastructure investment so our country won't be able to walk away from it".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Zudkowski |first=Dan |date=June 26, 2017 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Talks Tesla, Electric Big Rigs and the Impending Death of Fossil Fuels - EnviroNews {{!}} The Environmental News Specialists |url=https://www.environews.tv/062617-robert-f-kennedy-jr-talks-tesla-electric-vehicles-impending-death-fossil-fuels/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231228074630/https://www.environews.tv/062617-robert-f-kennedy-jr-talks-tesla-electric-vehicles-impending-death-fossil-fuels/ |archive-date=December 28, 2023 |access-date=May 3, 2025 |website=EnviroNews |language=en-US}}</ref>
Kennedy supported [[Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez]]'s [[Recognizing the duty of the Federal Government to create a Green New Deal|Green New Deal]] resolution, saying in a 2020 interview, "I think the Green New Deal and all that stuff is important. We ought to be pursuing it. My approach is more market-based than kind of top-down dictates. You know, I believe that we should use market mechanisms like carbon taxes and the elimination of subsidies."<ref name=yahoo2 />

Kennedy has spoken against [[geoengineering]], saying that geoengineering solutions are an attempt by big business to profit from climate change.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Reacts to Presidential Debate |url=https://www.c-span.org/video/?536668-1/robert-f-kennedy-jr-reacts-presidential-debate |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250128021833/https://www.c-span.org/program/campaign-2024/robert-f-kennedy-jr-reacts-to-presidential-debate/644132 |archive-date=January 28, 2025 |access-date=November 27, 2024 |website=[[C-SPAN]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sayers |first=Freddie |date=November 14, 2024 |title=Robert Kennedy Jr: America needs a revolution |url=https://unherd.com/2024/11/robert-kennedy-jr-america-needs-a-revolution/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427215435/https://unherd.com/2024/11/robert-kennedy-jr-america-needs-a-revolution/ |archive-date=April 27, 2025 |access-date=November 27, 2024 |website=UnHerd |language=en-GB}}</ref>

Kennedy has expressed support for [[regenerative farming]], and in May 2023, he voiced support for [[agrarianism|agrarian]] movements, saying, "If we want to have democracy, we need a broad ownership of our land by a wide variety of yeoman farmers, each with a stake in our system."<ref name="unherd1" /> In 1995, Premier [[Ralph Klein]] of Alberta declared Kennedy ''[[persona non grata]]'' in the province due to his activism against Alberta's large-scale hog production facilities.<ref>Rogers, Shelagh, host, and Carty, Bob, reporter (February 12, 2001). "Robert F. Kennedy Jr. warns Canada about Pollution from Pork Industry". This Morning: CBC Digital Archives.</ref> In 2002, [[Smithfield Foods]] sued Kennedy in Poland under a Polish law that makes criticizing a corporation illegal after he denounced the company in a debate with Smithfield's Polish director before the Polish parliament.<ref name="porkchop" />{{clarify|reason=What is the name of this law?  Was it repealed?|date=October 2024}}

Kennedy has opposed conventional [[nuclear power]], arguing that it is unsafe and not economically competitive.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Zukowski |first=Dan |date=June 23, 2017 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr.: 'I love James Hansen, but he is wrong on [the nuclear power issue] |url=https://www.environews.tv/062317-robert-f-kennedy-jr-love-james-hansen-wrong-nuclear-power-issue/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250209102409/https://www.environews.tv/062317-robert-f-kennedy-jr-love-james-hansen-wrong-nuclear-power-issue/ |archive-date=February 9, 2025 |access-date=May 3, 2025 |work=EnviroNews}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Brooks |first=Jon |date=June 22, 2011 |title=Interview: Robert F. Kennedy Jr. on 'Uninsurable' Nuke Energy Industry, BrightSource Solar Project |url=https://www.kqed.org/news/31875/interview-robert-f-kennedy-jr-on-nuclear-energy-brightsource-solar-project |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250204234048/https://www.kqed.org/news/31875/interview-robert-f-kennedy-jr-on-nuclear-energy-brightsource-solar-project |archive-date=February 4, 2025 |access-date=May 3, 2025 |publisher=[[KQED Inc.]]}}</ref> In June 1981, he spoke at an anti-nuclear rally at the [[Hollywood Bowl]] with the musicians [[Stephen Stills]], [[Bonnie Raitt]], and [[Jackson Browne]].<ref>Paul Chin photograph. (June 15, 1981). Caption reads, "Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. speaks out against nuclear power". Archivegrid, Los Angeles Public Library.</ref> He believes nuclear energy is a profit-making venture promoted by corporate lobbyists rather than environmental activists, and has claimed insurance companies are unwilling to insure nuclear plants, saying in a 2023 interview, "It's not hippies in tie-dyed T-shirts who are saying it's dangerous; it's guys on Wall Street with suits and ties."<ref name="unherd1"/>

Throughout the [[presidency of George W. Bush]], Kennedy was critical of Bush's environmental and energy policies, saying Bush was defunding and corrupting federal science projects.<ref>Griscom, Amanda (October/November 2004). "Environmental Justice". ''Mother Earth News''.</ref> Kennedy was also critical of Bush's 2003 [[hydrogen car]] initiative,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Roberts |first1=Joel |title=Bush: $1.2 Billion For Hydrogen Cars|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/bush-12-billion-for-hydrogen-cars |access-date=January 13, 2020 |publisher=CBS News |date=January 29, 2003 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113113653/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/bush-12-billion-for-hydrogen-cars/ |url-status=live}}</ref> arguing that, because of plans to extract the hydrogen from fossil fuels, it was a gift to the fossil fuel industry disguised as a green automobile.<ref>{{cite news |author1=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. |title=A Bad Element|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2003/02/16/opinion/a-bad-element.html |access-date=January 13, 2020 |work=The New York Times |date=February 16, 2003 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140939/https://www.nytimes.com/2003/02/16/opinion/a-bad-element.html |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2003, Kennedy wrote an article in ''[[Rolling Stone]]'' about Bush's environmental record,<ref name="must-be-done">{{cite news |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. on the Climate Crisis: What Must Be Done |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-on-the-climate-crisis-what-must-be-done-192191 |access-date=January 13, 2020 |magazine=Rolling Stone |date=June 28, 2007 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140907/https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/robert-f-kennedy-jr-on-the-climate-crisis-what-must-be-done-192191/ |url-status=live}}</ref> which he expanded into a ''New York Times'' bestselling book.<ref>Kennedy Jr., Robert F. (2004). ''Crimes Against Nature: How George W. Bush and His Corporate Pals Are Plundering the Country and Hijacking Our Democracy''. HarperCollins. {{ISBN|0-06-074688-2}}.</ref> His opposition to the Bush administration's environmental policies earned him recognition as one of ''Rolling Stone''{{'s}} "100 Agents of Change" on April 2, 2009.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/news/story/26754176/the_rs_100_agents_of_change/4 |title=The 100 People Who Are Changing America |magazine=Rolling Stone |access-date=October 15, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090503094135/http://www.rollingstone.com/news/story/26754176/the_rs_100_agents_of_change/4 |archive-date=May 3, 2009 }}</ref><ref name="mercurynews.com">{{cite news|url=http://www.mercurynews.com/2010/10/14/robert-f-kennedy-jr-advocates-for-green-power |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. advocates for green power |date=October 14, 2010 |newspaper=The Mercury News |access-date=October 15, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171010055245/http://www.mercurynews.com/2010/10/14/robert-f-kennedy-jr-advocates-for-green-power |archive-date=October 10, 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref>

During an October 2012 interview with ''[[Politico]]'', Kennedy called on environmentalists to direct their dissatisfaction toward [[U.S. Congress|Congress]] rather than President [[Barack Obama]], reasoning that Obama "didn't deliver" due to having a partisan Congress "like we haven't seen before in American history".<ref>Dixon, Darius (October 12, 2012). "RFK Jr. to greens: Not Obama's fault". ''Politico''.</ref> He said politicians who do not act on climate change policy serve special interests and sell out public trust. He said [[Charles Koch|Charles]] and [[David Koch]]—the owners of [[Koch Industries]], Inc., the nation's largest privately owned oil company—subverted democracy and made "themselves billionaires by impoverishing the rest of us".<ref>Robert F. Kennedy Jr. (2015) "What State Attorneys General can do about Climate-Change Deniers" Letter from the President", ''Waterkeeper Magazine'' Vol. 11. Issue 1.</ref>

Kennedy has spoken of the Koch brothers as leading "the apocalyptical forces of Ignorance and Greed".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Spear |first=Stefanie  |date=September 10, 2015 |title=Koch Brothers: Apocalyptical Forces of Ignorance and Greed, Says RFK Jr. |work=EcoWatch |url=https://www.ecowatch.com/koch-brothers-apocalyptical-forces-of-ignorance-and-greed-says-rfk-jr-1882095519.html}}</ref> During the [[2014 People's Climate March]], he said: "The Koch brothers have all the money. They're putting $300 million this year into their efforts to stop the climate bill. And the only thing we have in our power is people power, and that's why we need to put this demonstration on the street."<ref>Goodman, Amy (September 22, 2014). "Robert F. Kennedy Jr.: "The Only Thing We Have in Our Power is People Power". ''Democracy Now''.</ref>

In a 2020 interview on [[Yahoo Finance]]'s "Influencers with [[Andy Serwer]]", Kennedy called President Trump's [[Environmental policy of the first Trump administration|environmental policies]] a "cataclysm" and said Trump is "simply the radical step of a process that's been happening in our country and in the Republican Party from the past—really, since 1980—which is a growing hostility towards the environment, a growing orientation to representing the concentrated corporate power and power, particularly of the oil industry and the chemical industry and some of the other large polluting industries."<ref name=yahoo2 />

===Drug use===
Kennedy has said he intends to create "wellness farms" to rehabilitate [[Federal drug policy of the United States|illegal drug]] users, to be paid for from the revenue from taxing the sale of [[Legality of cannabis|legalized cannabis]].<ref name=":10" /> The farms' inmates would grow [[organic food]], without access to computer technology.<ref name="auto12">{{Cite web |last=Butler |first=Kiera |title=RFK Jr. wants to send people addicted to antidepressants to government "wellness farms" |url=https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/07/rfk-jr-wants-to-send-people-on-antidepressants-to-government-wellness-farms/ |access-date=February 21, 2025 |website=Mother Jones |language=en-US}}</ref> He has suggested that the farms might be used to treat people on [[psychiatric medication]]s:<ref name=":10">{{Cite web |date=July 30, 2024 |title=RFK Jr says 'addicts' should be sent to 'wellness farms' to grow organic food |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/rfk-jr-kennedy-addicts-wellness-farms-b2585835.html |access-date=February 21, 2025 |website=The Independent |language=en}}</ref> "I'm going to create these wellness farms where they can go to get off of illegal drugs, off of opiates, but also illegal drugs, other psychiatric drugs, if they want to, to get off of SSRIs, to get off of benzos, to get off of Adderall, and to spend time as much time as they need—three or four years if they need it—to learn to get [[Reparenting|reparented]], to reconnect with communities."<ref name="auto12" /> Kennedy has said the farms would be compulsory only for illegal drug users.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 21, 2024 |title=Fact Check: Yes, RFK Jr. Proposed 'Wellness Farms' As Solution to Drug Addiction |url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/fact-check-yes-rfk-jr-030000179.html |access-date=February 21, 2025 |website=Yahoo News |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Questioning the validity of elections===
Kennedy has been critical of the integrity of the voting process. In June 2006, he published an article in ''Rolling Stone'' purporting to show that GOP operatives stole the 2004 presidential election for President George W. Bush.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Kennedy |first=Robert F. Jr. |author-link=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. |date=June 1, 2006 |title=Was the 2004 Election Stolen? |url=http://www.rollingstone.com/news/story/10432334/was_the_2004_election_stolen |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]] |access-date=November 3, 2008 |archive-date=September 15, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080915175855/http://www.rollingstone.com/news/story/10432334/was_the_2004_election_stolen}}</ref> Most Democrats and Republicans regarded it as a conspiracy theory. The journalist [[Farhad Manjoo]] countered Kennedy's conclusions, writing: "If you do read Kennedy's article, be prepared to machete your way through numerous errors of interpretation and his deliberate omission of key bits of data."<ref>{{cite news |author1=Farhad Manjoo |author-link1=Farhad Manjoo |title=Was the 2004 election stolen? No.|url=https://www.salon.com/2006/06/03/kennedy_39/ |access-date=January 13, 2020 |work=Salon |date=June 3, 2006 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113140901/https://www.salon.com/2006/06/03/kennedy_39/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Kennedy has written about the ease of election hacking and the dangers of voter purges and [[Voter identification laws in the United States|voter-identification laws]]. He wrote the introduction and a chapter in ''Billionaires and Ballot Bandits'', a 2012 book on election hacking by the investigative journalist [[Greg Palast]].<ref>Greg Palast (Author) Ted Rall (Illustrator). Introduction by Robert F. Kennedy Jr. (2012). ''Billionaires & Ballot Bandits: How to Steal an Election in 9 Easy Steps''. Penguin Random House. p. 300. {{ISBN|978-1-60980-478-7}}</ref>

===Political endorsements===
[[File:Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. & Donald Trump (53951825132).jpg|thumb|Kennedy endorsing Trump at a rally in Arizona on August 23, 2024]]

Kennedy worked on his uncle [[Sargent Shriver]]'s [[Democratic Party presidential primaries|1976 presidential campaign]] in Massachusetts,<ref>{{cite news |title=Endorsements ranking high in Massachusetts |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-81DAAAAIBAJ&dq=robert+kennedy+jr+endorsements+ranking+high+in+Massachusetts&pg=PA1&article_id=1346,3641944 |agency=The Daily Sentinel |date=March 2, 1976}}</ref> and later was on the national staff and a state coordinator for his uncle Ted Kennedy's [[Ted Kennedy 1980 presidential campaign|1980 presidential campaign]].{{sfn|Oppenheimer|2015|p=175}}

Kennedy endorsed and campaigned for Vice President [[Al Gore]] during his [[Al Gore 2000 presidential campaign|2000 presidential campaign]] and openly opposed [[Ralph Nader]]'s [[Green Party of the United States|Green Party]] [[Ralph Nader 2000 presidential campaign|presidential campaign]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Bate |first1=Roger |title=Nader Has the Courage to Fight a Totem |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2000-sep-12-me-19770-story.html |website=Los Angeles Times |date=September 12, 2000 |access-date=December 13, 2024}}</ref>

In the [[2004 United States presidential election|2004 presidential election]], Kennedy endorsed [[John Kerry]], noting his strong environmental record.<ref>{{cite news|last=Welch|first=Craig|date=October 29, 2003|url=http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/localnews/2001777568_rfkjr29m.html|title=RFK Jr. blasts Bush, champions Kerry|work=The Seattle Times|access-date=November 2, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20031031035311/http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/localnews/2001777568_rfkjr29m.html|archive-date=October 31, 2003|url-status=dead}}</ref> After Kerry lost the election to [[George W. Bush]], Kennedy wrote an article for ''[[Rolling Stone]]'' falsely claiming that the results were fraudulent and that the election was stolen from Kerry, basing his argument on discrepancies between exit polling and reported results in swing states such as [[2004 United States presidential election in Ohio|Ohio]], as well as voter disenfranchisement.<ref>{{cite web|last=Barone|first=Michael|title=Robert Kennedy Jr. debunked and the latest moonbat theory|url=http://www.usnews.com/usnews/opinion/baroneblog/archives/060613/robert_kennedy.htm|website=Barone Blog|publisher=USNews.com|date=June 13, 2006|access-date=November 2, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060704183001/http://www.usnews.com/usnews/opinion/baroneblog/archives/060613/robert_kennedy.htm|archive-date=July 4, 2006|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Merlan |first1=Anna |title=RFK Jr. Is Priming His Audience for Election Denialism |url=https://www.wired.com/story/rfk-jr-election-denialism/ |website=wired.com |publisher=WIRED Magazine |access-date=November 24, 2024}}</ref>

In late 2007, Kennedy and his sisters [[Kerry Kennedy|Kerry]] and Kathleen endorsed [[Hillary Clinton]] in the [[2008 Democratic Party presidential primaries]].<ref>{{cite news |first1=Jeff |last1=Zeleny |author1-link=Jeff Zeleny |first2=Carl |last2=Hulse |author2-link=Carl Hulse |title=Kennedy Chooses Obama, Spurning Plea by Clintons |url=https://archive.nytimes.com/www.nytimes.com/2008/01/28/us/politics/28kennedy.html|access-date=November 2, 2025|newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=January 28, 2008 |archive-date=December 10, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081210221628/http://www.nytimes.com/2008/01/28/us/politics/28kennedy.html |url-status=live}}</ref> After the [[2008 Democratic National Convention|Democratic Convention]], Kennedy campaigned for Obama across the country.<ref>"eTown at the 2008 Democratic National Convention (Denver)". October 8, 2008. etown.org.</ref> After the election, the Obama administration was reportedly considering Kennedy for [[administrator of the Environmental Protection Agency]], but felt his controversial statements and arrest for heroin possession in the 1980s made him unlikely to win Senate confirmation.<ref>{{Cite news |first=Mike |last=Allen |date=November 5, 2008 |title=Obama considers stars for Cabinet |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2008/11/obama-considers-stars-for-cabinet-015320 |access-date=July 17, 2023 |newspaper=Politico}}</ref><ref name=":4">{{Cite news |last=Lovley |first=Erika |date=November 7, 2008 |title=RFK Jr.: Too controversial for EPA? |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2008/11/rfk-jr-too-controversial-for-epa-015403 |access-date=April 1, 2023 |newspaper=Politico |archive-date=December 7, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161207095332/https://www.politico.com/story/2008/11/rfk-jr-too-controversial-for-epa-015403 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2016, Kennedy called supporters of then-presidential candidate [[Donald Trump]] "belligerent idiots" and suggested that some were "outright Nazis". He also characterized Trump as a "bully" and a "threat to democracy",<ref>{{Cite news |first=Andrew |last=Kaczynsky |date=November 21, 2024 |title= RFK Jr. compared Trump to Hitler and praised descriptions of his supporters as 'Nazis' |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/11/21/politics/kfile-rfk-jr-trump-critique/index.html |access-date=November 23, 2023 |newspaper=CNN}}</ref> comparing him to [[Adolf Hitler]] and [[George Wallace]].<ref>{{Cite news |first=Brittany |last=Gibson |date=November 21, 2024 |title= RFK Jr. previously compared Trump to Hitler |url= https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2024/11/21/congress/rfk-jr-trump-hitler-00190944 |access-date=November 23, 2023 |newspaper=Politico}}</ref>

In 2024, Kennedy endorsed Trump for president at a Trump campaign rally in Arizona.<ref name="AP2024-08-23"/>

==Other views==
=== Animal testing ===
Kennedy has said he is "deeply committed to ending [[animal testing|animal experimentation]]" and proposed curtailing the use of primates in research.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Grimm |first1=David |title=Kennedy "deeply committed to ending animal experimentation" |url=https://www.science.org/content/article/kennedy-deeply-committed-ending-animal-experimentation |access-date=January 14, 2026 |work=Science |date=December 22, 2025}}</ref> He has directed federal agencies to prioritize and refine alternatives to animal testing such as [[3D bioprinting]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Schumaker |first1=Erin |title=Animal welfare is now part of RFK Jr.'s MAHA agenda |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/10/05/rfk-animals-testing-welfare-right-loomer-00591725 |access-date=January 14, 2026 |work=Politico |date=October 5, 2025}}</ref>

===Assassinations of John F. Kennedy and Robert F. Kennedy===
{{Further|CIA Kennedy assassination conspiracy theory}}
[[File:Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. with Attorney General Pam Bondi in March 2025.jpg|thumb|upright|Kennedy with Attorney General [[Pam Bondi]] beneath a portrait of his father, [[Robert F. Kennedy]], in March 2025]]
On the evening of January 11, 2013, [[Charlie Rose]] interviewed Kennedy and his sister [[Rory Kennedy|Rory]] at the [[Winspear Opera House]] in [[Dallas]] as a part of then Dallas mayor [[Mike Rawlings]]'s hand-chosen committee's year-long program of celebrating John F. Kennedy's life and presidency.<ref name="DiEugenio, Jim 2013">{{Cite web |last=DiEugenio |first=James |date=February 13, 2013 |title=The MSM and RFK Jr.: Only 45 years late this time |url=https://www.kennedysandking.com/news-items/the-msm-and-rfk-jr-only-45-years-late-this-time |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241105162656/https://www.kennedysandking.com/news-items/the-msm-and-rfk-jr-only-45-years-late-this-time |archive-date=November 5, 2024 |access-date=May 19, 2025 |website=www.kennedysandking.com |language=en-US}}</ref> Of JFK's [[Assassination of John F. Kennedy|assassination]], RFK Jr. said his father was "fairly convinced" [[Lee Harvey Oswald]] had not acted alone and believed the [[Warren Commission]] report was a "shoddy piece of craftsmanship". RFK Jr. said, "The evidence at this point I think is very, very convincing that it was not a lone gunman."<ref name=":3">{{cite news|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/rfk-jr-dad-believed-warren-commission-shoddy/|title=RFK Jr: Dad believed Warren Commission 'shoddy'|publisher=CBS News|agency=Associated Press|date=January 12, 2013|access-date=December 19, 2020|archive-date=March 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210302124836/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/rfk-jr-dad-believed-warren-commission-shoddy/|url-status=live}}</ref> He endorsed the 2013 edition of ''[[JFK and the Unspeakable]]'', saying it had moved him to visit [[Dealey Plaza]] for the first time.<ref name=Orbis>[[Orbis Books]], [http://www.orbisbooks.com/jfk-and-the-unspeakable.html ''JFK and the Unspeakable''] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191229220322/https://www.orbisbooks.com/jfk-and-the-unspeakable.html |date=December 29, 2019}}</ref> He was interviewed for the 2021 documentary film ''[[JFK Revisited: Through the Looking Glass]]''.<ref name="variety">{{cite news|url=https://variety.com/2019/tv/global/agc-television-picks-up-worldwide-oliver-stones-jfk-destiny-betrayed-1203368818/|title=AGC Television Picks up Worldwide on Oliver Stone's 'JFK: Destiny Betrayed'|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|first=John|last=Hopewell|date=October 12, 2019|access-date=July 12, 2021}}</ref>

In November 2023, RFK Jr. launched a petition on his presidential campaign website<ref>{{Cite web |title=Release the JFK Files Petition |url=https://jfkfiles.kennedy24.com/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241212214025/https://www.mahanow.org/jfk-files-petition |archive-date=December 12, 2024 |access-date=November 12, 2023 |website=Release the JFK Files Petition}}</ref> for the [[Biden administration]] to release the estimated remaining 1% of documents related to the case. He said that finally releasing full and unredacted documents could help restore trust in the government.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Chatelain |first=Ryan |date=November 22, 2023 |title=RFK Jr. calls for Biden to release last of JFK assassination records |url=https://ny1.com/nyc/all-boroughs/politics/2023/11/21/robert-f--kennedy-jr--calls-for-biden-to-release-last-of-jfk-assassination-records |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250213111436/https://ny1.com/nyc/all-boroughs/politics/2023/11/21/robert-f--kennedy-jr--calls-for-biden-to-release-last-of-jfk-assassination-records |archive-date=February 13, 2025 |access-date=November 22, 2023 |publisher=[[NY1]]}}</ref>

In an interview on [[Lex Fridman]]'s podcast, Kennedy said that the evidence that the CIA was involved in the assassination was "beyond any reasonable doubt".<ref>{{cite web |title=Podcast #388 - transcript 1:10:31 |url=https://lexfridman.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr-transcript#chapter7_jfk_assassination_conspiracy |website=Lex Fridman podcast |date=July 6, 2023 |access-date=December 1, 2023}}</ref>{{Primary source inline|date=October 2024}}

Kennedy does not believe that [[Sirhan Sirhan]] fired the shot that [[Assassination of Robert F. Kennedy|killed his father]], [[Robert F. Kennedy]]. Based on the testimony of eyewitnesses, especially [[Paul Schrade]], who was standing next to Kennedy and was shot himself, as well as the autopsy, he believes there was a [[Robert F. Kennedy assassination conspiracy theories|second gunman]].<ref name="WaPo20180605">{{Cite news|last=Jackman|first=Tom|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/retropolis/wp/2018/05/26/who-killed-bobby-kennedy-his-son-rfk-jr-doesnt-believe-it-was-sirhan-sirhan |title=Who killed Bobby Kennedy? His son RFK Jr. doesn't believe it was Sirhan Sirhan |date=June 5, 2018 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181007092707/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/retropolis/wp/2018/05/26/who-killed-bobby-kennedy-his-son-rfk-jr-doesnt-believe-it-was-sirhan-sirhan |archive-date=October 7, 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> In December 2017, he visited [[Richard J. Donovan Correctional Facility]] near San Diego to meet Sirhan. After meeting Sirhan, he gave his support for a reinvestigation of the assassination.<ref name="WaPo20180605"/>

===Fertility===
In April 2025, Kennedy said: "We have fertility rates that are just spiraling. A teenager today, an American teenager, has less testosterone than a 68-year-old man. Sperm counts are down 50%." In October 2025, he said that teenage girls are reaching puberty six years earlier. In May 2026, he said that men in 1970 had twice the sperm count of modern teenagers.<ref name="Yahoo 26">{{cite news |last1=Akpobare |first1=Ejiro |title=RFK Jr. Sparks Online Debate After Claiming Teen Boys Have Far Less Sperm Than Men Did in 1970 |url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/articles/rfk-jr-sparks-online-debate-125528703.html |access-date=13 May 2026 |work=Yahoo News |date=12 May 2026}}</ref> He described the country as undergoing a "fertility crisis" that threatens the economy and national security, and attributed the crisis to endocrine-disrupting chemicals, pesticides, and other factors causing hormonal imbalances.<ref name="Wired 26">{{cite news |last1=Dickson |first1=EJ |title=MAHA Keeps Being Weird as Hell About Fertility |url=https://www.wired.com/story/maha-is-still-creepily-obsessed-with-your-fertility/ |access-date=13 May 2026 |work=Wired |date=12 May 2026}}</ref>

===Food allergies===
Kennedy was a founding board member of the [[Food Allergy Initiative]]. His son has [[anaphylactic]] [[peanut allergies]]. Kennedy wrote the foreword to ''The Peanut Allergy Epidemic'', in which he and the authors falsely link increasing food allergies in children to certain vaccines that were approved beginning in 1989.<ref>Kennedy, Robert F. Jr. (June 6, 2017). Foreword for ''The Peanut Allergy Epidemic: What's Causing It And How To Stop It''. Third Edition. by Heather Fraser, New York: Skyhorse Publishing. {{ISBN|1-5107-2631-4}}.</ref><ref name=KW>{{Cite magazine|last=Weir|first=Keziah|date=May 13, 2021|title=How Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Became the Anti-vaxxer Icon of America's Nightmares|url=https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2021/05/how-robert-f-kennedy-jr-became-anti-vaxxer-icon-nightmare|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210704184014/https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2021/05/how-robert-f-kennedy-jr-became-anti-vaxxer-icon-nightmare|archive-date=July 4, 2021|access-date=July 7, 2021|magazine=Vanity Fair}}</ref>

=== Gender dysphoria ===
{{Further|LGBT chemicals conspiracy theory}}
In a June 2023 podcast interview with [[Jordan Peterson]], Kennedy posited that several issues in children, including [[gender dysphoria]], might be linked to [[atrazine]] contamination in the water supply. He cited a 2010 study by Hayes<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Hayes |first1=Tyrone B. |last2=Khoury |first2=Vicky |last3=Narayan |first3=Anne |last4=Nazir |first4=Mariam |last5=Park |first5=Andrew |last6=Brown |first6=Travis |last7=Adame |first7=Lillian |last8=Chan |first8=Elton |last9=Buchholz |first9=Daniel |last10=Stueve |first10=Theresa |last11=Gallipeau |first11=Sherrie |date=March 9, 2010 |title=Atrazine induces complete feminization and chemical castration in male African clawed frogs (''Xenopus laevis'') |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |volume=107 |issue=10 |pages=4612–4617 |doi=10.1073/pnas.0909519107 |issn=0027-8424 |pmc=2842049 |pmid=20194757 |bibcode=2010PNAS..107.4612H |doi-access=free }}</ref> that claims that acute atrazine exposure causes [[chemical castration]] and [[Feminization (biology)|feminization]] in frogs, leading some to become [[hermaphrodites]]. Kennedy suggested that there was other evidence indicating potential effects on humans.<ref name="Greg">{{cite news |last=Graziosi |first=Graig |date=June 20, 2023 |title=YouTube removes Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. video featuring bizarre claim that polluted water makes children transgender |journal=[[The Independent]] |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/youtube-removes-rfk-conspiracy-video-b2360440.html |access-date=June 22, 2023}}</ref><ref name=":11">{{Cite news |last1=Contreras |first1=Russell |last2=Holzman |first2=Jael |date=July 5, 2023 |title=Fringe gender-chemical theory goes mainstream |url=https://www.axios.com/2023/07/05/pollution-vaccine-theory-trans-people-mainstream |access-date=November 14, 2024 |work=[[Axios (website)|Axios]]}}</ref> [[YouTube]] removed the interview under its [[vaccine misinformation]] policy, a decision Peterson and Kennedy criticized as censorship.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Duffy |first=Clare |date=June 20, 2023 |title=YouTube removed video of Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. for violating vaccine misinformation policy |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/06/20/tech/youtube-robert-f-kennedy-jr-video-removed/index.html |access-date=July 20, 2023 |website=CNN Business}}</ref><ref name="Greg" /> [[Andrea Gore]], a professor of [[neuroendocrinology]] at the [[University of Texas at Austin]], said, "I don't think people should be making statements about the relationship between environmental chemicals and changes in sexuality when there's zero evidence."<ref name=":1" /> Several scientists interviewed by ''[[Axios (website)|Axios]]'' said the hypothesis lacked evidence.<ref name=":11" /> Following media criticism, a spokesperson for Kennedy's 2024 presidential campaign told ''CNN'' that he was being mischaracterized and that he was not claiming that endocrine disruptors were the sole cause of gender dysphoria, but rather proposing further research.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last2=Kaczynski |first2=Andrew |first1=Abby |last1=Turner  |date=July 13, 2023 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. repeatedly suggested that chemicals in water are impacting sexuality of children|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/07/13/politics/robert-kennedy-jr-chemicals-water-children-frogs/index.html |access-date=July 20, 2023 |website=CNN Politics}}</ref>

===Murder of Martha Moxley===
In 2003, Kennedy published an article in <!-- Name change dates from 2004. -->''[[The Atlantic|The Atlantic Monthly]]'' about the 1975 [[murder of Martha Moxley]] in [[Greenwich, Connecticut]], in which he insists that his cousin [[Murder of Martha Moxley#Michael Skakel|Michael Skakel]]'s indictment "was triggered by an inflamed media, and that an innocent man is now in prison". Kennedy argues that evidence suggests that Kenneth Littleton, the Skakel family's live-in tutor, killed Moxley, and calls investigative journalist [[Dominick Dunne]] the "driving force" behind Skakel's prosecution.<ref name="miscarriage">{{cite news |author1=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. |title=A Miscarriage of Justice |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2003/01/a-miscarriage-of-justice/304759 |access-date=January 13, 2020 |work=The Atlantic |date=January–February 2003 |archive-date=October 18, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191018115415/https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2003/01/a-miscarriage-of-justice/304759/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2016, Kennedy released the book ''Framed: Why Michael Skakel Spent over a Decade in Prison for a Murder He Didn't Commit''.<ref>{{cite news |first=Michael |last=Melia |date=July 12, 2016 |newspaper=Hartford Courant |title=RFK Jr. Book Stokes Intrigue In Michael Skakel Murder Case |url=http://www.courant.com/news/connecticut/hc-skakel-book-0713-20160712-story.html |access-date=July 12, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160713175621/http://www.courant.com/news/connecticut/hc-skakel-book-0713-20160712-story.html |archive-date=July 13, 2016 |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2017, the rights to the book were optioned by [[FX Productions]] to develop a multi-part television series.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Schneider |first1=Michael |title=FX Prods. to Develop 'Framed,' Robert F. Kennedy Jr's Crusade to Clear His Cousin's Murder Conviction |url=https://www.indiewire.com/2017/09/robert-f-kennedy-framed-michael-skakel-martha-moxley-fx-1201876985/ |publisher=[[IndieWire]] |date=September 20, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171011072523/http://www.indiewire.com/2017/09/robert-f-kennedy-framed-michael-skakel-martha-moxley-fx-1201876985|archive-date=October 11, 2017}}</ref>

In 2018, Skakel's conviction was [[vacated]],<ref name="Casarez">{{cite web |last1=Ellis |first1=Ralph |last2=Casarez |first2=Jean |title=Court vacates Michael Skakel's murder conviction and orders a new trial |website=CNN |date=May 4, 2018 |publisher=Cable News Network. Turner Broadcasting System, Inc. |url=https://www.cnn.com/2018/05/04/us/michael-skakel-conviction-vacated/index.html |access-date=May 5, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180505084903/https://www.cnn.com/2018/05/04/us/michael-skakel-conviction-vacated/index.html |archive-date=May 5, 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> and in 2020, prosecutors decided not to seek a new trial.<ref>{{cite news |first=Edmund H. |last=Mahony |date=October 30, 2020 |title=Prosecutor in infamous Greenwich murder case tells judge state will not retry Kennedy cousin Michael Skakel in 1975 Martha Moxley killing |newspaper=Hartford Courant |url=https://www.courant.com/breaking-news/hc-news-micheal-skakel-martha-moxley-case-over-20201030-yi7xvllu7jfwxnla773vuqx5fi-story.html |access-date=October 30, 2020 }}</ref>

=== Nicotine ===
Kennedy regularly uses [[nicotine pouch]]es and has said that nicotine "does not cause cancer".<ref name=":15">{{Cite web |last=Kolb |first=Phillip |title=RFK Jr. Says Vaping Best Alternative After Nicotine Pouches, "These Little Tins Are Popping Up Everywhere" Fox News Reports |url=https://2paragraphs.com/2025/08/rfk-jr-says-vaping-best-alternative-after-nicotine-pouches-these-little-tins-are-popping-up-everywhere-fox-news-reports/ |access-date=September 2, 2025 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name= "Atlantic">{{Cite web |last=Scherer |first=Michael |author-link=Michael Scherer (journalist) |date=November 24, 2025 |title=Why Is Robert F. Kennedy Jr. So Convinced He's Right? |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/2026/01/rfk-jr-public-health-science/684948/ |access-date=December 8, 2025 |website=The Atlantic |language=en}}</ref>

=== Raw milk ===
Kennedy says he drinks only [[raw milk]] and believes it has health benefits. In October 2024, he accused the FDA of "aggressive suppression" of raw milk. Experts and the FDA say raw milk has disease risks and is not more nutritious.<ref name="Bhekisisa">{{Cite news |last=Jooste |first=Ida |date=February 4, 2025 |title=He's promised to make America healthy. Experts fear he will do the opposite |url=https://bhekisisa.org/opinion/2025-02-04-hes-promised-to-make-america-healthy-experts-fear-he-will-do-the-opposite/ |access-date=February 12, 2025 |work=[[Bhekisisa Centre for Health Journalism]] |quote=In 2023, RFK Jr boasted that he only drinks raw milk, claiming health benefits.}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Cunningham |first1=Aimee |last2=Garcia de Jesús |first2=Erin |last3=Sanders |first3=Laura |date=November 19, 2024 |title=Vaccines, fluoride, raw milk: How RFK Jr.'s views may shape public health |url=https://www.sciencenews.org/article/rfk-vaccines-fluoride-autism-milk-covid |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241119190046/https://www.sciencenews.org/article/rfk-vaccines-fluoride-autism-milk-covid#expand |archive-date=November 19, 2024 |access-date=February 12, 2025 |work=[[Science News]]}}</ref><ref name="FDARawMilkDanger">{{Cite web |date=September 5, 2024 |title=The Dangers of Raw Milk: Unpasteurized Milk Can Pose a Serious Health Risk |url=https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/dangers-raw-milk-unpasteurized-milk-can-pose-serious-health-risk |access-date=February 12, 2025 |publisher=[[U.S. Food and Drug Administration]] }}</ref>

=== Tanning beds ===
Despite federal warnings against using [[indoor tanning|tanning beds]] due to the increased risk of [[skin cancer]], Kennedy regularly uses them, saying, "I'm not telling people that they should do anything that I do. I just say 'Get in shape.{{'"}}<ref name=":15" /><ref name="Atlantic" />

=== Testosterone ===
Kennedy said he doesn't take anabolic steroids but does take [[Testosterone (medication)|testosterone]], which is an anabolic-androgenic steroid.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Florko |first=Nicholas |date=December 12, 2024 |title=RFK Jr.'s Testosterone Regimen Is Almost Reasonable |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/health/archive/2024/12/rfk-jr-testosterone/680969/ |access-date=September 2, 2025 |website=The Atlantic |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kettmann |first=Steve |author-link=Steve Kettmann |date=May 9, 2024 |title=Before Robert F. Kennedy Jr.'s Brain Worm, There Was the Steroid Question |url=https://washingtonmonthly.com/2024/05/09/before-robert-f-kennedy-jr-s-brain-worms-there-was-the-steroid-question/ |access-date=September 2, 2025 |website=Washington Monthly |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name="Atlantic" />

==Personal life==

=== General interests ===
[[File:RfkjrOCT2017.jpg|thumb|Kennedy speaking at the Foro La Región in [[Ourense, Spain]] in 2017]]
Kennedy is a licensed master [[Falconry|falconer]] and has trained hawks since he was 11. He breeds hawks and falcons and is also licensed as a raptor propagator and a wildlife rehabilitator.<ref>Rubinowitz, Susan. (December 10, 2014). "Robert F. Kennedy Jr. – The Bird Rehabilitator". Pet Place.</ref> He holds permits for Federal [[Game keeper|Game Keeper]], [[Bird bander|Bird Bander]], and Scientific Collector. He was president of the New York State Falconry Association from 1988 to 1991. In 1987, while on Governor [[Mario Cuomo]]'s New York State Falconry Advising Committee, Kennedy authored New York State's examination to qualify apprentice falconers. Later that year, he wrote the ''New York State Apprentice Falconer's Manual'', which was published by the [[New York State Department of Environmental Conservation]] and remains in use.<ref>{{Cite news |date=February 15, 2002 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. to speak at Syracuse University |url=https://news.syr.edu/blog/2002/02/15/robert-f-kennedy-jr-to-speak-at-syracuse-university/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241209070855/https://news.syr.edu/blog/2002/02/15/robert-f-kennedy-jr-to-speak-at-syracuse-university/ |archive-date=December 9, 2024 |access-date=May 3, 2025 |work=[[Syracuse University News]]}}</ref>

Kennedy is also a [[Whitewater kayaking|whitewater kayaker]]. His father introduced him and his siblings to whitewater kayaking during trips down the [[Green River (Colorado River)|Green]] and [[Yampa River]]s in Utah and Colorado, the [[Columbia River]], the [[Middle Fork Salmon River|Middle Fork Salmon]] in Idaho, and the [[Upper Hudson]] Gorge. Between 1976 and 1981, Kennedy was a partner and guide at a whitewater company, Utopian, based in [[West Forks, Maine]]. He organized and led several "first-descent" whitewater expeditions to Latin America, including three hitherto unexplored rivers: the [[Apurímac River|Apurimac]], Peru, in 1975; the [[Atrato]], Colombia, in 1979; and the [[Caroní River|Caroni]], Venezuela, in 1982.<ref>[[Peter Collier (writer)|Collier, Peter]] and [[David Horowitz|Horowitz, David]] (1984). ''The Kennedys: An American Drama''. Published by Summit Books. p. 576. {{ISBN|0-671-44793-9}}.</ref> In 1993, he made an early descent of the [[Great Whale River]] in northern Quebec, Canada.<ref>[[Jon Bowermaster|Bowermaster, Jon]] (May 1993). "Sacrificial People–Will Quebec's Indians be Driven from Their Land?". ''Conde Nast Traveller''.</ref>

In 2015, Kennedy took two of his sons to the [[Yukon]] to visit [[Mount Kennedy]] and run the [[Alsek River]], a whitewater river fed by the [[Alsek Glacier]]. Mount Kennedy was Canada's highest unclimbed peak when the Canadian government named it for John F. Kennedy in 1964.<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Jourdan|first=Michael|url=https://www.nationalgeographic.com/science/article/130805-kennedy-rfk-jfk-mountain-climbing-adventure-yukon-assassination|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230128234334/https://www.nationalgeographic.com/science/article/130805-kennedy-rfk-jfk-mountain-climbing-adventure-yukon-assassination|archive-date=January 28, 2023|title=Mountain Tribute to JFK Evoked by Kennedy Trip to Yukon|date=August 6, 2013|magazine=[[National Geographic]]|access-date=July 28, 2023}}</ref> In 1965, Kennedy's father became the first person to climb Mount Kennedy.<ref>Kennedy, Robert (April 9, 1965). "Our Climb Up Mt. Kennedy". [[Life (magazine)|Life]].</ref>

===Marriages and children===
[[File:P20250213JB-0328 (cropped).jpg|thumb|Kennedy with his third wife, [[Cheryl Hines]], in February 2025]]
On April 3, 1982, Kennedy married Emily Ruth Black, whom he met at the [[University of Virginia School of Law]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Weds Law Classmate |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1982/04/04/us/robert-f-kennedy-jr-weds-law-classmate.html |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=The New York Times |agency=United Press International |date=April 4, 1982 |archive-date=October 29, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141029153555/http://www.nytimes.com/1982/04/04/us/robert-f-kennedy-jr-weds-law-classmate.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Kennedy and Black separated in 1992 and divorced in 1994.<ref>{{cite news |last=Shaw |first=Dan |date=March 28, 1994 |title=Chronicle |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1994/03/28/nyregion/chronicle-599883.html |page=6 |access-date=April 10, 2023 |archive-date=April 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230410090320/https://www.nytimes.com/1994/03/28/nyregion/chronicle-599883.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

On April 15, 1994, Kennedy married architect and designer [[Mary Richardson Kennedy|Mary Kathleen Richardson]], a close friend of his sister Kerry, aboard a research vessel on the [[Hudson River]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Brozan |first=Nadine |date=April 20, 1994 |title=Chronicle |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1994/04/20/style/chronicle-836761.html |via=The New York Times Archives |page=4 |access-date=April 10, 2023 |archive-date=December 11, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121211110455/http://www.nytimes.com/1994/04/20/style/chronicle-836761.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="The Guardian-17 May 2012">{{Cite news |last= |first= |date=May 17, 2012 |title=Mary Kennedy had battled drug and alcohol problems |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2012/may/17/mary-kennedy-battled-drug-alcohol |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241223192507/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2012/may/17/mary-kennedy-battled-drug-alcohol |archive-date=December 23, 2024 |access-date=December 13, 2024 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Kennedy has six children: two with Black and four with Richardson.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Gurley |first=Alex |date=August 26, 2024 |title=All About Robert F. Kennedy Jr.'s 6 Children |url=https://people.com/all-about-robert-f-kennedy-jr-kids-8576394 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250419083902/https://people.com/all-about-robert-f-kennedy-jr-kids-8576394 |archive-date=April 19, 2025 |access-date=August 31, 2024 |magazine=[[People (magazine)|People Magazine]]}}</ref>

During his marriage to Richardson, Kennedy was known among his friends for sending explicit nude photos of women that they presumed he had taken, according to ''[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]]''.<ref name="Hagan_20240702"/> He reportedly engaged in multiple affairs during the marriage.<ref>{{cite web |last=McAfee |first=Tierney |date=September 16, 2015 |title=New Book Details Robert F. Kennedy Jr.'s Troubled Marriages |url=https://people.com/books/robert-kennedy-jrs-troubled-marriages-detailed-in-new-book/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250207000931/https://people.com/books/robert-kennedy-jrs-troubled-marriages-detailed-in-new-book/ |archive-date=February 7, 2025 |access-date=August 25, 2024 |publisher=[[People Magazine]]}}</ref><ref name="Hagan_20240702"/><ref>{{cite web |last=Gibson |first=Brittany |date=July 12, 2024 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. texted apology to woman he allegedly sexually assaulted, Washington Post and NBC News reports say |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2024/07/12/rfk-jr-sexual-assault-apology-00167867 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250502154916/https://www.politico.com/news/2024/07/12/rfk-jr-sexual-assault-apology-00167867 |archive-date=May 2, 2025 |access-date=August 25, 2024 |publisher=[[Politico]]}}</ref> His friends later called him a "lifelong philanderer".<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Malone |first=Clare |date=August 5, 2024 |title=What Does Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., Actually Want? |url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2024/08/12/robert-f-kennedy-jr-profile-presidential-campaign |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250415233503/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2024/08/12/robert-f-kennedy-jr-profile-presidential-campaign |archive-date=April 15, 2025 |access-date=August 26, 2024 |magazine=[[The New Yorker]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Burleigh |first=Nina |author-link=Nina Burleigh |date=August 1, 2023 |title=RFK Jr. Was a Compulsive Womanizer, and Yes, We Should Care |url=https://newrepublic.com/article/174667/rfk-jr-compulsive-womanizer |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426234417/https://newrepublic.com/article/174667/rfk-jr-compulsive-womanizer |archive-date=April 26, 2025 |access-date=August 26, 2024 |publisher=[[The New Republic]]}}</ref> On May 12, 2010, Kennedy filed for divorce from Richardson. On May 16, 2012, Richardson was found dead in a building on the grounds of her home in [[Bedford (town), New York|Bedford, New York]]. The Westchester County Medical Examiner ruled the death a [[Suicide by hanging|suicide due to asphyxiation from hanging]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Hall |first=Christine |date=May 17, 2012 |title=Police: Mary Kennedy Committed Suicide |website=The Bedford Daily Voice |url=https://dailyvoice.com/new-york/bedford/news/police-mary-kennedy-committed-suicide/483357/ |access-date=April 10, 2023 |archive-date=April 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230410101800/https://dailyvoice.com/new-york/bedford/news/police-mary-kennedy-committed-suicide/483357/ |url-status=live}}</ref> The medical examiner's report noted her fingers were caught between the neck and the noose when she was found dead, indicating a struggle to free herself.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Konig |first1=Joseph |last2=McNeil |first2= Liz |date=2026-04-08 |url=https://people.com/chilling-detail-from-mary-richardson-kennedy-suicide-11944773 |title=The Chilling Detail from Mary Richardson Kennedy's Suicide That Made Her Doctor Think "Maybe She Didn't Want to Die" (Exclusive)|access-date=2026-04-11 |website=People |language=en}}</ref> Upon arriving at the scene, Richardson's sister told Kennedy, "you have killed my sister".<ref>{{cite web |last1=Tagen-Dye |first1=Carly |last2=McNeil |first2= Liz |date=2026-04-09 |url=https://people.com/mary-richardson-kennedy-suicide-sister-told-rfk-jr-was-his-fault-11945860 |title=Hours After Mary Richardson Kennedy's Suicide, Her Sister Told RFK Jr. It Was His Fault (Exclusive)|access-date=2026-04-11 |website=People |language=en}}</ref>

Before her death, Richardson had discovered Kennedy's personal journal from 2001, in which he recorded sexual encounters with 37 different women. According to Kennedy, Richardson passed the journal along "to her sisters with instructions that, if anything happened to her, [it should be] published in the press".<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Amira |first=Dan |date=September 9, 2013 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Allegedly Had Affairs With 37 Women in 2001 |url=https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2013/09/rfk-jr-journal-affairs-diary-kennedy.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250403082547/https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2013/09/rfk-jr-journal-affairs-diary-kennedy.html |archive-date=April 3, 2025 |access-date=July 31, 2023 |magazine=[[New York (magazine)|New York]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite AV media |people= [[DJ Vlad|Vlad Lyubovny]], Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. |date=November 15, 2023 |title= Robert F Kennedy Jr on Ex-Wife Taking Her Life After Finding Diary of Women He Slept With (Part 10)|url=https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=Uu3sw2P5PA8s&t=06:50|access-date=August 25, 2024 |format=video|publisher=Vlad TV}}</ref> During their divorce, Richardson began exhibiting signs of drug and alcohol abuse and psychiatric distress.<ref name="The Guardian-17 May 2012" /><ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=May 17, 2012 |title=Mary Richardson Kennedy's Death Attributed to Hanging, Psychologists See Signs in Recent Past |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Health/mary-kennedys-death-asphyxiation-hanging/story?id=16368771 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250212163600/https://abcnews.go.com/Health/mary-kennedys-death-asphyxiation-hanging/story?id=16368771 |archive-date=February 12, 2025 |access-date=December 13, 2024 |website=ABC News }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Corn |first=David |date=January 28, 2025 |title=SCOOP: RFK Jr. Secretly Recorded Second Wife During Divorce and Acknowledged Being "Polygamous" |url=https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2025/01/robert-f-kennedy-jr-richardson-divorce-polygamous-secret-recording-suicide-department-health-and-human-services-trump/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427044523/https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2025/01/robert-f-kennedy-jr-richardson-divorce-polygamous-secret-recording-suicide-department-health-and-human-services-trump/ |archive-date=April 27, 2025 |access-date=January 30, 2025 |website=Mother Jones }}</ref>

After her death, Kennedy won a court case against Richardson's siblings to have her buried alongside fellow Kennedy family members in St. Francis Xavier Cemetery in [[Centerville, Massachusetts]], instead of closer to her siblings in New York. Shortly after her burial, Kennedy had her body disinterred and moved to an unmarked grave in an empty area of the cemetery. At the time, the Kennedy family was planning to purchase 50 plots in the same area due to overcrowding.<ref>{{cite web |last=Robillard |first=Kevin |date=July 12, 2012 |title=Report: RFK Jr. moved wife's grave |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2012/07/rfk-jr-moved-wifes-grave-078430 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250202084240/https://www.politico.com/story/2012/07/rfk-jr-moved-wifes-grave-078430 |archive-date=February 2, 2025 |access-date=August 28, 2024 |work=Politico}}</ref> Kennedy's niece Saoirse Kennedy Hill was buried next to Richardson after her death from a drug overdose at age 22.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Carlson |first1=Adam |last2=Lynch |first2=Jennifer |date=August 5, 2019 |title=Saoirse Kennedy Hill Is Buried Next to RFK Jr.'s Wife Mary Richardson |url=https://people.com/politics/saoirse-kennedy-hill-buried-next-to-mary-richardson-kennedy/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241225041822/https://people.com/politics/saoirse-kennedy-hill-buried-next-to-mary-richardson-kennedy/ |archive-date=December 25, 2024 |access-date=October 2, 2024 |website=People.com }}</ref>

In 2011, Kennedy began dating the actress [[Cheryl Hines]]. They married on August 2, 2014, at the [[Kennedy Compound]]. The couple were introduced by [[Larry David]], Hines's co-star on [[HBO]]'s ''[[Curb Your Enthusiasm]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Clift |first=Eleanor |date=2012-05-17 |title=Mary Kennedy Death: RFK Jr.’s Wife Was Depressed Over Split, Friends Say |url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/mary-kennedy-death-rfk-jrs-wife-was-depressed-over-split-friends-say/ |access-date=2026-01-14 |website=The Daily Beast |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Woletz |first1=Bob |title=No Curbs on Their Enthusiasm |newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/03/fashion/weddings/no-curbs-on-their-enthusiasm.html |access-date=January 13, 2020 |date=August 3, 2014 |archive-date=January 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200113141007/https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/03/fashion/weddings/no-curbs-on-their-enthusiasm.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Andrews-Dyer |first1=Helena |title=Actress Cheryl Hines marries Robert F. Kennedy Jr. at Kennedy family compound in Cape Code|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/reliable-source/wp/2014/08/03/actress-cheryl-hines-marries-robert-f-kennedy-jr-at-kennedy-family-compound-in-cape-cod |access-date=January 13, 2020 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=August 3, 2014 |archive-date=January 15, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200115001440/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/reliable-source/wp/2014/08/03/actress-cheryl-hines-marries-robert-f-kennedy-jr-at-kennedy-family-compound-in-cape-cod/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Kennedy and Hines reside in [[Los Angeles]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rogers |first=Josh |date=March 6, 2023 |title=RFK Jr. tells NH crowd he's considering a presidential run |url=https://www.wbur.org/news/2023/03/06/robert-kennedy-jr-considers-presidential-run-2024-new-hampshire |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230409123958/https://www.wbur.org/news/2023/03/06/robert-kennedy-jr-considers-presidential-run-2024-new-hampshire |archive-date=April 9, 2023 |website=www.wbur.org}}</ref> and [[Cape Cod, Massachusetts]].<ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1641816214644113409 |user=RobertKennedyJr |title=With my friends @delbigtree + @AaronSiriSG at strategy session at my home on Cape Cod. |first=Robert F. Jr. |last=Kennedy |date=March 31, 2023 |access-date=April 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230423153538/https://twitter.com/RobertKennedyJr/status/1641816214644113409?cxt=HHwWgoC2xcDJ88gtAAAA |archive-date=April 23, 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In September 2024, [[Olivia Nuzzi]], a reporter for ''[[New York (magazine)|New York]]'' magazine who had been covering his presidential campaign, told her editors that she had been in a relationship with Kennedy, which she described as personal but not physical.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Patrick |title=New York Magazine reporter on leave over relationship with RFK Jr. |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/olivia-nuzzi-relationship-rfk-jr-new-york-magazine-reporter-leave-rcna171949 |access-date=September 20, 2024 |work=NBC News |date=September 20, 2024 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=October 21, 2024 |title=Olivia Nuzzi and New York Magazine Part Ways |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/10/21/business/media/olivia-nuzzi-rfk-jr-new-york-magazine.html |access-date=August 29, 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Polantz |first=Katelyn |date=October 15, 2024 |title=Olivia Nuzzi said she was manipulated by RFK Jr., ex-fiancé claims in court filings {{!}} CNN Business |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/10/15/media/olivia-nuzzi-rfk-ryan-lizza-court |access-date=August 29, 2025 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Cartwright |first=Lachlan |date=September 26, 2024 |title=Where Does the Olivia Nuzzi–RFK Jr. Fallout End? |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/news/story/olivia-nuzzi-rfk-jr-fallout |access-date=August 29, 2025 |magazine=Vanity Fair |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Health===
During Kennedy's college years, he began having heart problems, which he has said were caused by [[caffeine]], [[Psychological stress|stress]], and [[sleep deprivation]].<ref name=":6" />

In his 40s, Kennedy developed adductor [[spasmodic dysphonia]], an organic voice disorder that causes his voice to quaver and makes speech difficult. It is a form of involuntary movement affecting the [[larynx]], related to [[dystonia]].<ref name="KW"/><ref name="living-dangerously"/><ref name="voice-disorder">{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Lauren |title=Kennedy's Voice Draws Attention to Rare Disorder|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Health/story?id=5296702&page=1 |access-date=January 13, 2020 |publisher=ABC News |date=January 7, 2009 |archive-date=March 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210302124835/https://abcnews.go.com/Health/story?id=5296702&page=1 |url-status=live}}</ref> Kennedy said he traveled to [[Kyoto]], Japan, for a procedure where a titanium bridge was inserted between his vocal cords to try to relieve the disorder.<ref name=":6" />

Kennedy began experiencing severe short- and long-term memory loss and [[mental fog]] in 2010. In a 2012 divorce court deposition, he attributed neurological issues to "a worm that got into my brain and ate a portion of it and then died", in addition to [[mercury poisoning]] from eating large quantities of tuna.<ref name=":6">{{Cite news |last=Pengelly |first=Martin |date=May 8, 2024 |title=Robert F Kennedy Jr says health issue caused by dead worm in his brain |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/08/robert-f-kennedy-jr-worm-brain |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250412144457/https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/08/robert-f-kennedy-jr-worm-brain |archive-date=April 12, 2025 |access-date=May 10, 2024 |newspaper=The Guardian}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Craig |first=Susanne |date=May 8, 2024 |title=R.F.K. Jr. Says Doctors Found a Dead Worm in His Brain |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/08/us/rfk-jr-brain-health-memory-loss.html |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250425081122/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/08/us/rfk-jr-brain-health-memory-loss.html |archive-date=April 25, 2025 |access-date=May 8, 2024 |newspaper=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> ''The Washington Post'' reported that Kennedy's campaign "has not released his medical records that could verify his account, and Kennedy has previously spread health misinformation, including about mercury in vaccines".<ref name="Nirappil">{{cite news |last1=Nirappil |first1=Fenit |title=RFK Jr. revealed he had a parasitic brain worm. Here's what to know. |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2024/05/08/parasitic-brain-worm-rfk/ |access-date=August 9, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=May 8, 2024}}</ref>

=== Religion ===
Kennedy is a [[Catholic Church|Roman Catholic]].<ref name="globe1">{{Cite news |last=Paulson |first=Michael |author-link=Michael Paulson |date=March 15, 2005 |title=A natural devotion |url=https://archive.boston.com/ae/books/articles/2005/03/15/a_natural_devotion/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230315205007/https://archive.boston.com/ae/books/articles/2005/03/15/a_natural_devotion/ |archive-date=March 15, 2023 |access-date=March 15, 2023 |newspaper=The Boston Globe}}</ref> In 2005, journalist [[Michael Paulson]] called him "a deeply devout Catholic who attends daily Mass".<ref name="chron1">{{cite web |url=https://www.chron.com/life/houston-belief/article/Robert-F-Kennedy-Jr-takes-a-lesson-from-St-1939959.php |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. takes a lesson from St. Francis |last=Paulson |first=Michael |author-link=Michael Paulson |date=March 19, 2005 |via=Houston Chronicle |newspaper=The Boston Globe |access-date=May 11, 2023 |archive-date=May 11, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230511141203/https://www.chron.com/life/houston-belief/article/Robert-F-Kennedy-Jr-takes-a-lesson-from-St-1939959.php |url-status=live}}</ref> Kennedy considers [[Francis of Assisi]] his patron saint and a role model.<ref name="globe1"/> In a 2005 interview with ''[[The Boston Globe]]'', he said he was deeply inspired by Francis's devotion to [[social justice]], [[poverty relief|helping the poor]], [[animal welfare]], and [[environmentalism]]; Francis is a [[patron saint]] of [[ecology]].<ref name="chron1"/>

In 2004, Kennedy published a biography, ''Saint Francis of Assisi: A Life of Joy''.<ref name="chron1"/> He said Catholicism was a vehicle of his environmentalism, adding, "environmental work is spiritual work".<ref name="chron1"/> Despite identifying as [[pro-life]],<ref name="chron1"/> Kennedy also identifies with [[Progressive Christianity|liberal Catholicism]].<ref name="unherd1"/> He criticized the church's argument that [[John Kerry]] should have been denied communion because of his support for abortion rights.<ref name="chron1"/>

In a 2018 interview with ''[[Vatican News]]'', Kennedy expressed his admiration for [[Pope John XXIII]], who is best known for his modernization of the church in the 1960s. Kennedy said, "the Church should be an instrument of justice and kindness around the world."<ref name="vat1">{{cite news |url=https://www.vaticannews.va/en/world/news/2018-06/robert-kennedy-jr-father-interview-anniversary.html |title=Robert Kennedy Jr: My father's story, values, hopes |last=Gisotti |first=Alessandro |date=June 5, 2018 |work=Vatican News |access-date=May 11, 2023 |archive-date=May 11, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230511141203/https://www.vaticannews.va/en/world/news/2018-06/robert-kennedy-jr-father-interview-anniversary.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Sexual assault allegations ===
In July 2024, ''[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]]'' reported that in the late 1990s, when he was in his 40s, Kennedy engaged in [[sexual misconduct]] with Eliza Cooney, a 23-year-old part-time babysitter for his children.<ref name="BBConVF">{{cite news |last=Wendling |first=Mike |author-link=Mike Wendling |date=July 2, 2024 |title='I am not a church boy': RFK Jr responds to sex assault allegation |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c6p27evjz49o |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427234351/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c6p27evjz49o |archive-date=April 27, 2025 |access-date=July 2, 2024 |publisher=BBC News}}</ref> Cooney alleges that Kennedy groped her and touched her inappropriately on multiple occasions and asked her to rub lotion on his back when the two were alone in a bedroom.<ref name="Hagan_20240702"/>

Kennedy called this ''Vanity Fair'' piece a "lot of garbage". When asked specifically about Cooney's allegation, he responded, "I am not a church boy. I had a very, very rambunctious youth. I said in my announcement speech that I have so many skeletons in my closet that if they could all vote, I could run for king of the world." When pressed further, he said he had no comment.<ref name=BBConVF />

After the ''Vanity Fair'' piece was published, Cooney said that Kennedy texted her: "I have no memory of this incident, but I apologize sincerely for anything I ever did that made you feel uncomfortable or anything I did or said that offended you or hurt your feelings. I never intended you any harm. If I hurt you, it was inadvertent. I feel badly for doing so."<ref name="Mansfield">{{cite news |last=Mansfield |first=Erin |date=November 21, 2024 |title=Exclusive: Former babysitter to RFK Jr.'s kids speaks about sexual assault allegation |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/elections/2024/11/20/rfk-jr-sexual-assault-allegation-eliza-cooney/76450231007/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250419001256/https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/elections/2024/11/20/rfk-jr-sexual-assault-allegation-eliza-cooney/76450231007/ |archive-date=April 19, 2025 |access-date=November 26, 2024 |publisher=[[USA Today]]}}</ref> Cooney said, "I don't know if it's an apology if you say 'I don't remember'{{nbsp}}... In the context of all his public appearances, it seemed a little bit—it didn't match. It was like a throwaway."<ref name="Mansfield" />

=== Relationship with Jeffrey Epstein ===
{{see also|List of people named in the Epstein files}}

In August 2025, [[Ghislaine Maxwell]] said Kennedy was among those who traveled with [[Jeffrey Epstein]], a claim backed by travel logs released during Maxwell's 2021 trial, though she said she "never saw anything inappropriate" from him during that time.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Richards |first1=Zoe |last2=Gregorian |first2=Dareh |last3=Arkin |first3=Daniel |last4=Egwuonwu |first4=Nnamdi |date=August 22, 2025 |title=Justice Department releases Ghislaine Maxwell interview transcript and audio file|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/live-blog/live-updates-justice-department-releases-transcript-ghislaine-maxwell-rcna226675 |website=NBC News |language=en-US |access-date=August 25, 2025}}</ref> In response, Kennedy initially claimed that he had flown only once on [[Lolita Express|Epstein's plane]] for a family Easter trip, only to later confirm a second instance in which he joined Epstein for a fossil hunting trip to [[South Dakota]].<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Smith |first1=Ryan |date=November 1, 2023 |title=Exclusive: Robert F. Kennedy Jr Says He Flew on Jeffrey Epstein's 'Lolita Express' |url=https://www.newsweek.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr-flying-jeffrey-epstein-lolita-express-private-jet-1839807 |website=Newsweek |language=en-US |access-date=February 4, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Briquelet |first1=Kate |date=December 6, 2023 |title=RFK Jr. Flew on Epstein's Private Jet for 'Fossil Hunting' Trip |url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr-flew-on-epsteins-private-jet-for-fossil-hunting-trip/ |website=The Daily Beast |language=en-US |access-date=February 4, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Smith |first1=Ryan |date=December 6, 2023 |title=Exclusive: RFK Jr Caught in Lie About Jeffrey Epstein Flight |url=https://www.newsweek.com/robert-f-kennedy-jr-jeffrey-epstein-private-jet-rfk-interview-1849875 |website=Newsweek |language=en-US |access-date=February 4, 2026}}</ref>

In November 2025, Kennedy was confirmed to be among those listed in Epstein's personal contact book.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Baio |first1=Ariana |last2=Rissman |first2=Kelly |date=November 12, 2025 |title=Epstein files: Full list of names in disgraced financier's contact book |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/epstein-files-names-full-list-trump-clinton-b2863859.html |work=The Independent |language=en-GB |access-date=November 17, 2025}}</ref> The release of the [[Epstein Files]] under the [[Epstein Files Transparency Act]] included an email from Epstein replying "whoops" to an email from an associate informing him of Richardson Kennedy's death.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Molot |first1=Clara |date=February 3, 2026 |title=The 10 Things That Haunt Me From the Latest Batch of Epstein Files |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/news/story/ten-haunting-new-epstein-files |website=Vanity Fair |language=en-US |access-date=February 4, 2026}}</ref> Kennedy has dismissed his ties to Epstein as merely social, pointing to his similarly knowing sexual predators [[Harvey Weinstein]] and [[Bill Cosby]], among others.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Derysh |first1=Igor |date=March 7, 2024 |title=RFK Jr. bizarrely defends Jeffrey Epstein meetings by listing other "sexual predators" he met |url=https://www.salon.com/2024/03/07/rfk-jr-bizarrely-defends-jeffrey-epstein-meetings-by-listing-other-sexual-predators-he-met/ |website=Salon |language=en-US |access-date=April 17, 2026}}</ref>

=== Treatment of dead animals ===
In July 2024, an image of Kennedy holding a charred animal carcass captured in 2010 surfaced in a ''Vanity Fair'' story, which alleged that the carcass belonged to a dog and that Kennedy ate it.<ref name="Hagan_20240702"/> Kennedy denied that he ate [[dog meat]], and said the animal carcass in the picture was a goat.<ref>{{cite magazine |author-last1=Stieb |author-first1=Matt |date=July 8, 2024 |title=RFK Jr. Says He Loves, Does Not Eat, Dogs |url=https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/rfk-jr-dog-videos.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250227053446/https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/rfk-jr-dog-videos.html |archive-date=February 27, 2025 |access-date=August 4, 2024 |magazine=The New Yorker}}</ref> According to ''[[Snopes]]'', the carcass in the photo is lamb.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kasprak |first=Alex |date=July 9, 2024 |title=Photo Does Not Show RFK Jr. Eating Dog — or Goat |url=https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/rfk-dog-photo/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250415051318/https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/rfk-dog-photo/ |archive-date=April 15, 2025 |access-date=September 10, 2024 |website=[[Snopes]] }}</ref> Kennedy had eaten dog, [[Horse meat|horse]], and [[guinea pig]] meat before 2001.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kennedy |first=Robert F. Jr. |date=June 17, 2001 |title=Eating Meat Sustainably |url=https://www.ecomall.com/greenshopping/rkjmeat.htm |url-status=deviated |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20010617104831/https://www.ecomall.com/greenshopping/rkjmeat.htm |archive-date=June 17, 2001 |access-date=September 15, 2024 |website= |quote=I've eaten all kinds of insects and nematodes, caterpillars, snakes, frogs, alligators, [[terrapins]], sea urchins, octopus, birds eggs, a mouse (by mistake), wild game including armadillo, [[wildebeest]], warthog, coons and capybara, and some domestic animals including horse, dog and guinea pig. I have eaten [[road kill]] and I'm fond of viscera; [[tripe]], tongue, brain and [[offal]] and [[sweet meats]] and pate, [[kidney pie]], sheep's eyes and even [[airline food]].}}</ref>

In August 2024, Kennedy released a video on Twitter, acknowledging that in October 2014 he placed a dead six-month-old bear in [[Central Park]] after initially planning to skin it for [[Bear hunting#Meat|meat]].<ref>{{cite web |author-last1=Lebowitz |author-first1=Megan |date=August 4, 2024 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. posts video saying he put a young dead bear in Central Park |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/robert-f-kennedy-jr-posts-video-saying-put-young-dead-bear-central-par-rcna165095 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427234400/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/robert-f-kennedy-jr-posts-video-saying-put-young-dead-bear-central-par-rcna165095 |archive-date=April 27, 2025 |access-date=August 4, 2024 |work=NBC News}}</ref> Kennedy said that the bear had been [[roadkill|hit by a car]] in front of him and that he ultimately abandoned the carcass for fear that it would spoil before he could preserve it, deliberately positioning the body to give the impression that it had been struck by a cyclist in Central Park.<ref name="RFKJ Bear Treisman 2024">{{cite news |last1=Treisman |first1=Rachel |title=RFK Jr. admits to dumping a dead bear in Central Park, solving a decade-old mystery |url=https://www.npr.org/2024/08/05/nx-s1-5063939/rfk-jr-central-park-bear-bicycle |access-date=August 7, 2024 |work=[[NPR]] |publisher=[[WRN Broadcast]] |date=August 5, 2024 |ref=RFKJ Bear Treisman 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240807072449/https://www.npr.org/2024/08/05/nx-s1-5063939/rfk-jr-central-park-bear-bicycle |archive-date=August 7, 2024}}</ref> He released the video in advance of a story in ''[[The New Yorker]]'' that detailed the incident.<ref>{{cite magazine |author-last1=Danner |author-first1=Chas |date=August 4, 2024 |title=RFK Jr. Admits Planting Dead Bear in Central Park |url=https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/rfk-jr-admits-planting-dead-bear-in-central-park.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250213213337/https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/rfk-jr-admits-planting-dead-bear-in-central-park.html |archive-date=February 13, 2025 |access-date=August 4, 2024 |magazine=The New Yorker}}</ref> At the time of the incident, the spectacle of a dead bear in a New York City park made the local news. A resulting [[necropsy]] by the [[New York State Department of Environmental Conservation]] found that the death was caused by "[[blunt force injuries]] consistent with a motor vehicle collision".<ref>{{cite web |author-last1=Fitzsimmons |author-first1=Emma G. |date=August 4, 2024 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Admits He Left a Dead Bear in Central Park |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/08/04/us/politics/robert-f-kennedy-jr-bear-central-park.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250429042140/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/08/04/us/politics/robert-f-kennedy-jr-bear-central-park.html |archive-date=April 29, 2025 |access-date=August 4, 2024 |work=The New York Times}}</ref>

In a 2012 ''Town & Country'' magazine profile of Kennedy's daughter Kathleen ("Kick"), she recounted a story about how her father—who, she said, liked to study animal skulls and skeletons—used a chainsaw to sever the head of a dead [[Cetacean stranding|beached whale]] in [[Hyannis Port, Massachusetts]], then strapped the whale's head to the top of their minivan with bungee cords for the five-hour drive home, saying, "every time we accelerated on the highway, whale juice would pour into the windows of the car" and they "had plastic bags over our heads with mouth holes cut out, and people on the highway were giving us the finger, but that was just normal day-to-day stuff for us".<ref name="Hudson-25 Dec 2012">{{cite web |author-last1=Morgan |author-first1=Hudson |date=December 25, 2012 |title=Camelot Continued: Kick Kennedy's Private Tour of the Family Compound |url=https://www.townandcountrymag.com/society/tradition/a924/kick-kennedy-interview/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250401135141/https://www.townandcountrymag.com/society/tradition/a924/kick-kennedy-interview/ |archive-date=April 1, 2025 |access-date=August 25, 2024 |work=[[Town & Country (magazine)|Town & Country]]}}</ref> In 2024, an environmental advocacy group asked the [[National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration Fisheries Office of Law Enforcement]] to investigate possible violations of the [[Marine Mammal Protection Act|Marine Mammal Protection]] and [[Endangered Species Act|Endangered Species]] laws. The agency determined the allegation was unfounded.<ref>{{cite news |last=Rubin |first=April |date=August 26, 2024 |title=Environmental group calls for investigation of RFK Jr. chainsawing whale head |url=https://www.axios.com/2024/08/26/rfk-jr-whale-chainsaw-investigation |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427234359/https://www.axios.com/2024/08/26/rfk-jr-whale-chainsaw-investigation |archive-date=April 27, 2025 |access-date=September 15, 2024 |work=Axios}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |author-last1=Cooper |author-first1=Jonathan J. |date=September 19, 2024 |title=Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is being investigated for collecting dead whale |url=https://apnews.com/article/rfk-jr-kennedy-whale-investigation-09c494d8164c6f9bde9ece39637ea4d3 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427013648/https://apnews.com/article/rfk-jr-kennedy-whale-investigation-09c494d8164c6f9bde9ece39637ea4d3 |archive-date=April 27, 2025 |access-date=September 20, 2024 |work=Associated Press}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Suter |first=Tara |date=October 30, 2024 |title=NOAA: RFK Jr. whale head allegation unfounded |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/4962143-noaa-rfk-jr-whale-head-allegation-unfounded/ |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |access-date=November 6, 2025}}</ref>

According to [[Isabel Vincent]]'s 2026 book ''RFK Jr: The Fall and Rise'', Kennedy wrote in his November 11, 2001, diary entry that he had cut the penis off a road-killed raccoon.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Marcus |first1=Josh |date=April 16, 2026 |title=RFK Jr. once removed a dead raccoon's penis to 'study later' while his wife and kids waited in the car, book claims |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/rfk-jr-raccoon-penis-animals-bear-roadkill-b2958627.html |website=The Independent |language=en-GB |access-date=April 17, 2026}}</ref> Vincent writes that Kennedy took the raccoon's genitals so he could "study them later".<ref>{{cite web |last1=Konig |first1=Joseph |last2=McNeil |first2=Liz |date=April 14, 2026 |title=The Biggest Bombshells from RFK Jr.'s Diaries: Slicing Off a Raccoon's Penis, Flying with Epstein and Listing the Women He Bedded |url=https://people.com/rfk-jr-diaries-biography-biggest-bombshells-11947007 |website=People |language=en-US |access-date=April 17, 2026}}</ref>

